module.exports = [
  // ── Module 172: MDPs, States, Actions and Rewards ──
  ...set(m172, l172_1, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'The Markov Property: Why the Past Doesn\'t Matter',
      titleKn: 'Markov Property: Past ಏಕೆ ಮುಖ್ಯವಲ್ಲ',
      problem: "A Markov Decision Process (MDP) assumes the Markov property: the probability of transitioning to the next state depends ONLY on the current state and action, not on the full history of how the agent arrived at that state.\n\n1) Give a concrete example of a real-world sequential decision scenario where this assumption seems reasonable (the current state genuinely captures everything relevant for predicting the future), and a second example where it seems clearly VIOLATED (knowing more of the history would genuinely help predict what happens next, beyond what the current state alone tells you).\n\n2) Explain why, when the Markov property is violated in a real problem, a common practical fix is to REDEFINE the state to include additional relevant history (e.g. augmenting the state with recent past observations) rather than abandoning the MDP framework entirely -- why does this trick let you keep using MDP-based algorithms even for problems that don't naturally satisfy the Markov property with their most obvious state definition.",
      problemKn: "ಒಂದು Markov Decision Process (MDP) Markov property ಅನ್ನೂ ಊಹಿಸುತ್ತದೆ: ಮುಂದಿನ state ಗೆ ಪರಿವರ್ತನೆಗೊಳ್ಳುವ probability ಕೇವಲ ಪ್ರಸ್ತುತ state ಮತ್ತು action ಮೇಲೆ ಮಾತ್ರ ಅವಲಂಬಿತವಾಗಿದೆ, agent ಆ state ಗೆ ಹೇಗೆ ಬಂದಿತು ಎಂಬ ಪೂರ್ಣ ಇತಿಹಾಸದ ಮೇಲೆ ಅಲ್ಲ.\n\n1) ಈ ಊಹೆ ಸಮಂಜಸವಾಗಿ ಕಾಣುವ ಒಂದು ನಿಜ-ಜಗತ್ತಿನ ಸನ್ನಿವೇಶ ಉದಾಹರಣೆ ಕೊಡಿ, ಮತ್ತು ಸ್ಪಷ್ಟವಾಗಿ ಉಲ್ಲಂಘಿಸಲ್ಪಟ್ಟಿರುವ ಒಂದು ಎರಡನೇ ಉದಾಹರಣೆ.\n\n2) Markov property ಉಲ್ಲಂಘಿಸಿದಾಗ, ಒಂದು ಸಾಮಾನ್ಯ ಪ್ರಾಯೋಗಿಕ ಪರಿಹಾರ MDP framework ಬಿಟ್ಟುಬಿಡುವ ಬದಲಿಗೆ state ಅನ್ನೂ ಹೆಚ್ಚುವರಿ ಸಂಬಂಧಿತ ಇತಿಹಾಸ ಸೇರಿಸಲು ಮರುವ್ಯಾಖ್ಯಾನಿಸುವುದೂ ಏಕೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Building an MDP and Verifying the Markov Property Empirically',
      titleKn: 'ಒಂದು MDP ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ Markov Property ಅನ್ನೂ ಪ್ರಾಯೋಗಿಕವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Implement a small grid-world MDP from scratch in Python: define a set of states (grid cells), actions (up/down/left/right), a transition function (possibly stochastic, e.g. 80% chance of moving in the intended direction, 20% chance of slipping sideways), and a reward function (e.g. -1 per step, +10 for reaching a goal cell).\n\n1) Genuinely implement the transition and reward functions as code, and simulate several full episodes (sequences of state-action-reward-next-state) by sampling actions randomly, printing the trajectory.\n\n2) Genuinely verify the Markov property holds in your implementation: pick a specific state, and confirm that the transition probabilities out of that state are IDENTICAL regardless of what sequence of states/actions led the agent there (since your transition function only takes the current state and action as input, not history, this should trivially hold by construction -- but explicitly demonstrate it by simulating reaching the same state via two different paths and confirming the next-state distribution is the same both times).\n\n3) Genuinely construct a DELIBERATELY non-Markovian variant of your grid-world (e.g. add a rule where the reward for reaching the goal depends on whether the agent visited a specific OTHER cell earlier in the episode, which the current-state-alone can't capture) -- show concretely how you'd need to augment your state definition (e.g. adding a 'have I visited cell X' boolean flag to the state) to restore the Markov property for this modified environment, and verify your augmented-state version behaves correctly.",
      problemKn: "Python ನಲ್ಲಿ ಮೊದಲಿನಿಂದ ಒಂದು ಸಣ್ಣ grid-world MDP ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ: states, actions, ಒಂದು transition function, ಮತ್ತು ಒಂದು reward function ವ್ಯಾಖ್ಯಾನಿಸಿ.\n\n1) transition ಮತ್ತು reward functions ಅನ್ನೂ code ಆಗಿ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ, ಮತ್ತು ಯಾದೃಚ್ಛಿಕವಾಗಿ actions sampling ಮೂಲಕ ಹಲವಾರು ಪೂರ್ಣ episodes ಸಿಮ್ಯುಲೇಟ್ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) ನಿಮ್ಮ implementation ನಲ್ಲಿ Markov property ಹಿಡಿದಿದೆ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ grid-world ya ಒಂದು ಉದ್ದೇಶಪೂರ್ವಕವಾಗಿ non-Markovian variant ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ರಚಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು Markov property ಮರುಸ್ಥಾಪಿಸಲು state definition ಅನ್ನೂ ಹೇಗೆ ವಿಸ್ತರಿಸಬೇಕು ಎಂದು ತೋರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Measuring the Cost of State-Space Augmentation to Restore Markov Behavior',
      titleKn: 'Markov ವರ್ತನೆ ಮರುಸ್ಥಾಪಿಸಲು State-Space Augmentation ya ವೆಚ್ಚವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Augmenting the state space to restore the Markov property (as in the previous exercise) has a genuine cost: it can substantially increase the total number of distinct states, which matters for the computational cost of many RL algorithms (e.g. tabular methods that must store a value per state). Genuinely measure this cost.\n\n1) Genuinely compute the total state-space SIZE of your original grid-world (before augmentation) versus your augmented version (with the additional 'visited cell X' flag from the previous exercise) -- report the actual multiplicative factor by which state-space size grew.\n\n2) Genuinely generalize this calculation: if you needed to track visitation of K different 'special' cells (rather than just\n\n1) to make a more complex non-Markovian dependency Markovian again, compute how the state-space size would scale with K (each additional boolean flag doubles the augmented state count, for K independent flags) -- report the resulting state-space size for a few values of K (e.g. K=1, 3, 5, 10) and discuss why this exponential blowup is a genuine practical concern for state-augmentation approaches to handling non-Markovian dependencies.\n\n3) Discuss, based on your genuine calculation, why this exponential cost motivates alternative approaches to handling long-range/non-Markovian dependencies in real RL systems (e.g. using a recurrent or memory-augmented neural network as the value/policy function approximator, letting the network itself learn to summarize relevant history into a fixed-size internal representation, rather than manually engineering an ever-growing explicit state space) -- connecting this concern to the general theme of representation learning versus hand-engineered features that has recurred throughout this curriculum.",
      problemKn: "Markov property ಮರುಸ್ಥಾಪಿಸಲು state space ವಿಸ್ತರಿಸುವುದೂ ಒಂದು ನಿಜ ವೆಚ್ಚ ಹೊಂದಿದೆ: ಇದೂ ಒಟ್ಟು ಭಿನ್ನ states ya ಸಂಖ್ಯೆಯನ್ನೂ ಗಣನೀಯವಾಗಿ ಹೆಚ್ಚಿಸಬಹುದು. ಈ ವೆಚ್ಚವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಮೂಲ grid-world ya ಮತ್ತು augmented ಆವೃತ್ತಿಯ ಒಟ್ಟು state-space SIZE ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಿ.\n\n2) ಈ ಲೆಕ್ಕಾಚಾರವನ್ನೂ ಸಾಮಾನ್ಯೀಕರಿಸಿ: K ಭಿನ್ನ 'ವಿಶೇಷ' cells ya ಭೇಟಿಯನ್ನೂ ಟ್ರ್ಯಾಕ್ ಮಾಡಬೇಕಾದರೆ state-space size K ಜೊತೆ ಹೇಗೆ scale ಆಗುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಿ.\n\n3) ಈ exponential ವೆಚ್ಚ ನಿಜ RL systems ನಲ್ಲಿ ಪರ್ಯಾಯ ವಿಧಾನಗಳನ್ನೂ ಏಕೆ ಪ್ರೇರೇಪಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m172, l172_2, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'The Discount Factor: Why Future Rewards Are Worth Less',
      titleKn: 'Discount Factor: Future Rewards ಏಕೆ ಕಡಿಮೆ ಮೌಲ್ಯದ್ದಾಗಿವೆ',
      problem: "An MDP's objective typically discounts future rewards by a factor gamma (0 <= gamma <\n\n1) raised to the power of how many steps in the future the reward occurs, so a reward received immediately is worth more than the same reward received far in the future.\n\n1) Explain the mathematical necessity of this discounting for INFINITE-horizon problems (tasks that could in principle continue forever, with no fixed episode end) -- if you summed an infinite sequence of undiscounted future rewards (each rewards worth exactly 1, forever), why would the total sum diverge to infinity, making it impossible to meaningfully compare different policies by their total expected reward, and how does discounting (multiplying by gamma^t, which shrinks toward zero as t grows) fix this by guaranteeing the infinite sum converges to a finite value?\n\n2) Explain a second, more intuitive motivation for discounting beyond just mathematical convenience -- in many real decision-making problems (financial, biological, or everyday), why might a reward received SOONER genuinely be preferable to the identical reward received later, even setting aside any risk of the future reward not materializing at all (e.g. connecting to real-world time-value-of-money intuitions), and how does the discount factor's specific numeric value (closer to 1 versus closer to 0) reflect how strongly an agent should prioritize immediate over distant rewards.",
      problemKn: "ಒಂದು MDP ya objective ಸಾಮಾನ್ಯವಾಗಿ future rewards ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಂದು discount factor gamma ಇಂದ ಕಡಿಮೆ ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ, ಆದ್ದರಿಂದ ತಕ್ಷಣ ಪಡೆದ ಒಂದು reward ಭವಿಷ್ಯದಲ್ಲಿ ದೂರ ಪಡೆದ ಅದೇ reward ಗಿಂತ ಹೆಚ್ಚು ಮೌಲ್ಯಯುತವಾಗಿದೆ.\n\n1) INFINITE-horizon ಸಮಸ್ಯೆಗಳಿಗೆ ಈ discounting ya ಗಣಿತದ ಅಗತ್ಯವನ್ನೂ ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) discounting ಗಾಗಿ ಗಣಿತದ ಅನುಕೂಲ ಮೀರಿದ ಒಂದು ಎರಡನೇ, ಹೆಚ್ಚು ಸ್ವಾಭಾವಿಕ ಪ್ರೇರಣೆಯನ್ನೂ ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Computing Discounted Returns and Comparing Different Gamma Values',
      titleKn: 'Discounted Returns ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಿ ಭಿನ್ನ Gamma ಮೌಲ್ಯಗಳನ್ನೂ ಹೋಲಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Using your grid-world MDP from the previous lesson, genuinely compute discounted returns for several sample trajectories at different gamma values.\n\n1) Genuinely simulate at least 3 different trajectories (sequences of rewards) in your grid-world, and for each, compute the discounted return G = sum(gamma^t * r_t) at THREE different gamma values (e.g. 0.5, 0.9, 0.99) -- show your exact arithmetic and report the actual computed returns.\n\n2) Genuinely construct a specific comparison case: two different trajectories where trajectory A gets a smaller reward SOONER and trajectory B gets a larger reward LATER (with the same total undiscounted sum) -- report which trajectory has the higher discounted return at each of your three gamma values, and verify whether the RANKING between the two trajectories actually FLIPS depending on which gamma value you use (a low gamma should favor the sooner-reward trajectory A, while a gamma close to 1 should treat them more similarly to their undiscounted comparison) -- report your actual measured ranking at each gamma.\n\n3) Genuinely explain, using your concrete computed numbers, why the CHOICE of gamma is not just a mathematical technicality but an actual behavioral specification -- a low gamma effectively tells the agent to behave 'myopically' (caring mostly about near-term reward) while a high gamma tells it to behave more 'far-sightedly,' and different real tasks genuinely call for different choices depending on how much the task's true objective values long-term versus short-term outcomes.",
      problemKn: "ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ ನಿಮ್ಮ grid-world MDP ಬಳಸಿ, ಹಲವಾರು sample trajectories ಗಾಗಿ discounted returns ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಿ.\n\n1) ಕನಿಷ್ಠ 3 ಭಿನ್ನ trajectories ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಸಿಮ್ಯುಲೇಟ್ ಮಾಡಿ, ಮತ್ತು ಪ್ರತಿಯೊಂದಕ್ಕೂ ಮೂರು ಭಿನ್ನ gamma ಮೌಲ್ಯಗಳಲ್ಲಿ discounted return ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಿ.\n\n2) ಒಂದು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ ಹೋಲಿಕೆ ಪ್ರಕರಣ ರಚಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ranking gamma ಮೌಲ್ಯದ ಮೇಲೆ flip ಆಗುತ್ತದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ ನಿಜ ಲೆಕ್ಕಾಚಾರ ಬಳಸಿ, gamma ya ಆಯ್ಕೆ ಕೇವಲ ಒಂದು ಗಣಿತದ technicality ಅಲ್ಲ ಆದರೆ ಒಂದು ನಿಜ behavioral specification ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Measuring How Gamma Affects Learned Policy Behavior',
      titleKn: 'Gamma ಕಲಿತ Policy ವರ್ತನೆಯ ಮೇಲೆ ಹೇಗೆ ಪರಿಣಾಮ ಬೀರುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely construct a grid-world scenario where the choice of gamma should meaningfully change what the OPTIMAL policy looks like, and verify this empirically once you have a working value-computation method (this exercise anticipates the dynamic programming methods covered in the next module, so a simple brute-force approach is acceptable here).\n\n1) Genuinely design a grid-world with TWO possible goal cells: a nearby goal with a small reward, and a distant goal with a much larger reward, positioned so that reaching the distant goal requires passing through many more steps (and thus more discounting) than the nearby goal.\n\n2) Genuinely compute, by brute-force enumeration of the small number of simple candidate policies in your small grid (e.g. 'always go to nearby goal' versus 'always go to distant goal'), the discounted return each candidate policy achieves at several different gamma values.\n\n3) Genuinely report whether the OPTIMAL choice among your candidate policies flips depending on gamma (e.g. a low gamma favoring the small-but-close reward, a high gamma favoring the large-but-far reward) -- report the actual computed returns at each gamma and the specific gamma threshold (if you can identify one) where the optimal choice switches, providing a concrete, quantitative demonstration that gamma is a genuine behavioral lever with real consequences for what an RL agent will learn to do, not merely an abstract mathematical convergence parameter.",
      problemKn: "gamma ya ಆಯ್ಕೆ OPTIMAL policy ಅನ್ನೂ ಅರ್ಥಪೂರ್ಣವಾಗಿ ಬದಲಾಯಿಸಬೇಕಾದ ಒಂದು grid-world ಸನ್ನಿವೇಶವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ರಚಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಇದನ್ನೂ ಪ್ರಾಯೋಗಿಕವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಎರಡು ಸಂಭಾವ್ಯ goal cells ಇರುವ ಒಂದು grid-world ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ: ಒಂದು ಸಣ್ಣ reward ಇರುವ ಹತ್ತಿರದ goal, ಮತ್ತು ಬಹಳ ದೊಡ್ಡ reward ಇರುವ ಒಂದು ದೂರದ goal.\n\n2) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸಣ್ಣ grid ನಲ್ಲಿ ಸರಳ candidate policies ya brute-force enumeration ಮೂಲಕ, ಪ್ರತಿ candidate policy ಹಲವಾರು ಭಿನ್ನ gamma ಮೌಲ್ಯಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ಸಾಧಿಸುವ discounted return ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಿ.\n\n3) OPTIMAL ಆಯ್ಕೆ gamma ಮೇಲೆ flip ಆಗುತ್ತದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m172, l172_3, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'Policies, Value Functions, and the Relationship Between Them',
      titleKn: 'Policies, Value Functions, ಮತ್ತು ಅವುಗಳ ನಡುವಿನ ಸಂಬಂಧ',
      problem: "A policy pi specifies what action to take in each state (deterministically, or as a probability distribution over actions). A value function V^pi(s) specifies the expected discounted return an agent would get starting from state s and following policy pi thereafter.\n\n1) Explain why the value function is defined RELATIVE TO a specific policy (V^pi, not just V) -- since different policies will lead the agent through different future trajectories from the same starting state, why must the expected future return genuinely depend on which policy is being followed, making 'the value of a state' meaningless without specifying which policy's behavior you're evaluating?\n\n2) Explain the relationship between the value function V^pi and a second related function, the action-value function Q^pi(s,a) (expected return starting from state s, taking action a, THEN following policy pi thereafter) -- explain why Q^pi(s,a) is generally MORE useful than V^pi(s) alone for actually SELECTING an action (since Q directly tells you the value of each possible action from a state, letting you compare them and pick the best, whereas V alone only tells you the value of following the CURRENT policy, without directly comparing alternative actions).",
      problemKn: "ಒಂದು policy pi ಪ್ರತಿ state ನಲ್ಲಿ ಯಾವ action ತೆಗೆದುಕೊಳ್ಳಬೇಕು ಎಂದು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟಪಡಿಸುತ್ತದೆ. ಒಂದು value function V^pi(s) state s ಇಂದ ಪ್ರಾರಂಭಿಸಿ policy pi ಅನುಸರಿಸುವ ಒಂದು agent ಪಡೆಯುವ ನಿರೀಕ್ಷಿತ discounted return ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟಪಡಿಸುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) value function ಒಂದು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ policy ಗೆ RELATIVE ಆಗಿ ಏಕೆ ವ್ಯಾಖ್ಯಾನಿಸಲಾಗಿದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) value function V^pi ಮತ್ತು ಎರಡನೇ ಸಂಬಂಧಿತ function, action-value function Q^pi(s,a) ನಡುವಿನ ಸಂಬಂಧ ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Computing Value Functions for a Fixed Policy via Direct Simulation',
      titleKn: 'ಒಂದು Fixed Policy ಗಾಗಿ Direct Simulation ಮೂಲಕ Value Functions ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Using your grid-world MDP from earlier lessons in this module, genuinely estimate the value function for a FIXED, given policy via Monte Carlo simulation (running many episodes and averaging observed returns -- a preview of the Monte Carlo methods covered formally in a later module, used here just as a simple, direct estimation approach).\n\n1) Genuinely define a specific, simple fixed policy (e.g. 'always move toward the goal in the most direct straight-line sense,' ignoring obstacles for simplicity if your grid has none) for your grid-world.\n\n2) Genuinely run MANY episodes (e.g. 1000) starting from each state, following your fixed policy, and recording the discounted return for each episode -- average these returns per starting state to get an ESTIMATED V^pi(s) for every state.\n\n3) Genuinely verify a basic sanity property of your estimated value function: report whether states CLOSER to the goal (under your fixed policy) show HIGHER estimated values than states farther away, as you'd intuitively expect for a policy that moves toward the goal -- report your actual estimated values for a few specific states, and discuss how the accuracy/noise of your Monte-Carlo-estimated values would be expected to depend on how many episodes you averaged over (more episodes should reduce estimation noise, connecting to the general law-of-large-numbers intuition behind Monte Carlo estimation).",
      problemKn: "ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠಗಳ ನಿಮ್ಮ grid-world MDP ಬಳಸಿ, Monte Carlo simulation ಮೂಲಕ ಒಂದು FIXED, ಕೊಟ್ಟ policy ಗಾಗಿ value function ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಂದಾಜು ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ grid-world ಗಾಗಿ ಒಂದು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ, ಸರಳ fixed policy ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವ್ಯಾಖ್ಯಾನಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಪ್ರತಿ state ಇಂದ ಪ್ರಾರಂಭಿಸಿ, ನಿಮ್ಮ fixed policy ಅನುಸರಿಸಿ, MANY episodes ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಪ್ರತಿ episode ya discounted return ದಾಖಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಅಂದಾಜು value function ya ಒಂದು ಮೂಲಭೂತ sanity property ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Comparing Value Functions Across Multiple Policies to Find the Best One',
      titleKn: 'ಅತ್ಯುತ್ತಮ ಒಂದನ್ನೂ ಕಂಡುಹಿಡಿಯಲು ಅನೇಕ Policies ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ Value Functions ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಹೋಲಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely use your Monte-Carlo value-estimation methodology from the previous exercise to compare MULTIPLE candidate policies and empirically identify which is best -- a preview of the policy-improvement idea that will be formalized in the next module on dynamic programming.\n\n1) Genuinely define at least 3 different candidate policies for your grid-world (e.g. your original 'direct toward goal' policy, a 'random walk' policy, and one more deliberately-designed alternative policy of your choice).\n\n2) Genuinely estimate V^pi for ALL states under EACH of your 3 policies using Monte Carlo simulation (as in the previous exercise), and compute each policy's OVERALL average value (averaged across a reasonable distribution of starting states, e.g. uniformly over all states) as a single summary comparison number.\n\n3) Genuinely report which of your 3 candidate policies achieves the highest overall average value, and genuinely verify this ranking makes intuitive sense given what you know about each policy's actual behavior (e.g. does the 'direct toward goal' policy indeed outperform 'random walk' as expected?) -- report your actual measured values for all 3 policies, and discuss how this exercise of comparing candidate policies' values is conceptually the seed of POLICY ITERATION (formally covered in the next module), where you'd use exactly this kind of value comparison to iteratively improve a policy toward optimality.",
      problemKn: "ಹಿಂದಿನ exercise ya ನಿಮ್ಮ Monte-Carlo value-estimation ವಿಧಾನವನ್ನೂ ಅನೇಕ candidate policies ಹೋಲಿಸಲು ಮತ್ತು ಅತ್ಯುತ್ತಮ ಒಂದನ್ನೂ ಪ್ರಾಯೋಗಿಕವಾಗಿ ಗುರುತಿಸಲು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಬಳಸಿ.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ grid-world ಗಾಗಿ ಕನಿಷ್ಠ 3 ಭಿನ್ನ candidate policies ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವ್ಯಾಖ್ಯಾನಿಸಿ.\n\n2) EACH policy ಅಡಿಯಲ್ಲಿ ALL states ಗಾಗಿ V^pi ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಂದಾಜು ಮಾಡಿ, ಮತ್ತು ಪ್ರತಿ policy ya ಒಟ್ಟಾರೆ average value ಅನ್ನೂ ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ 3 candidate policies ಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ಯಾವುದೂ ಅತ್ಯುನ್ನತ overall average value ಸಾಧಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
  ]),

  // ── Module 173: Dynamic Programming ──
  ...set(m173, l173_1, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'The Bellman Equation: Recursion at the Heart of RL',
      titleKn: 'Bellman Equation: RL ya ಹೃದಯದಲ್ಲಿ Recursion',
      problem: "The Bellman equation expresses a state's value recursively: V^pi(s) = expected value of (immediate reward + gamma * value of the next state), where the 'value of the next state' is itself defined by the same equation applied one step later.\n\n1) Explain why this recursive structure is what makes DYNAMIC PROGRAMMING approaches to RL possible -- since the value of a state can be expressed purely in terms of the values of its immediate successor states (rather than needing to explicitly enumerate every possible full future trajectory), why does this let you compute value functions through repeated, local updates rather than needing to reason about entire infinite trajectories directly?\n\n2) Explain, at a conceptual level, why the Bellman equation requires knowing the MDP's transition probabilities and reward function explicitly (the 'model' of the environment) to be directly USED for computing values via dynamic programming -- why does this make classical dynamic programming methods (covered in this module) fundamentally different from the MODEL-FREE methods (Monte Carlo, Q-learning) covered in later modules, which learn from sampled experience without ever needing explicit access to the environment's transition/reward functions.",
      problemKn: "Bellman equation ಒಂದು state ya value ಅನ್ನೂ recursively ವ್ಯಕ್ತಪಡಿಸುತ್ತದೆ: V^pi(s) = (immediate reward + gamma * next state ya value) ya ನಿರೀಕ್ಷಿತ ಮೌಲ್ಯ, ಇಲ್ಲಿ 'next state ya value' ಸ್ವತಃ ಒಂದು ಹಂತ ನಂತರ ಅನ್ವಯಿಸಿದ ಅದೇ equation ಇಂದ ವ್ಯಾಖ್ಯಾನಿಸಲ್ಪಟ್ಟಿದೆ.\n\n1) ಈ recursive ರಚನೆ RL ಗೆ DYNAMIC PROGRAMMING ವಿಧಾನಗಳನ್ನೂ ಸಾಧ್ಯಗೊಳಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಏಕೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) Bellman equation ಅನ್ನೂ dynamic programming ಮೂಲಕ values ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಲು ನೇರವಾಗಿ ಬಳಸಲು MDP ya transition probabilities ಮತ್ತು reward function ಸ್ಪಷ್ಟವಾಗಿ ತಿಳಿಯಬೇಕು ಏಕೆ ಎಂದು ಪರಿಕಲ್ಪನಾತ್ಮಕವಾಗಿ ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing Policy Evaluation via Iterative Bellman Updates',
      titleKn: 'Iterative Bellman Updates ಮೂಲಕ Policy Evaluation ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Implement iterative policy evaluation from scratch: given a fixed policy and your grid-world's KNOWN transition/reward model (from an earlier module in this phase), repeatedly apply the Bellman equation as an UPDATE rule (V(s) <- expected value of immediate reward plus discounted next-state value, using your CURRENT value estimates for the next-state values) across all states, until the values stop changing significantly.\n\n1) Genuinely implement this iterative update and run it to convergence for the same fixed policy you used in the previous module's Monte-Carlo value estimation exercise.\n\n2) Genuinely compare your dynamic-programming-computed values against your earlier Monte-Carlo-estimated values for the SAME policy and SAME states -- report whether they agree closely (they should, since both are estimating the same true V^pi, just via different computational approaches), and report the actual measured discrepancy.\n\n3) Genuinely measure and compare the computational efficiency of the two approaches: report how many iterations your dynamic programming approach needed to converge versus how many Monte Carlo episodes your earlier exercise needed to get a comparably accurate estimate -- discuss why dynamic programming (which uses the model directly rather than sampling) can often converge with substantially less total computation than sampling-based Monte Carlo estimation, when the model is genuinely available.",
      problemKn: "ಮೊದಲಿನಿಂದ iterative policy evaluation ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಈ iterative update ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಹಿಂದಿನ module ya ನಿಮ್ಮ Monte-Carlo value estimation exercise ya ಅದೇ fixed policy ಗಾಗಿ convergence ವರೆಗೆ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ನಿಮ್ಮ dynamic-programming-computed values ಅನ್ನೂ ಅದೇ policy ಮತ್ತು states ಗಾಗಿ ನಿಮ್ಮ ಹಿಂದಿನ Monte-Carlo-estimated values ಗೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಎರಡು ವಿಧಾನಗಳ ಕಂಪ್ಯುಟೇಶನಲ್ efficiency ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆದು ಹೋಲಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Measuring Convergence Speed as a Function of Gamma and Grid Size',
      titleKn: 'Gamma ಮತ್ತು Grid Size ya ಒಂದು ಫಂಕ್ಷನ್ ಆಗಿ Convergence Speed ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely investigate how iterative policy evaluation's convergence speed depends on two factors: the discount factor gamma and the size of the state space.\n\n1) Genuinely run your iterative policy evaluation from the previous exercise at several different gamma values (e.g. 0.5, 0.9, 0.99, 0.999) on the SAME grid-world, and report the actual number of iterations needed to reach convergence (within a small tolerance) at each gamma -- report whether higher gamma values require MORE iterations to converge, and discuss why this makes sense given that the Bellman update's convergence rate is theoretically governed by gamma (a contraction-mapping argument where gamma closer to 1 means slower contraction/convergence).\n\n2) Genuinely run policy evaluation on grid-worlds of several different SIZES (e.g. 4x4, 8x8, 16x16) at a FIXED gamma, and report how the number of iterations needed to converge scales with grid size -- report your actual measured pattern.\n\n3) Genuinely report your combined findings and discuss the practical implication: for a real, much larger state space (as arises in genuinely complex real-world MDPs) combined with a gamma close to 1 (needed for tasks that genuinely care about long-term consequences), why would classical tabular dynamic programming's iteration count and per-iteration cost (which scales with the number of states) become a genuine computational bottleneck -- connecting this concern to why later modules in this phase introduce function-approximation-based methods (like DQN) that don't require explicitly representing/updating a value for every single state.",
      problemKn: "iterative policy evaluation ya convergence speed ಎರಡು ಅಂಶಗಳ ಮೇಲೆ ಹೇಗೆ ಅವಲಂಬಿತವಾಗಿದೆ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ತನಿಖೆ ಮಾಡಿ: discount factor gamma ಮತ್ತು state space ya ಗಾತ್ರ.\n\n1) ಅದೇ grid-world ಮೇಲೆ ಹಲವಾರು ಭಿನ್ನ gamma ಮೌಲ್ಯಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ನಿಮ್ಮ iterative policy evaluation ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ, ಮತ್ತು convergence ತಲುಪಲು ಬೇಕಾದ ನಿಜ ಸಂಖ್ಯೆಯ iterations ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) ಹಲವಾರು ಭಿನ್ನ ಗಾತ್ರಗಳ grid-worlds ಮೇಲೆ ಒಂದು ಸ್ಥಿರ gamma ನಲ್ಲಿ policy evaluation ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು convergence iterations ya ಸಂಖ್ಯೆ grid size ಜೊತೆ ಹೇಗೆ scale ಆಗುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸಂಯೋಜಿತ ಶೋಧನೆಗಳನ್ನೂ ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m173, l173_2, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'Policy Improvement: Acting Greedily with Respect to a Value Function',
      titleKn: 'Policy Improvement: ಒಂದು Value Function ಗೆ ಸಂಬಂಧಿಸಿ Greedily ವರ್ತಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Given a computed value function V^pi for some policy pi, POLICY IMPROVEMENT constructs a NEW, improved policy pi' by, in each state, choosing the action that maximizes expected immediate-reward-plus-discounted-next-value (a 'greedy' choice with respect to V^pi), rather than continuing to follow the original policy pi's action choice.\n\n1) Explain why the Policy Improvement Theorem guarantees this new greedy policy pi' is AT LEAST AS GOOD AS the original policy pi (V^pi'(s) >= V^pi(s) for every state s) -- at an intuitive level, why does choosing, in each state, the single best action according to what you've already learned about the CONSEQUENCES of different actions (via V^pi) never make things worse than blindly continuing with the old policy's choice?\n\n2) Explain why this greedy improvement step, despite being guaranteed to not make things worse, is not immediately guaranteed to reach the OPTIMAL policy in one step -- why might repeating the full cycle (evaluate the new policy's value, then improve again) multiple times be necessary before convergence to optimality, connecting to why this repeated evaluate-then-improve cycle is called POLICY ITERATION, the algorithm covered in the next lesson.",
      problemKn: "ಕೆಲವು policy pi ಗಾಗಿ ಒಂದು ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಿದ value function V^pi ಕೊಟ್ಟರೆ, POLICY IMPROVEMENT ಪ್ರತಿ state ನಲ್ಲಿ, expected immediate-reward-plus-discounted-next-value ಗರಿಷ್ಠಗೊಳಿಸುವ action ಆಯ್ಕೆಮಾಡುವ ಮೂಲಕ ಒಂದು ಹೊಸ, ಸುಧಾರಿಸಿದ policy pi' ನಿರ್ಮಿಸುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) Policy Improvement Theorem ಈ ಹೊಸ greedy policy pi' ಮೂಲ policy pi ಗಿಂತ ಕನಿಷ್ಠ ಅಷ್ಟೇ ಉತ್ತಮ ಎಂದು ಏಕೆ ಖಾತ್ರಿಪಡಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಈ greedy improvement ಹಂತ, ಕೆಟ್ಟದಾಗದಂತೆ ಖಾತ್ರಿಪಡಿಸಿದ್ದರೂ, ಒಂದೇ ಹಂತದಲ್ಲಿ OPTIMAL policy ತಲುಪುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ತಕ್ಷಣ ಖಾತ್ರಿಪಡಿಸುವುದಿಲ್ಲ ಏಕೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ, ಮತ್ತು ಇದೂ ಮುಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ POLICY ITERATION algorithm ಗೆ ಹೇಗೆ ಸಂಪರ್ಕಿಸುತ್ತದೆ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing Policy Improvement and Verifying It Never Makes Things Worse',
      titleKn: 'Policy Improvement ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಇದೂ ಎಂದೂ ಕೆಟ್ಟದಾಗಿಸುವುದಿಲ್ಲ ಎಂದು ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Using your iterative policy evaluation from the previous lesson, implement the policy improvement step from scratch.\n\n1) Genuinely compute V^pi for one of your simple candidate policies from an earlier module's exercise, then genuinely construct the greedy-improved policy pi' by, for each state, choosing the action that maximizes the one-step Bellman lookahead using your computed V^pi.\n\n2) Genuinely evaluate V^pi' (running policy evaluation again on this new, improved policy).\n\n3) Genuinely verify the Policy Improvement Theorem's guarantee directly: compare V^pi'(s) against V^pi(s) for EVERY state s in your grid-world, and report whether V^pi'(s) >= V^pi(s) holds for all of them (as guaranteed by the theorem) -- report your actual measured comparison across all states, including whether you find any state where the improvement is exactly zero (meaning the original policy was already optimal in that state) versus states with a genuine, measurable improvement.",
      problemKn: "ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ ನಿಮ್ಮ iterative policy evaluation ಬಳಸಿ, ಮೊದಲಿನಿಂದ policy improvement ಹಂತವನ್ನೂ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಹಿಂದಿನ module ya exercise ya ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸರಳ candidate policies ಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ಒಂದಕ್ಕೆ V^pi ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಿ, ನಂತರ greedy-improved policy pi' ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಈ ಹೊಸ, ಸುಧಾರಿಸಿದ policy ಮೇಲೆ V^pi' ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಮೌಲ್ಯಮಾಪನ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) Policy Improvement Theorem ya ಗ್ಯಾರಂಟಿಯನ್ನೂ ನೇರವಾಗಿ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Implementing Full Policy Iteration and Measuring Convergence to Optimality',
      titleKn: 'ಪೂರ್ಣ Policy Iteration ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ Optimality ಗೆ Convergence ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Implement full Policy Iteration from scratch: alternate between policy evaluation (computing V^pi via your iterative Bellman-update method from the previous lesson) and policy improvement (greedily updating the policy based on the newly-computed V^pi), repeating this evaluate-improve cycle until the policy STOPS CHANGING between iterations (a signal that you've reached a fixed point, which is provably the optimal policy).\n\n1) Genuinely run your Policy Iteration implementation to convergence on your grid-world, starting from an arbitrary (e.g. random or uniformly-fixed-direction) initial policy.\n\n2) Genuinely report how many full evaluate-improve CYCLES were needed to reach convergence (the policy stops changing) -- report your actual measured count.\n\n3) Genuinely verify your converged policy is genuinely optimal by comparing it against a brute-force alternative: for a small enough grid-world, genuinely enumerate ALL possible deterministic policies (a finite, though possibly large, number for a small grid), compute each one's value function via your policy evaluation method, and confirm that your Policy-Iteration-converged policy achieves the highest overall value among ALL enumerated alternatives -- report whether this exhaustive verification confirms your Policy Iteration implementation genuinely found the true optimal policy, providing strong empirical confidence in your implementation's correctness beyond just observing that it converged.",
      problemKn: "ಮೊದಲಿನಿಂದ ಪೂರ್ಣ Policy Iteration ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ: policy evaluation ಮತ್ತು policy improvement ನಡುವೆ ಪರ್ಯಾಯಗೊಳಿಸಿ, policy iterations ಗಳ ನಡುವೆ ಬದಲಾಗುವುದನ್ನೂ STOP ಮಾಡುವವರೆಗೆ ಈ evaluate-improve cycle ಪುನರಾವರ್ತಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಒಂದು ಅನಿಯಂತ್ರಿತ ಆರಂಭಿಕ policy ಇಂದ ಪ್ರಾರಂಭಿಸಿ, ನಿಮ್ಮ grid-world ಮೇಲೆ ನಿಮ್ಮ Policy Iteration implementation ಅನ್ನೂ convergence ವರೆಗೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ.\n\n2) convergence ತಲುಪಲು ಎಷ್ಟು ಪೂರ್ಣ evaluate-improve CYCLES ಬೇಕಾಯಿತು ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ಒಂದು brute-force ಪರ್ಯಾಯದ ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಹೋಲಿಸುವ ಮೂಲಕ ನಿಮ್ಮ converged policy ನಿಜವಾಗಿ optimal ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m173, l173_3, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'Value Iteration: Skipping Full Policy Evaluation',
      titleKn: 'Value Iteration: Full Policy Evaluation ಬಿಟ್ಟುಬಿಡುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Value Iteration is an alternative to Policy Iteration from the previous lesson: instead of running FULL policy evaluation to convergence before each improvement step, Value Iteration combines evaluation and improvement into a SINGLE update per sweep -- at each state, directly update the value estimate to the MAXIMUM over actions of the one-step Bellman lookahead (rather than the value under a currently-fixed policy), repeating this across all states until values converge, then extracting the final greedy policy only at the very end.\n\n1) Explain why Value Iteration can be understood as 'policy iteration with only ONE step of policy evaluation per improvement' -- rather than fully converging V^pi for the current policy before improving, Value Iteration takes just one Bellman update (which is like one step of the evaluation process) before immediately taking the max over actions (the improvement step) -- why does interleaving these more tightly, rather than fully separating them, still converge to the correct optimal value function?\n\n2) Explain the practical tradeoff this creates: since Policy Iteration's full evaluation step is often computationally expensive (potentially many inner iterations per single policy improvement), while Value Iteration does less total work per outer sweep but may need more outer sweeps overall to converge -- why might Value Iteration often be preferred in practice despite Policy Iteration sometimes converging in fewer POLICY-improvement steps, in terms of overall total computation.",
      problemKn: "Value Iteration ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ Policy Iteration ಗೆ ಒಂದು ಪರ್ಯಾಯವಾಗಿದೆ: ಪ್ರತಿ improvement ಹಂತದ ಮೊದಲು ಪೂರ್ಣ policy evaluation ಅನ್ನೂ convergence ವರೆಗೆ ಚಲಾಯಿಸುವ ಬದಲಿಗೆ, Value Iteration evaluation ಮತ್ತು improvement ಅನ್ನೂ ಪ್ರತಿ sweep ಗೆ ಒಂದೇ update ಗೆ ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) Value Iteration ಅನ್ನೂ 'ಪ್ರತಿ improvement ಗೆ ಕೇವಲ ONE ಹಂತದ policy evaluation ಜೊತೆ policy iteration' ಎಂದು ಏಕೆ ಅರ್ಥಮಾಡಿಕೊಳ್ಳಬಹುದು ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಇದೂ ಸೃಷ್ಟಿಸುವ ಪ್ರಾಯೋಗಿಕ tradeoff ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing Value Iteration and Verifying It Matches Policy Iteration\'s Result',
      titleKn: 'Value Iteration ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಇದೂ Policy Iteration ya ಫಲಿತಾಂಶ ಹೊಂದಿಕೆಯಾಗುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Implement Value Iteration from scratch on your grid-world.\n\n1) Genuinely implement the Value Iteration update loop (max-over-actions Bellman update applied repeatedly to all states) and run it to convergence, then extract the final greedy policy from your converged value function.\n\n2) Genuinely compare this Value-Iteration-derived optimal policy AND its value function against the optimal policy/value function your Policy Iteration implementation from the previous lesson found on the SAME grid-world -- report whether they match (they should, since both provably converge to the unique optimal solution for a well-posed MDP) -- report your actual measured comparison of both the policies and the values.\n\n3) Genuinely measure and compare TOTAL computational cost between your two implementations: count the total number of individual state-value UPDATES performed across the entire run for both Policy Iteration (summing updates across all inner evaluation loops and outer improvement steps) and Value Iteration (summing updates across all its sweeps) -- report the actual measured totals, and discuss which algorithm required less total computation on your specific grid-world, connecting your genuine measurement to the theoretical tradeoff discussed in the previous exercise.",
      problemKn: "ನಿಮ್ಮ grid-world ಮೇಲೆ ಮೊದಲಿನಿಂದ Value Iteration ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) Value Iteration update loop ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಇದನ್ನೂ convergence ವರೆಗೆ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ, ನಂತರ ಫಲಿತಾಂಶ policy ಹೊರತೆಗೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n2) ಈ Value-Iteration-derived optimal policy ಮತ್ತು value function ಅನ್ನೂ ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ ನಿಮ್ಮ Policy Iteration ya ಫಲಿತಾಂಶಗಳಿಗೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಎರಡು implementations ನಡುವೆ ಒಟ್ಟು ಕಂಪ್ಯುಟೇಶನಲ್ ವೆಚ್ಚವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆದು ಹೋಲಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Measuring Convergence Behavior of Value Iteration Under Different Initializations',
      titleKn: 'ಭಿನ್ನ Initializations ಅಡಿಯಲ್ಲಿ Value Iteration ya Convergence ವರ್ತನೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely investigate whether Value Iteration's convergence speed and final result depend on how you initialize the value function before starting the iteration (theory says Value Iteration converges to the SAME optimal values regardless of initialization, but genuinely verify this and measure any speed differences).\n\n1) Genuinely run Value Iteration on your grid-world starting from THREE different initializations: all-zero values, all-very-negative values, and all-very-positive/optimistic values.\n\n2) Genuinely report, for each initialization, the number of sweeps needed to converge (within a fixed tolerance) -- report whether initialization affects convergence SPEED (it likely will, to some degree) while confirming the FINAL converged values are identical (or nearly so, within tolerance) across all three initializations, regardless of starting point.\n\n3) Genuinely discuss your measured findings in terms of a real practical concern: for a much larger, more complex real-world MDP where you might have SOME prior domain knowledge suggesting reasonable initial value estimates (rather than starting from zero), would a well-informed 'warm start' initialization genuinely be expected to speed up convergence based on your measured pattern -- and discuss why value iteration's provable convergence to the unique optimum REGARDLESS of initialization is a reassuring theoretical guarantee (you can't get a 'wrong' answer from a bad initialization, only potentially a slower one) that distinguishes it from methods without such convergence guarantees.",
      problemKn: "Value Iteration ya convergence speed ಮತ್ತು ಅಂತಿಮ ಫಲಿತಾಂಶ iteration ಪ್ರಾರಂಭಿಸುವ ಮೊದಲು value function ಅನ್ನೂ ಹೇಗೆ ಆರಂಭಿಸುತ್ತೀರಿ ಎಂಬುದನ್ನೂ ಅವಲಂಬಿಸಿದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ತನಿಖೆ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n1) ಮೂರು ಭಿನ್ನ initializations ಇಂದ ಪ್ರಾರಂಭಿಸಿ ನಿಮ್ಮ grid-world ಮೇಲೆ Value Iteration ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಪ್ರತಿ initialization ಗೆ, convergence ತಲುಪಲು ಬೇಕಾದ sweeps ya ಸಂಖ್ಯೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ, ಮತ್ತು ಅಂತಿಮ converged values ಎಲ್ಲಾ ಮೂರರಲ್ಲೂ identical ಆಗಿವೆ ಎಂದು ಖಚಿತಪಡಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಅಳೆದ ಶೋಧನೆಗಳನ್ನೂ ಒಂದು ನಿಜ ಪ್ರಾಯೋಗಿಕ ಕಾಳಜಿಯ ಪರಿಭಾಷೆಯಲ್ಲಿ ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),

  // ── Module 174: Monte Carlo Methods ──
  ...set(m174, l174_1, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'Learning from Experience: Why Monte Carlo Doesn\'t Need the Model',
      titleKn: 'Experience ಇಂದ ಕಲಿಯುವುದೂ: Monte Carlo ಗೆ Model ಏಕೆ ಬೇಕಿಲ್ಲ',
      problem: "Unlike the dynamic programming methods from the previous module (which require KNOWING the MDP's exact transition probabilities and reward function to compute updates), Monte Carlo methods learn value functions purely by AVERAGING OBSERVED RETURNS from actually experienced (or simulated) episodes -- no explicit model of the environment's dynamics is required at all.\n\n1) Explain why this makes Monte Carlo methods 'model-free' -- since you only need to be able to GENERATE episodes (by interacting with the environment, real or simulated) and observe what returns actually resulted, why don't you need to know the underlying transition probabilities that produced those episodes, unlike dynamic programming's Bellman-update approach which explicitly uses those probabilities in its update formula?\n\n2) Explain why this model-free property matters enormously for REAL-WORLD applicability -- give a concrete example of a real decision-making problem where you could plausibly interact with the environment and observe outcomes, but where writing down the exact mathematical transition probabilities (as dynamic programming would require) would be extremely difficult or genuinely impossible.",
      problemKn: "ಹಿಂದಿನ module ya dynamic programming ವಿಧಾನಗಳಿಗೆ ವಿರುದ್ಧವಾಗಿ, Monte Carlo ವಿಧಾನಗಳು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅನುಭವಿಸಿದ (ಅಥವಾ simulated) episodes ಇಂದ OBSERVED RETURNS ಅನ್ನೂ ಸರಾಸರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವ ಮೂಲಕ ಶುದ್ಧವಾಗಿ value functions ಕಲಿಯುತ್ತವೆ -- ಪರಿಸರ ya dynamics ya ಯಾವುದೇ ಸ್ಪಷ್ಟ model ಸಂಪೂರ್ಣವಾಗಿ ಅಗತ್ಯವಿಲ್ಲ.\n\n1) ಇದೂ Monte Carlo ವಿಧಾನಗಳನ್ನೂ 'model-free' ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ ಏಕೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಈ model-free property ನಿಜ-ಜಗತ್ತಿನ ಅನ್ವಯಿಸುವಿಕೆಗೆ ಏಕೆ ಬಹಳ ಮುಖ್ಯ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ, ಒಂದು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ ಉದಾಹರಣೆಯೊಂದಿಗೆ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing First-Visit Monte Carlo Policy Evaluation',
      titleKn: 'First-Visit Monte Carlo Policy Evaluation ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Implement First-Visit Monte Carlo policy evaluation from scratch: for a fixed policy, generate many episodes, and for each state, average the return following the FIRST time that state is visited within each episode (ignoring subsequent visits to the same state within the same episode, if any) across all episodes to estimate V^pi(s).\n\n1) Genuinely implement this algorithm on your grid-world MDP from earlier modules, using the same fixed policy you used for your dynamic-programming-based evaluation in an earlier module.\n\n2) Genuinely run your First-Visit Monte Carlo evaluation with an increasing number of episodes (e.g. 10, 100, 1000, 10000) and, at each episode count, compare your Monte-Carlo-estimated V^pi against the TRUE V^pi computed via dynamic programming in an earlier module (which you can treat as ground truth, since dynamic programming converges exactly given the true model) -- report the actual measured estimation error at each episode count.\n\n3) Genuinely report whether estimation error DECREASES as episode count increases (as the law of large numbers would predict for Monte Carlo averaging), and estimate roughly how many episodes were needed to get within a small, specific error tolerance of the true values on your grid-world -- report your actual measured convergence pattern.",
      problemKn: "ಮೊದಲಿನಿಂದ First-Visit Monte Carlo policy evaluation ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಈ algorithm ಅನ್ನೂ ಹಿಂದಿನ modules ya ನಿಮ್ಮ grid-world MDP ಮೇಲೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ, ಹಿಂದಿನ module ya ನಿಮ್ಮ dynamic-programming-based evaluation ಗಾಗಿ ಬಳಸಿದ ಅದೇ fixed policy ಬಳಸಿ.\n\n2) ಹೆಚ್ಚುತ್ತಿರುವ ಸಂಖ್ಯೆಯ episodes ಜೊತೆ ನಿಮ್ಮ First-Visit Monte Carlo evaluation ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಪ್ರತಿ episode count ನಲ್ಲಿ ನಿಮ್ಮ Monte-Carlo-estimated V^pi ಅನ್ನೂ TRUE V^pi ಗೆ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) episode count ಹೆಚ್ಚಾದಂತೆ estimation error ಕಡಿಮೆಯಾಗುತ್ತದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Comparing First-Visit and Every-Visit Monte Carlo',
      titleKn: 'First-Visit ಮತ್ತು Every-Visit Monte Carlo ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಹೋಲಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "An alternative to First-Visit Monte Carlo is Every-Visit Monte Carlo, which averages the return following EVERY visit to a state within an episode (not just the first), including states visited multiple times within a single episode. Genuinely investigate whether this choice matters in practice.\n\n1) Genuinely implement Every-Visit Monte Carlo evaluation alongside your First-Visit implementation from the previous exercise.\n\n2) Genuinely construct or identify a specific grid-world scenario/policy where a state is GENUINELY likely to be visited multiple times within a single episode (e.g. a policy that can loop back through the same cell) -- run both First-Visit and Every-Visit Monte Carlo on this scenario with the SAME number of episodes.\n\n3) Genuinely compare the estimation ACCURACY (against the true dynamic-programming-computed values) and estimation VARIANCE (run each method multiple independent times with different random seeds and measure how much the estimates vary across runs) between First-Visit and Every-Visit -- report your actual measured comparison, and discuss which method converges faster/more reliably on your specific multi-visit-prone scenario, connecting to the theoretical property that Every-Visit MC is technically a biased estimator in finite samples (though still asymptotically consistent) while First-Visit MC is unbiased even in finite samples, and discussing whether this theoretical distinction shows up as a measurable practical difference in your specific experiment.",
      problemKn: "First-Visit Monte Carlo ಗೆ ಒಂದು ಪರ್ಯಾಯವೆಂದರೆ Every-Visit Monte Carlo, ಇದೂ ಒಂದು episode ಒಳಗೆ ಒಂದು state ಗೆ EVERY ಭೇಟಿಯ ನಂತರದ return ಅನ್ನೂ ಸರಾಸರಿಗೊಳಿಸುತ್ತದೆ. ಈ ಆಯ್ಕೆ ಪ್ರಾಯೋಗಿಕವಾಗಿ ಮುಖ್ಯವಾಗಿದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ತನಿಖೆ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n1) ಹಿಂದಿನ exercise ya ನಿಮ್ಮ First-Visit implementation ಜೊತೆಗೆ Every-Visit Monte Carlo evaluation ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಒಂದು state ಒಂದೇ episode ಒಳಗೆ ಅನೇಕ ಬಾರಿ ಭೇಟಿಯಾಗುವ ಸಾಧ್ಯತೆ ಇರುವ ಒಂದು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ ಸನ್ನಿವೇಶವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ರಚಿಸಿ ಅಥವಾ ಗುರುತಿಸಿ.\n\n3) First-Visit ಮತ್ತು Every-Visit ನಡುವೆ estimation accuracy ಮತ್ತು estimation variance ಎರಡನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m174, l174_2, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'Exploring Starts and the On-Policy Control Problem',
      titleKn: 'Exploring Starts ಮತ್ತು On-Policy Control ಸಮಸ್ಯೆ',
      problem: "To use Monte Carlo methods for CONTROL (finding a good policy, not just evaluating a given one), you need to estimate Q(s,a) (not just V(s)) for many state-action pairs, and improve the policy to be greedy with respect to Q -- but this creates a genuine problem: if you always act greedily according to your CURRENT Q estimates, you might never actually try certain actions, so you can never learn whether they might actually be BETTER than your current greedy choice.\n\n1) Explain this exploration problem concretely with an example: describe a scenario where a Monte Carlo control algorithm that always picks the currently-best-looking action would get permanently 'stuck' on a suboptimal action, simply because it never tries the genuinely better alternative enough times to discover it's better.\n\n2) Explain the 'exploring starts' assumption as one theoretical fix -- every episode begins from a RANDOMLY chosen state-action pair (not necessarily one the current policy would choose), guaranteeing every state-action pair eventually gets tried infinitely often across enough episodes -- and explain why this assumption, while mathematically convenient for proving convergence, is often IMPRACTICAL for real environments (where you typically can't just teleport an agent to start from an arbitrary state-action pair of your choosing), motivating the epsilon-greedy alternative covered in the next exercise.",
      problemKn: "CONTROL ಗಾಗಿ Monte Carlo ವಿಧಾನಗಳನ್ನೂ ಬಳಸಲು, ನೀವು ಅನೇಕ state-action pairs ಗಾಗಿ Q(s,a) ಅಂದಾಜು ಮಾಡಬೇಕು ಮತ್ತು Q ಗೆ ಸಂಬಂಧಿಸಿ greedy ಆಗಿರಲು policy ಸುಧಾರಿಸಬೇಕು -- ಆದರೆ ಇದೂ ಒಂದು ನಿಜ ಸಮಸ್ಯೆ ಸೃಷ್ಟಿಸುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) ಈ exploration ಸಮಸ್ಯೆಯನ್ನೂ ಒಂದು ಉದಾಹರಣೆಯೊಂದಿಗೆ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟವಾಗಿ ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) 'exploring starts' ಊಹೆಯನ್ನೂ ಒಂದು ಸೈದ್ಧಾಂತಿಕ ಪರಿಹಾರವಾಗಿ ವಿವರಿಸಿ, ಮತ್ತು ಇದೂ ನಿಜ ಪರಿಸರಗಳಿಗೆ ಏಕೆ ಅಪ್ರಾಯೋಗಿಕವಾಗಿದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing Monte Carlo Control with Epsilon-Greedy Exploration',
      titleKn: 'Epsilon-Greedy Exploration ಜೊತೆ Monte Carlo Control ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Implement On-Policy Monte Carlo Control with epsilon-greedy exploration from scratch: maintain Q(s,a) estimates, and act according to an epsilon-greedy policy (with probability 1-epsilon choose the currently-best action according to Q, otherwise choose a uniformly random action), updating Q(s,a) via Monte Carlo averaging of observed returns (reusing your First-Visit MC methodology from the previous lesson, applied to state-ACTION pairs instead of just states) after each episode.\n\n1) Genuinely implement this algorithm and run it on your grid-world for many episodes, tracking the total return achieved per episode over training.\n\n2) Genuinely report whether episode returns IMPROVE over the course of training (evidence the policy is genuinely learning to act better) -- plot/report your actual measured learning curve.\n\n3) Genuinely extract the final learned policy (acting greedily according to your final Q estimates) and compare it against the TRUE optimal policy you found via dynamic programming in an earlier module -- report whether your Monte-Carlo-learned policy matches or comes close to the true optimal policy, and genuinely test a few different epsilon values (e.g. 0.01, 0.1, 0.5) to see how the exploration-exploitation tradeoff affects both learning speed and final policy quality -- report your actual measured comparison across epsilon values.",
      problemKn: "ಮೊದಲಿನಿಂದ epsilon-greedy exploration ಜೊತೆ On-Policy Monte Carlo Control ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಈ algorithm ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ನಿಮ್ಮ grid-world ಮೇಲೆ ಹಲವಾರು episodes ಗಾಗಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ, training ಅವಧಿಯಲ್ಲಿ ಪ್ರತಿ episode ya ಒಟ್ಟು return ಟ್ರ್ಯಾಕ್ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) episode returns training ಅವಧಿಯಲ್ಲಿ ಸುಧಾರಿಸುತ್ತವೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ಫಲಿತಾಂಶ ಕಲಿತ policy ಅನ್ನೂ ಹಿಂದಿನ module ya ನಿಜ optimal policy ಗೆ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ, ಮತ್ತು ಕೆಲವು ಭಿನ್ನ epsilon ಮೌಲ್ಯಗಳನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Implementing Epsilon Decay and Measuring Its Effect on Final Policy Quality',
      titleKn: 'Epsilon Decay ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಅಂತಿಮ Policy Quality ಮೇಲಿನ ಅದರ ಪರಿಣಾಮ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "A fixed epsilon means the agent NEVER stops exploring randomly, even after it has learned a good policy -- genuinely wasting some fraction of episodes on random exploration indefinitely. A common refinement is EPSILON DECAY: starting with a high epsilon (favoring exploration early, when the agent knows little) and gradually reducing it over training (favoring exploitation later, once the agent has learned more). Genuinely investigate this refinement.\n\n1) Genuinely implement an epsilon decay schedule (e.g. epsilon starting at 1.0 and decaying multiplicatively toward a small final value like 0.01 over the course of training) and integrate it into your Monte Carlo Control implementation from the previous exercise.\n\n2) Genuinely train with this decaying-epsilon schedule and compare the resulting LEARNING CURVE (episode return over training) against your fixed-epsilon baselines from the previous exercise -- report whether decay produces a learning curve that improves more and eventually stabilizes at a HIGHER final average return (since less exploration noise remains late in training) compared to a fixed moderate epsilon.\n\n3) Genuinely compare final policy quality (how close the FINAL learned policy comes to true optimal, measured via your value comparison methodology from earlier exercises) between your best fixed-epsilon run and your epsilon-decay run -- report your actual measured comparison, and discuss why epsilon decay is a common practical technique precisely because it tries to get the best of both worlds: enough early exploration to discover good actions, and enough late exploitation to actually reliably execute on what was learned, avoiding fixed-epsilon's permanent tradeoff between these two competing needs.",
      problemKn: "ಒಂದು fixed epsilon ಎಂದರೆ agent ಎಂದೂ ಯಾದೃಚ್ಛಿಕ exploration ನಿಲ್ಲಿಸುವುದಿಲ್ಲ, ಒಂದು ಉತ್ತಮ policy ಕಲಿತ ನಂತರವೂ. ಒಂದು ಸಾಮಾನ್ಯ ಸುಧಾರಣೆ EPSILON DECAY ಆಗಿದೆ. ಈ ಸುಧಾರಣೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ತನಿಖೆ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n1) ಒಂದು epsilon decay schedule ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಇದನ್ನೂ ಹಿಂದಿನ exercise ya ನಿಮ್ಮ Monte Carlo Control implementation ಗೆ ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಈ decaying-epsilon schedule ಜೊತೆ train ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಫಲಿತಾಂಶ learning curve ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ fixed-epsilon baselines ಗೆ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಅತ್ಯುತ್ತಮ fixed-epsilon run ಮತ್ತು ನಿಮ್ಮ epsilon-decay run ನಡುವೆ ಅಂತಿಮ policy quality ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m174, l174_3, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'On-Policy versus Off-Policy: Learning About One Policy While Following Another',
      titleKn: 'On-Policy vs Off-Policy: ಒಂದು Policy ಅನುಸರಿಸುತ್ತಾ ಇನ್ನೊಂದರ ಬಗ್ಗೆ ಕಲಿಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "On-Policy Monte Carlo Control (from the previous lesson) learns about the SAME policy it uses to generate behavior (e.g. the epsilon-greedy policy is both what generates the episodes AND what gets evaluated/improved). Off-Policy methods instead learn about a different TARGET policy (often the fully greedy, deterministic policy) while generating experience using a different, more exploratory BEHAVIOR policy -- using importance sampling to correctly reweight the observed returns to account for the difference between the two policies.\n\n1) Explain why you might genuinely WANT this separation between behavior and target policy -- give a concrete motivation (e.g. wanting to learn the best possible deterministic policy for eventual deployment, while still exploring with a stochastic policy during training/data-collection, since a fully deterministic policy would never explore at all).\n\n2) Explain, at a conceptual level, why simply using returns generated under the exploratory behavior policy to directly estimate the target policy's value WITHOUT any correction would be systematically WRONG -- since the behavior policy might take actions the target policy never would (or take some actions with different frequency), why would naively averaging behavior-policy-generated returns give a biased estimate of the target policy's true value, and what role does importance sampling (reweighting each observed return by the probability ratio between target and behavior policies) play in correcting for this mismatch.",
      problemKn: "On-Policy Monte Carlo Control ಅದೇ policy ya ಬಗ್ಗೆ ಕಲಿಯುತ್ತದೆ ಅದೂ behavior ಉತ್ಪಾದಿಸಲು ಬಳಸುತ್ತದೆ. Off-Policy ವಿಧಾನಗಳು ಬದಲಿಗೆ ಒಂದು ಭಿನ್ನ TARGET policy ya ಬಗ್ಗೆ ಕಲಿಯುತ್ತವೆ, ಒಂದು ಭಿನ್ನ, ಹೆಚ್ಚು exploratory BEHAVIOR policy ಬಳಸಿ experience ಉತ್ಪಾದಿಸುತ್ತಾ.\n\n1) ನೀವು ಈ ಪ್ರತ್ಯೇಕತೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಏಕೆ ಬಯಸಬಹುದು ಎಂದು ಒಂದು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ ಪ್ರೇರಣೆಯೊಂದಿಗೆ ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) exploratory behavior policy ಅಡಿಯಲ್ಲಿ ಉತ್ಪಾದಿಸಿದ returns ಅನ್ನೂ ಯಾವುದೇ ತಿದ್ದುಪಡಿ ಇಲ್ಲದೆ ನೇರವಾಗಿ target policy ya value ಅಂದಾಜು ಮಾಡಲು ಬಳಸುವುದೂ ಏಕೆ ವ್ಯವಸ್ಥಿತವಾಗಿ ತಪ್ಪಾಗಿರುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ಪರಿಕಲ್ಪನಾತ್ಮಕವಾಗಿ ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing Off-Policy Monte Carlo with Importance Sampling',
      titleKn: 'Importance Sampling ಜೊತೆ Off-Policy Monte Carlo ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Implement Off-Policy Monte Carlo evaluation with importance sampling from scratch: generate episodes using an exploratory behavior policy (e.g. a fixed epsilon-greedy or uniformly random policy), but estimate the value of a DIFFERENT target policy (e.g. the fully greedy policy derived from your Q-values) by reweighting each episode's observed return by the importance sampling ratio (the product, across all steps in the episode, of target_policy_probability / behavior_policy_probability for each action actually taken).\n\n1) Genuinely implement this weighted-averaging procedure on your grid-world.\n\n2) Genuinely verify correctness: compare your off-policy-estimated value of the target (greedy) policy against the TRUE value of that same policy computed via dynamic programming in an earlier module (ground truth) -- report the actual measured estimation error.\n\n3) Genuinely investigate importance sampling's known high-variance problem: report the actual distribution of importance sampling ratios you observe across your episodes (are some ratios extremely large, e.g. because a low-probability-under-target action was taken many times under behavior?), and discuss how this connects to why off-policy Monte Carlo with importance sampling, despite being theoretically unbiased, can require substantially MORE episodes than on-policy methods to achieve comparably low estimation variance -- report whether you observe this practical difficulty (e.g. noisier/slower-converging estimates) on your specific small-scale implementation.",
      problemKn: "ಮೊದಲಿನಿಂದ importance sampling ಜೊತೆ Off-Policy Monte Carlo evaluation ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಈ weighted-averaging procedure ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ grid-world ಮೇಲೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n2) correctness ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ: ನಿಮ್ಮ off-policy-estimated target policy value ಅನ್ನೂ dynamic programming ಇಂದ ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಿದ TRUE ಮೌಲ್ಯಕ್ಕೆ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) importance sampling ya ತಿಳಿದ high-variance ಸಮಸ್ಯೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ತನಿಖೆ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Comparing Ordinary and Weighted Importance Sampling',
      titleKn: 'Ordinary ಮತ್ತು Weighted Importance Sampling ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಹೋಲಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "'Ordinary' importance sampling (the straightforward version you implemented in the previous exercise) is an unbiased but potentially high-variance estimator. 'Weighted' importance sampling instead normalizes by the sum of importance-sampling weights across episodes (rather than dividing by the raw episode count), trading a small amount of bias for substantially reduced variance. Genuinely compare these two variants.\n\n1) Genuinely implement Weighted Importance Sampling alongside your Ordinary Importance Sampling from the previous exercise.\n\n2) Genuinely run both estimators on the SAME set of behavior-policy-generated episodes (same underlying data, just different weighting/averaging formulas), and compare their estimated target-policy values against the TRUE dynamic-programming-computed values -- report the actual measured estimation error for both.\n\n3) Genuinely measure ESTIMATION VARIANCE for both methods: run each estimator multiple independent times with different random episode batches (of the same size) and measure how much the estimates vary across runs -- report whether Weighted Importance Sampling shows measurably LOWER variance than Ordinary Importance Sampling on your specific setup (as the theory predicts), and whether this comes at the cost of measurably higher BIAS (systematic deviation from the true value, visible especially at small episode counts) -- report your genuine measured bias-variance tradeoff between the two methods, and discuss why Weighted Importance Sampling is generally preferred in practice despite its theoretical bias, given that the variance reduction is often the more practically impactful consideration for getting usable estimates from a limited number of episodes.",
      problemKn: "'Ordinary' importance sampling ಒಂದು unbiased ಆದರೆ ಸಂಭಾವ್ಯ high-variance estimator ಆಗಿದೆ. 'Weighted' importance sampling ಬದಲಿಗೆ episodes ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ importance-sampling weights ya ಮೊತ್ತದಿಂದ normalize ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ. ಈ ಎರಡು variants ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಹಿಂದಿನ exercise ya ನಿಮ್ಮ Ordinary Importance Sampling ಜೊತೆಗೆ Weighted Importance Sampling ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಅದೇ episodes ಸೆಟ್ ಮೇಲೆ ಎರಡೂ estimators ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು TRUE ಮೌಲ್ಯಗಳಿಗೆ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಎರಡಕ್ಕೂ ESTIMATION VARIANCE ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ ಮತ್ತು ಫಲಿತಾಂಶ bias-variance tradeoff ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),

  // ── Module 175: Q-Learning, SARSA ──
  ...set(m175, l175_1, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'Temporal Difference Learning: Updating from an Incomplete Episode',
      titleKn: 'Temporal Difference Learning: ಅಪೂರ್ಣ Episode ಇಂದ Update ಮಾಡುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Monte Carlo methods (from the previous module) must wait until an ENTIRE episode finishes before updating any value estimates, since they need the full observed return. Temporal Difference (TD) learning instead updates value estimates after just ONE step, using the observed immediate reward PLUS the current (possibly still-inaccurate) estimated value of the next state as a 'bootstrap' target, rather than waiting for the true final return.\n\n1) Explain why this bootstrapping approach (using your own current, possibly-wrong value estimate as part of the update target) is a genuinely different kind of learning than Monte Carlo's approach (using only the actual, true observed return) -- what's the specific tradeoff: TD can update immediately after every single step (not needing to wait for episode completion), but its update target is itself an ESTIMATE (potentially biased/wrong early in training) rather than ground truth, unlike Monte Carlo's target which is always the genuine observed return.\n\n2) Explain why TD learning can be used in problems Monte Carlo cannot handle as naturally -- specifically CONTINUING tasks that never terminate (no natural 'episode end' to wait for) -- why does TD's step-by-step updating make it applicable to such continuing tasks in a way that Monte Carlo's need-to-wait-for-the-full-return approach fundamentally cannot support.",
      problemKn: "ಹಿಂದಿನ module ya Monte Carlo ವಿಧಾನಗಳು ಯಾವುದೇ value estimates ಅಪ್ಡೇಟ್ ಮಾಡುವ ಮೊದಲು ಒಂದು ENTIRE episode ಮುಗಿಯುವವರೆಗೆ ಕಾಯಬೇಕು. Temporal Difference (TD) learning ಬದಲಿಗೆ ಕೇವಲ ONE ಹಂತದ ನಂತರ value estimates ಅಪ್ಡೇಟ್ ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ, ನಿಜ ಅಂತಿಮ return ಗಾಗಿ ಕಾಯುವ ಬದಲಿಗೆ ಒಂದು 'bootstrap' ಗುರಿ ಬಳಸಿ.\n\n1) ಈ bootstrapping ವಿಧಾನ Monte Carlo ya ವಿಧಾನಕ್ಕಿಂತ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಭಿನ್ನ ಬಗೆಯ learning ಏಕೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) TD learning ಅನ್ನೂ Monte Carlo ನಿರ್ವಹಿಸಲಾಗದ ಸಮಸ್ಯೆಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ಏಕೆ ಬಳಸಬಹುದು ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ, ವಿಶೇಷವಾಗಿ ಎಂದೂ ಮುಗಿಯದ CONTINUING tasks ಗಳಲ್ಲಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing SARSA and Comparing Its Learning Curve to Monte Carlo',
      titleKn: 'SARSA ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಅದರ Learning Curve ಅನ್ನೂ Monte Carlo ಗೆ ಹೋಲಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Implement SARSA (State-Action-Reward-State-Action) from scratch: an ON-POLICY TD control algorithm that updates Q(s,a) after each single step using the TD target r + gamma*Q(s',a') (where a' is the action ACTUALLY taken next under the current epsilon-greedy policy, giving SARSA its name from the quintuple it uses for each update).\n\n1) Genuinely implement this algorithm and train it on your grid-world MDP from earlier modules in this phase.\n\n2) Genuinely track the total return per episode over training, and compare this learning curve against your On-Policy Monte Carlo Control's learning curve from an earlier module (same grid-world, same epsilon schedule if possible) -- report whether SARSA's per-episode updates lead to visibly SMOOTHER or FASTER early learning progress compared to Monte Carlo's wait-for-full-episode updates, or whether the difference is less pronounced on your specific small grid-world.\n\n3) Genuinely extract SARSA's final learned policy and compare it against the true optimal policy from dynamic programming -- report whether SARSA converges to a comparably good policy as your Monte Carlo Control implementation did, and report your actual measured comparison of both learning speed and final policy quality between the two model-free control approaches.",
      problemKn: "ಮೊದಲಿನಿಂದ SARSA ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ: ಒಂದು ON-POLICY TD control algorithm, ಇದೂ ಪ್ರತಿ single ಹಂತದ ನಂತರ TD target r + gamma*Q(s',a') ಬಳಸಿ Q(s,a) ಅಪ್ಡೇಟ್ ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) ಈ algorithm ಅನ್ನೂ ಈ phase ya ಹಿಂದಿನ modules ya ನಿಮ್ಮ grid-world MDP ಮೇಲೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) ಪ್ರತಿ episode ya ಒಟ್ಟು return ಅನ್ನೂ ಟ್ರ್ಯಾಕ್ ಮಾಡಿ, ಮತ್ತು ಈ learning curve ಅನ್ನೂ ಹಿಂದಿನ module ya ನಿಮ್ಮ On-Policy Monte Carlo Control ya learning curve ಗೆ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) SARSA ya ಫಲಿತಾಂಶ ಕಲಿತ policy ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜ optimal policy ಗೆ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Measuring the Effect of Step-Size (Learning Rate) on SARSA\'s Convergence',
      titleKn: 'SARSA ya Convergence ಮೇಲೆ Step-Size (Learning Rate) ya ಪರಿಣಾಮವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Unlike Monte Carlo's simple averaging, TD methods like SARSA use an explicit learning rate/step-size (alpha) to control how much each new TD update shifts the current Q estimate -- Q(s,a) <- Q(s,a) + alpha * (TD_target - Q(s,a)). Genuinely investigate this hyperparameter's effect.\n\n1) Genuinely train your SARSA implementation from the previous exercise at several different fixed alpha values (e.g. 0.01, 0.1, 0.5, 0.9) on the same grid-world, and track the learning curve (episode return over training) for each.\n\n2) Genuinely report whether a very SMALL alpha produces slow-but-stable learning (gradual, low-variance improvement) while a very LARGE alpha produces fast-but-noisy or unstable learning (rapid but volatile changes in the learning curve, potentially even worsening at times) -- report your actual measured learning curves and characterize the qualitative differences you observe at each alpha value.\n\n3) Genuinely implement a simple DECAYING alpha schedule (starting high and reducing over training, similar in spirit to your epsilon decay from an earlier module) and compare its learning curve against your best FIXED alpha from step 1 -- report whether decaying alpha achieves a genuinely better combination of fast early learning and stable late-training convergence than any single fixed alpha value could, and discuss why this alpha-scheduling consideration is analogous to (and often used alongside) the epsilon-scheduling consideration from earlier RL exercises in this phase -- both are examples of adapting an exploration/learning-rate hyperparameter over the course of training rather than using one fixed value throughout.",
      problemKn: "Monte Carlo ya ಸರಳ ಸರಾಸರಿಗೆ ವಿರುದ್ಧವಾಗಿ, SARSA ಯಂತಹ TD ವಿಧಾನಗಳು ಒಂದು ಸ್ಪಷ್ಟ learning rate/step-size (alpha) ಬಳಸುತ್ತವೆ. ಈ hyperparameter ya ಪರಿಣಾಮವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ತನಿಖೆ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n1) ಅದೇ grid-world ಮೇಲೆ ಹಲವಾರು ಭಿನ್ನ fixed alpha ಮೌಲ್ಯಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ಹಿಂದಿನ exercise ya ನಿಮ್ಮ SARSA implementation ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) ಒಂದು ಬಹಳ ಚಿಕ್ಕ alpha ನಿಧಾನ-ಆದರೆ-ಸ್ಥಿರ learning ಉತ್ಪಾದಿಸುತ್ತದೆಯೇ ಮತ್ತು ಒಂದು ಬಹಳ ದೊಡ್ಡ alpha ವೇಗದ-ಆದರೆ-ಗದ್ದಲದ learning ಉತ್ಪಾದಿಸುತ್ತದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ಒಂದು ಸರಳ DECAYING alpha schedule ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಫಲಿತಾಂಶಗಳನ್ನೂ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m175, l175_2, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'Q-Learning: Off-Policy TD Control',
      titleKn: 'Q-Learning: Off-Policy TD Control',
      problem: "Q-Learning is an OFF-POLICY TD control algorithm: unlike SARSA (from the previous lesson), which updates Q(s,a) using the value of the action ACTUALLY taken next (a', chosen by the current exploratory policy), Q-Learning updates using the MAXIMUM Q-value over all possible next actions (max_a' Q(s',a')), regardless of which action the exploratory behavior policy actually takes next.\n\n1) Explain why this single formula change makes Q-Learning off-policy -- since the update target uses the BEST possible next action (as judged by current Q estimates) rather than the actually-taken next action, explain why Q-Learning is effectively learning about the OPTIMAL (greedy) target policy while its actual behavior can follow a different, more exploratory policy (e.g. epsilon-greedy) -- connecting this directly to the on-policy/off-policy distinction covered conceptually for Monte Carlo methods in an earlier module.\n\n2) Explain a genuine practical consequence of this difference: in an environment with a risk of occasionally very bad outcomes if exploration leads the agent into a dangerous state (e.g. a 'cliff' the agent could fall off while exploring), why might SARSA (which accounts for the actual exploratory behavior in its updates) learn a more CAUTIOUS policy that avoids risky areas the exploring behavior policy might wander into, while Q-Learning (which always assumes optimal future behavior in its updates) might learn a more AGGRESSIVE, optimal-assuming policy that takes a riskier but shorter path, even though the actual epsilon-greedy exploration used during training could occasionally cause it to fall off the cliff.",
      problemKn: "Q-Learning ಒಂದು OFF-POLICY TD control algorithm ಆಗಿದೆ: ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ SARSA ಗೆ ವಿರುದ್ಧವಾಗಿ, ಇದೂ ಎಲ್ಲಾ ಸಂಭಾವ್ಯ next actions ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ MAXIMUM Q-value ಬಳಸಿ Q(s,a) ಅಪ್ಡೇಟ್ ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ, exploratory behavior policy ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಮುಂದೆ ಯಾವ action ತೆಗೆದುಕೊಳ್ಳುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂಬುದನ್ನೂ ಲೆಕ್ಕಿಸದೆ.\n\n1) ಈ single formula ಬದಲಾವಣೆ Q-Learning ಅನ್ನೂ off-policy ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ ಏಕೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಈ ವ್ಯತ್ಯಾಸದ ಒಂದು ನಿಜ ಪ್ರಾಯೋಗಿಕ ಪರಿಣಾಮ ವಿವರಿಸಿ, ಅಪಾಯಕಾರಿ ಸ್ಥಿತಿಗಳಿರುವ ಒಂದು ಪರಿಸರದ ಉದಾಹರಣೆಯೊಂದಿಗೆ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing Q-Learning and Comparing It Head-to-Head with SARSA',
      titleKn: 'Q-Learning ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ SARSA ಜೊತೆ Head-to-Head ಹೋಲಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Implement Q-Learning from scratch on your grid-world, and genuinely construct the classic 'cliff-walking' style scenario to make the SARSA-versus-Q-Learning behavioral difference concrete.\n\n1) Genuinely design a grid-world with a narrow 'safe' path next to a 'cliff' region (stepping into the cliff gives a large negative reward and ends the episode) -- the shortest path to the goal runs directly along the cliff edge, while a longer, safer path avoids it.\n\n2) Genuinely train BOTH SARSA and Q-Learning on this cliff-walking grid-world using the SAME epsilon-greedy exploration schedule for both.\n\n3) Genuinely extract and compare the two algorithms' final learned policies -- report whether Q-Learning learns the riskier, optimal (shortest, cliff-adjacent) path while SARSA learns a safer, longer path that keeps more distance from the cliff (the classic reported result), and genuinely measure ACTUAL performance during training (not just the final policy) by tracking each algorithm's average per-episode return WHILE STILL EXPLORING (with epsilon-greedy behavior active) -- report whether SARSA's actual training-time performance is measurably better than Q-Learning's (since Q-Learning's optimal-but-risky learned policy, combined with ongoing epsilon-greedy exploration, may cause it to fall off the cliff more often DURING training even if its final target policy is technically superior) -- report your genuine measured comparison on both dimensions.",
      problemKn: "ನಿಮ್ಮ grid-world ಮೇಲೆ ಮೊದಲಿನಿಂದ Q-Learning ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ, ಮತ್ತು SARSA-vs-Q-Learning behavioral ವ್ಯತ್ಯಾಸವನ್ನೂ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟಗೊಳಿಸಲು ಶಾಸ್ತ್ರೀಯ 'cliff-walking' style ಸನ್ನಿವೇಶವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ರಚಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಒಂದು 'cliff' region ಪಕ್ಕದಲ್ಲಿ ಒಂದು ಕಿರಿದಾದ 'safe' path ಇರುವ ಒಂದು grid-world ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಅದೇ epsilon-greedy exploration schedule ಬಳಸಿ ಈ cliff-walking grid-world ಮೇಲೆ BOTH SARSA ಮತ್ತು Q-Learning ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ಎರಡು algorithms ya ಫಲಿತಾಂಶ ಕಲಿತ policies ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಹೊರತೆಗೆದು ಹೋಲಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Testing Whether Reducing Exploration Closes the SARSA-Q-Learning Gap',
      titleKn: 'Exploration ಕಡಿಮೆ ಮಾಡುವುದೂ SARSA-Q-Learning ಅಂತರವನ್ನೂ ಮುಚ್ಚುತ್ತದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "If SARSA and Q-Learning differ specifically because of how they account for exploration in their updates, then genuinely REDUCING exploration (smaller epsilon) should shrink the behavioral gap between them, since with less exploration, the actually-taken next action becomes more likely to match the greedy/optimal action anyway. Genuinely test this prediction.\n\n1) Genuinely retrain both SARSA and Q-Learning on your cliff-walking grid-world from the previous exercise, but at a substantially SMALLER epsilon (e.g. 0.01 instead of your original 0.1 or 0.3) for both algorithms.\n\n2) Genuinely compare the two algorithms' final learned policies at this smaller epsilon -- report whether SARSA's learned policy is now CLOSER to Q-Learning's optimal, cliff-adjacent path than it was at the larger epsilon from the previous exercise (since with less exploration, SARSA's 'accounting for actual exploratory behavior' matters less, since that behavior itself now rarely deviates from greedy).\n\n3) Genuinely measure and report the actual size of the policy difference (e.g. using your path-distance-from-cliff measure, or overall value difference) between SARSA and Q-Learning at BOTH your original epsilon and this smaller epsilon -- report whether the gap genuinely shrinks as predicted, providing concrete empirical confirmation (or a genuine, honestly-reported disconfirmation) of the theoretical mechanism connecting exploration rate to the on-policy/off-policy behavioral difference, and discuss what this reveals about epsilon as a hyperparameter that affects not just learning speed (as investigated in an earlier lesson) but also which of several qualitatively different final policies an on-policy algorithm like SARSA will converge to.",
      problemKn: "SARSA ಮತ್ತು Q-Learning ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟವಾಗಿ ಅವು exploration ಅನ್ನೂ ಹೇಗೆ ಪರಿಗಣಿಸುತ್ತವೆ ಎಂಬುದರಿಂದ ಭಿನ್ನವಾಗಿದ್ದರೆ, exploration ಕಡಿಮೆ ಮಾಡುವುದೂ ಅವುಗಳ ನಡುವಿನ ಅಂತರವನ್ನೂ ಕುಗ್ಗಿಸಬೇಕು. ಈ ಭವಿಷ್ಯವಾಣಿಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಹಿಂದಿನ exercise ya ನಿಮ್ಮ cliff-walking grid-world ಮೇಲೆ SARSA ಮತ್ತು Q-Learning ಎರಡನ್ನೂ ಒಂದು ಗಣನೀಯವಾಗಿ ಚಿಕ್ಕ epsilon ನಲ್ಲಿ ಮರುtrain ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) ಈ ಚಿಕ್ಕ epsilon ನಲ್ಲಿ ಎರಡು algorithms ya ಫಲಿತಾಂಶ ಕಲಿತ policies ಅನ್ನೂ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) SARSA ಮತ್ತು Q-Learning ನಡುವಿನ ನಿಜ policy ವ್ಯತ್ಯಾಸವನ್ನೂ ಎರಡೂ epsilon ಮೌಲ್ಯಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆದು ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m175, l175_3, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'Expected SARSA: A Middle Ground Between SARSA and Q-Learning',
      titleKn: 'Expected SARSA: SARSA ಮತ್ತು Q-Learning ನಡುವಿನ ಒಂದು Middle Ground',
      problem: "Expected SARSA modifies the update target once more: instead of using the value of the actually-taken next action (SARSA) or the maximum over next actions (Q-Learning), it uses the EXPECTED value of the next state under the current policy -- a weighted average of Q(s',a') across all possible next actions a', weighted by their probability under the current epsilon-greedy policy.\n\n1) Explain why Expected SARSA can be understood as sitting conceptually 'between' SARSA and Q-Learning -- explain what happens to Expected SARSA's update formula in the limiting case where epsilon approaches 0 (making the epsilon-greedy policy nearly deterministic/greedy) -- does Expected SARSA's expectation-over-actions target become equivalent to Q-Learning's max-over-actions target in this limit, and why does this make sense given that a nearly-deterministic greedy policy puts almost all its probability mass on the single best action?\n\n2) Explain a genuine advantage Expected SARSA has over regular SARSA: since Expected SARSA's update target doesn't depend on which SPECIFIC next action happened to be sampled (it uses the full expectation instead), why does this reduce the VARIANCE of the update compared to SARSA's target (which depends on one specific, randomly-sampled next action each time) -- and why might this variance reduction lead to more stable, less noisy learning, similar in spirit to why Weighted Importance Sampling reduced variance compared to Ordinary Importance Sampling in an earlier module's exercise.",
      problemKn: "Expected SARSA update target ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಮ್ಮೆ ಹೆಚ್ಚು ಮಾರ್ಪಡಿಸುತ್ತದೆ: actually-taken next action (SARSA) ಅಥವಾ next actions ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ maximum (Q-Learning) ಬಳಸುವ ಬದಲಿಗೆ, ಇದೂ ಪ್ರಸ್ತುತ policy ಅಡಿಯಲ್ಲಿ next state ya EXPECTED value ಬಳಸುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) Expected SARSA ಪರಿಕಲ್ಪನಾತ್ಮಕವಾಗಿ SARSA ಮತ್ತು Q-Learning ನಡುವೆ ಇದೆ ಎಂದು ಏಕೆ ಅರ್ಥಮಾಡಿಕೊಳ್ಳಬಹುದು ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಸಾಮಾನ್ಯ SARSA ಗಿಂತ Expected SARSA ya ಒಂದು ನಿಜ ಅನುಕೂಲ ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing Expected SARSA and Verifying Its Variance Reduction',
      titleKn: 'Expected SARSA ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಅದರ Variance Reduction ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Implement Expected SARSA from scratch on your grid-world MDP.\n\n1) Genuinely implement the expectation-over-actions update target and train Expected SARSA using the same epsilon-greedy behavior policy and hyperparameters as your SARSA and Q-Learning implementations from earlier lessons.\n\n2) Genuinely verify the limiting-case claim from the previous exercise directly: run Expected SARSA and Q-Learning at a VERY SMALL epsilon (e.g. 0.001) and compare their learned Q-values -- report whether they become nearly identical at this small epsilon, confirming the theoretical convergence you reasoned about conceptually.\n\n3) Genuinely measure the variance-reduction claim: run SARSA and Expected SARSA multiple independent times (different random seeds) at a MODERATE epsilon (e.g. 0.2, where the theoretical variance-reduction benefit should be more visible than at very small epsilon) and measure how much each algorithm's learning curve (episode return over training) VARIES across independent runs -- report whether Expected SARSA shows measurably lower run-to-run variance than regular SARSA, providing concrete empirical evidence for the variance-reduction claim discussed conceptually in the previous exercise.",
      problemKn: "ನಿಮ್ಮ grid-world MDP ಮೇಲೆ ಮೊದಲಿನಿಂದ Expected SARSA ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) expectation-over-actions update target ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠಗಳ ನಿಮ್ಮ SARSA ಮತ್ತು Q-Learning implementations ya ಅದೇ hyperparameters ಬಳಸಿ Expected SARSA ಅನ್ನೂ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) ಹಿಂದಿನ exercise ya limiting-case claim ಅನ್ನೂ ನೇರವಾಗಿ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) variance-reduction claim ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Comparing All Three TD Control Methods Across Multiple Environments',
      titleKn: 'ಅನೇಕ Environments ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ ಎಲ್ಲಾ ಮೂರು TD Control Methods ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಹೋಲಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely conduct a comprehensive three-way comparison of SARSA, Q-Learning, and Expected SARSA across multiple environment configurations, synthesizing everything from this module.\n\n1) Genuinely construct at least 2 different grid-world configurations: your original cliff-walking scenario, and a SECOND, genuinely different scenario (e.g. a grid with no risky cliff-like region at all, just a simple goal-seeking task) -- ensure the two scenarios genuinely differ in whether risk/exploration-danger is a meaningful factor.\n\n2) Genuinely train all THREE algorithms (SARSA, Q-Learning, Expected SARSA) on BOTH environments, using matched hyperparameters (epsilon, alpha, gamma) across all runs.\n\n3) Genuinely report a full comparison table: final policy quality, training-time (exploration-inclusive) average return, and learning-curve variance across seeds, for all 3 algorithms x 2 environments (6 combinations) -- discuss your genuine findings: does the SARSA-versus-Q-Learning behavioral difference (cautious versus risk-taking) only show up meaningfully in the risky cliff environment and disappear in the non-risky environment (as theory would predict, since there's no genuine risk to be cautious about in the second environment)? Does Expected SARSA consistently show the lowest learning-curve variance across both environments, as its variance-reduction property would predict? Ground your conclusions in your actual measured numbers from this specific multi-environment comparison, providing a rigorous, evidence-based synthesis of this module's three core TD control algorithms rather than relying on generic textbook characterizations of their differences.",
      problemKn: "ಈ module ya ಎಲ್ಲವನ್ನೂ ಸಂಶ್ಲೇಷಿಸುತ್ತಾ, ಅನೇಕ environment configurations ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ SARSA, Q-Learning, ಮತ್ತು Expected SARSA ya ಒಂದು ಸಮಗ್ರ three-way ಹೋಲಿಕೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಡೆಸಿ.\n\n1) ಕನಿಷ್ಠ 2 ಭಿನ್ನ grid-world configurations ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ರಚಿಸಿ: ನಿಮ್ಮ ಮೂಲ cliff-walking ಸನ್ನಿವೇಶ, ಮತ್ತು ಯಾವುದೇ ಅಪಾಯಕಾರಿ region ಇಲ್ಲದ ಒಂದು ಎರಡನೇ ಸನ್ನಿವೇಶ.\n\n2) ಎಲ್ಲಾ ಮೂರು algorithms ಅನ್ನೂ BOTH environments ಮೇಲೆ, ಹೊಂದಿಸಿದ hyperparameters ಬಳಸಿ, ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ಒಂದು ಪೂರ್ಣ ಹೋಲಿಕೆ table ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ನಿಮ್ಮ ನಿಜ ಶೋಧನೆಗಳನ್ನೂ ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),

  // ── Module 176: Deep Q-Networks (DQN) ──
  ...set(m176, l176_1, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'From Q-Tables to Q-Networks: Why Tabular Methods Don\'t Scale',
      titleKn: 'Q-Tables ಇಂದ Q-Networks ಗೆ: Tabular Methods ಏಕೆ Scale ಆಗುವುದಿಲ್ಲ',
      problem: "Tabular Q-Learning (from the previous module) stores a separate Q-value for every individual (state, action) pair in an explicit table. Deep Q-Networks (DQN) instead use a neural network to APPROXIMATE Q(s,a), taking the state as input and outputting Q-values for all actions.\n\n1) Explain why tabular methods become genuinely infeasible for environments with very large or continuous state spaces (e.g. raw pixel images as states, connecting to the exponential state-space-blowup concern from an earlier module in this phase) -- why can't you simply build a table with one row per possible state when the number of distinct possible states is astronomically large or literally infinite (as with continuous-valued state variables)?\n\n2) Explain why a neural network function approximator addresses this by GENERALIZING across similar states -- rather than needing to have visited a specific exact state before to have any Q-value estimate for it, why does a trained network's learned weights let it produce a REASONABLE Q-value estimate even for states it has never seen exactly before, as long as they're similar to states it HAS seen (in the same way a trained image classifier can reasonably classify a genuinely novel image it never saw during training, connecting to the general theme of neural network generalization from earlier in this curriculum).",
      problemKn: "ಹಿಂದಿನ module ya Tabular Q-Learning ಪ್ರತಿ ವೈಯಕ್ತಿಕ (state, action) pair ಗೆ ಒಂದು ಪ್ರತ್ಯೇಕ Q-value ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಂದು ಸ್ಪಷ್ಟ table ನಲ್ಲಿ ಸಂಗ್ರಹಿಸುತ್ತದೆ. Deep Q-Networks (DQN) ಬದಲಿಗೆ Q(s,a) ಅಂದಾಜು ಮಾಡಲು ಒಂದು neural network ಬಳಸುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) ಬಹಳ ದೊಡ್ಡ ಅಥವಾ continuous state spaces ಇರುವ environments ಗಳಿಗೆ tabular methods ಏಕೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಪ್ರಾಯೋಗಿಕವಾಗುತ್ತವೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಒಂದು neural network function approximator ಒಂದೇ ಹೋಲುವ states ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ GENERALIZING ಮಾಡುವ ಮೂಲಕ ಇದನ್ನೂ ಹೇಗೆ ಪರಿಹರಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing a Minimal DQN and Verifying It Matches Tabular Q-Learning',
      titleKn: 'ಒಂದು ಕನಿಷ್ಠ DQN ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಇದೂ Tabular Q-Learning ಹೊಂದಿಕೆಯಾಗುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Implement a minimal DQN from scratch in PyTorch: a small neural network (e.g. 2 hidden layers) taking a state representation as input and outputting a Q-value for each possible action, trained with a Q-Learning-style TD loss (MSE between predicted Q(s,a) and the target r + gamma*max_a' Q(s',a')).\n\n1) Genuinely apply your DQN to your SAME small grid-world MDP from earlier modules in this phase (representing the state as, e.g. a one-hot vector over grid cells, so your network's input is simple and interpretable) -- train it using the same basic Q-Learning update loop from an earlier module, just with a neural network instead of a table computing/storing the Q-values.\n\n2) Genuinely verify your DQN converges to a policy comparable to your tabular Q-Learning's result from an earlier module on the SAME small grid-world -- report whether the final learned Q-values and policy are close to your earlier tabular results (they should be, roughly, since a neural network with enough capacity applied to a small, simple state representation should be ABLE to represent the same function a table could).\n\n3) Genuinely report any training instabilities you observe with this naive DQN implementation (e.g. does the loss curve oscillate or diverge at some hyperparameter settings, even on this small problem) -- note any such instability, since this motivates the specific stabilization techniques (target networks, experience replay) covered in the next two lessons.",
      problemKn: "PyTorch ನಲ್ಲಿ ಮೊದಲಿನಿಂದ ಒಂದು ಕನಿಷ್ಠ DQN ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಇದೇ phase ya ಹಿಂದಿನ modules ya ನಿಮ್ಮ ಅದೇ ಸಣ್ಣ grid-world MDP ಗೆ ನಿಮ್ಮ DQN ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅನ್ವಯಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ನಿಮ್ಮ DQN ಒಂದೇ ಸಣ್ಣ grid-world ಮೇಲೆ ಹಿಂದಿನ module ya ನಿಮ್ಮ tabular Q-Learning ya ಫಲಿತಾಂಶಕ್ಕೆ ಹೋಲಿಸಬಹುದಾದ ಒಂದು policy ಗೆ ಒಗ್ಗಿಕೊಳ್ಳುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಈ naive DQN implementation ಜೊತೆ ನೀವು ಗಮನಿಸುವ ಯಾವುದೇ training ಅಸ್ಥಿರತೆಗಳನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Diagnosing DQN\'s Core Instability: Correlated Data and a Moving Target',
      titleKn: 'DQN ya ಪ್ರಮುಖ Instability ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ Diagnose ಮಾಡುವುದೂ: Correlated Data ಮತ್ತು ಒಂದು Moving Target',
      problem: "Naive DQN (as implemented in the previous exercise) suffers from two compounding instability sources: (1) training on HIGHLY CORRELATED consecutive experiences (since consecutive states in a single trajectory are similar to each other, violating the i.i.d. data assumption most neural network training relies on), and (2) a MOVING TARGET (since the same network is used to both predict Q(s,a) and compute the target r + gamma*max Q(s',a'), every gradient update that changes the network also changes the target for the NEXT update, creating a feedback loop distinct from standard supervised learning's fixed targets). Genuinely diagnose both issues empirically.\n\n1) Genuinely measure and report the correlation between consecutive TRAINING BATCHES' average state representations when training your naive DQN with simple sequential (non-shuffled) experience -- report whether consecutive batches are indeed highly similar/correlated, as the concern predicts.\n\n2) Genuinely measure the 'moving target' issue directly: track the TD target value for a FIXED, specific (s,a,r,s') tuple across many training steps (recomputing the target using the network's CURRENT weights at each step, even though the tuple itself doesn't change) -- report whether this target value visibly drifts/changes over training, confirming the target is indeed 'moving' due to it being computed from the same network being updated.\n\n3) Genuinely report your measured evidence for both instability sources, and discuss conceptually (without yet implementing the fixes, which are covered in the next two lessons) why decorrelating training data (e.g. via experience replay) and stabilizing the target (e.g. via a separate, periodically-updated target network) are the two natural fixes these specific diagnosed problems call for.",
      problemKn: "Naive DQN ಎರಡು compounding instability ಮೂಲಗಳಿಂದ ಬಳಲುತ್ತದೆ: (1) ಬಹಳ CORRELATED consecutive experiences ಮೇಲೆ training, ಮತ್ತು (2) ಒಂದು MOVING TARGET. ಎರಡು ಸಮಸ್ಯೆಗಳನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪ್ರಾಯೋಗಿಕವಾಗಿ diagnose ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n1) ಸರಳ sequential experience ಜೊತೆ ನಿಮ್ಮ naive DQN train ಮಾಡುವಾಗ consecutive TRAINING BATCHES ya average state representations ನಡುವಿನ correlation ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆದು ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) 'moving target' ಸಮಸ್ಯೆಯನ್ನೂ ನೇರವಾಗಿ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n3) ಎರಡೂ instability ಮೂಲಗಳಿಗೆ ನಿಮ್ಮ ಅಳೆದ ಸಾಕ್ಷ್ಯ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಪರಿಕಲ್ಪನಾತ್ಮಕವಾಗಿ ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m176, l176_2, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'Experience Replay: Breaking the Correlation',
      titleKn: 'Experience Replay: Correlation ಮುರಿಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Experience Replay fixes the correlated-data problem identified in the previous lesson by storing past (state, action, reward, next_state) transitions in a large buffer, and training the network on RANDOMLY SAMPLED MINIBATCHES drawn from this buffer (mixing experiences from many different points in time and many different trajectories) rather than training directly on the most recent, sequentially-correlated experience.\n\n1) Explain why sampling randomly from a large buffer of past experiences produces training batches that are much less correlated than training directly on consecutive steps of the current trajectory -- since the buffer contains experiences from many different times and states, why does random sampling from it approximate the i.i.d.-data assumption much better than sequential training does?\n\n2) Explain a SECOND, distinct benefit of experience replay beyond decorrelation: since each stored experience can be reused for MULTIPLE training updates (rather than being used once and discarded, as in a purely online algorithm), why does this improve SAMPLE EFFICIENCY -- getting more learning signal out of each real environment interaction, which matters especially for environments where gathering new experience (e.g. real robot interactions) is slow or expensive.",
      problemKn: "Experience Replay ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದಲ್ಲಿ ಗುರುತಿಸಿದ correlated-data ಸಮಸ್ಯೆಯನ್ನೂ ಹಿಂದಿನ (state, action, reward, next_state) transitions ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಂದು ದೊಡ್ಡ buffer ನಲ್ಲಿ ಸಂಗ್ರಹಿಸುವ ಮೂಲಕ ಮತ್ತು network ಅನ್ನೂ ಈ buffer ಇಂದ RANDOMLY SAMPLED MINIBATCHES ಮೇಲೆ train ಮಾಡುವ ಮೂಲಕ ಸರಿಪಡಿಸುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) ಒಂದು ದೊಡ್ಡ buffer ಇಂದ ಯಾದೃಚ್ಛಿಕವಾಗಿ sampling ಮಾಡುವುದೂ ಸತತ ಹಂತಗಳ ಮೇಲೆ ನೇರವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡುವುದಕ್ಕಿಂತ ಏಕೆ ಕಡಿಮೆ correlated training batches ಉತ್ಪಾದಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) decorrelation ಮೀರಿದ experience replay ya ಎರಡನೇ, ಭಿನ್ನ ಅನುಕೂಲ ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing Experience Replay and Measuring Its Stabilizing Effect',
      titleKn: 'Experience Replay ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಅದರ ಸ್ಥಿರೀಕರಣ ಪರಿಣಾಮ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Implement a replay buffer from scratch: a fixed-capacity data structure storing (s,a,r,s',done) tuples, supporting adding new experiences (overwriting the oldest when full) and sampling random minibatches.\n\n1) Genuinely integrate this replay buffer into your DQN from the previous lesson: collect experience by interacting with your grid-world (storing each transition in the buffer), and train on randomly-sampled minibatches from the buffer rather than on the immediately-preceding transition.\n\n2) Genuinely retrain your DQN with experience replay on the SAME grid-world and compare its training loss curve against your naive (no-replay) DQN's loss curve from the previous lesson -- report whether the replay-buffer version shows a visibly SMOOTHER, less erratic loss curve, providing direct empirical evidence for the stabilization benefit.\n\n3) Genuinely measure the effect of REPLAY BUFFER SIZE: train your DQN with several different buffer capacities (e.g. a very small buffer holding only the last 10 transitions, versus a much larger buffer holding thousands) -- report whether a too-small buffer fails to provide meaningful decorrelation benefit (since it still mostly contains recent, correlated experience) while a sufficiently large buffer shows the stabilization benefit clearly, reporting your actual measured comparison across buffer sizes.",
      problemKn: "ಮೊದಲಿನಿಂದ ಒಂದು replay buffer ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ: (s,a,r,s',done) tuples ಸಂಗ್ರಹಿಸುವ ಒಂದು fixed-capacity data structure.\n\n1) ಈ replay buffer ಅನ್ನೂ ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ ನಿಮ್ಮ DQN ಗೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಈ replay ಜೊತೆ ನಿಮ್ಮ DQN ಅನ್ನೂ ಅದೇ grid-world ಮೇಲೆ ಮರುtrain ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಅದರ training loss curve ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ naive DQN ya loss curve ಗೆ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) REPLAY BUFFER SIZE ya ಪರಿಣಾಮವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Measuring Sample Efficiency Gains from Experience Reuse',
      titleKn: 'Experience Reuse ಇಂದ Sample Efficiency ಲಾಭಗಳನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely test the second claimed benefit from the first exercise -- that replaying past experience improves sample efficiency (learning more from the same number of real environment interactions).\n\n1) Genuinely measure and compare, for both your naive (no-replay) DQN and your experience-replay DQN, the number of REAL ENVIRONMENT STEPS (not training gradient updates, but actual interactions with the grid-world) needed to reach a fixed target performance level (e.g. average episode return above some threshold) -- report the actual measured environment-step counts for both.\n\n2) Genuinely report whether your experience-replay DQN reaches the target performance level using FEWER real environment steps than your naive DQN, confirming the sample-efficiency benefit -- since experience replay reuses each stored transition for multiple gradient updates, report the actual measured ratio of gradient updates to environment steps you configured for your replay version, and discuss how this ratio (sometimes called the 'replay ratio') is itself a tunable hyperparameter with its own tradeoffs.\n\n3) Genuinely test what happens with a VERY aggressive replay ratio (reusing each experience for many more updates than your original setting) -- report whether this further improves sample efficiency, or whether excessive reuse of the SAME limited set of experiences eventually causes overfitting to that specific experience/stops providing additional benefit, discussing the diminishing-returns pattern you observe.",
      problemKn: "ಮೊದಲ exercise ya ಎರಡನೇ claimed ಅನುಕೂಲವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸಿ -- ಹಿಂದಿನ experience replay ಮಾಡುವುದೂ sample efficiency ಸುಧಾರಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂಬುದೂ.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ naive DQN ಮತ್ತು experience-replay DQN ಎರಡಕ್ಕೂ, ಒಂದು ಸ್ಥಿರ target performance level ತಲುಪಲು ಬೇಕಾದ REAL ENVIRONMENT STEPS ya ಸಂಖ್ಯೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆದು ಹೋಲಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ನಿಮ್ಮ experience-replay DQN ಕಡಿಮೆ real environment steps ಬಳಸಿ target performance level ತಲುಪುತ್ತದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ಒಂದು ಬಹಳ ಆಕ್ರಮಣಕಾರಿ replay ratio ಜೊತೆ ಏನೂ ಸಂಭವಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m176, l176_3, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'Target Networks: Freezing the Moving Target',
      titleKn: 'Target Networks: Moving Target ಅನ್ನೂ Freeze ಮಾಡುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Target networks fix the 'moving target' problem identified in an earlier lesson by maintaining a SEPARATE, periodically-updated copy of the Q-network specifically for computing TD targets -- the main network is updated every step via gradient descent, but the target network's weights are only copied over from the main network occasionally (e.g. every N steps), staying FIXED in between.\n\n1) Explain why using this separate, slowly-updated target network for computing r + gamma*max_a' Q_target(s',a') (rather than using the main, constantly-changing network for both prediction AND target computation) removes the feedback-loop instability discussed in an earlier lesson -- since the target network's weights stay fixed for many consecutive updates, why does this make the TD target behave more like a genuine, stable supervised-learning target (similar to how a fixed ground-truth label doesn't change during ordinary supervised training) for those N steps, even though it will eventually be updated to reflect the main network's improved knowledge?\n\n2) Explain the tradeoff in choosing the target-network update frequency N -- explain why updating the target network TOO frequently (small N) would reintroduce much of the original moving-target instability (since the target barely differs from the constantly-changing main network), while updating it TOO infrequently (very large N) would mean the target network reflects an increasingly OUTDATED version of the network's knowledge, potentially slowing down learning by providing targets based on a much worse, stale set of Q-value estimates.",
      problemKn: "Target networks ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದಲ್ಲಿ ಗುರುತಿಸಿದ 'moving target' ಸಮಸ್ಯೆಯನ್ನೂ TD targets ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಲು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟವಾಗಿ Q-network ya ಒಂದು ಪ್ರತ್ಯೇಕ, ಆವರ್ತಕವಾಗಿ-updated ಪ್ರತಿಯನ್ನೂ ನಿರ್ವಹಿಸುವ ಮೂಲಕ ಸರಿಪಡಿಸುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) ಈ ಪ್ರತ್ಯೇಕ, ನಿಧಾನವಾಗಿ-updated target network ಬಳಸುವುದೂ ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದಲ್ಲಿ ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿದ feedback-loop instability ಅನ್ನೂ ಏಕೆ ತೆಗೆದುಹಾಕುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) target-network update frequency N ಆಯ್ಕೆಮಾಡುವುದರಲ್ಲಿ tradeoff ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing a Target Network and Measuring Its Additional Stabilization',
      titleKn: 'ಒಂದು Target Network ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಅದರ ಹೆಚ್ಚುವರಿ ಸ್ಥಿರೀಕರಣ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Implement a target network from scratch: maintain two copies of your Q-network's weights (a 'main' network updated every step, and a 'target' network used only for computing TD targets), and periodically copy the main network's weights into the target network (e.g. every 100 steps).\n\n1) Genuinely integrate this target network into your experience-replay DQN from the previous lesson, so you now have BOTH stabilization techniques (replay AND target network) combined.\n\n2) Genuinely compare training loss curves across THREE versions: your original naive DQN (neither fix), your replay-only DQN from the previous lesson, and your replay-plus-target-network DQN (both fixes) -- report whether adding the target network on top of experience replay produces a further, additional smoothing/stabilization of the loss curve beyond what replay alone achieved.\n\n3) Genuinely measure final policy quality for all three versions (comparing against the true optimal policy from dynamic programming in an earlier module) -- report whether the combination of both stabilization techniques produces the best final policy quality among the three, or whether one technique alone was already sufficient on your specific small grid-world, reporting your genuine measured comparison rather than assuming both techniques must always be necessary.",
      problemKn: "ಮೊದಲಿನಿಂದ ಒಂದು target network ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ: ನಿಮ್ಮ Q-network ya weights ya ಎರಡು ಪ್ರತಿಗಳನ್ನೂ ನಿರ್ವಹಿಸಿ, ಮತ್ತು ಆವರ್ತಕವಾಗಿ main network ya weights ಅನ್ನೂ target network ಗೆ ಕಾಪಿ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n1) ಈ target network ಅನ್ನೂ ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ ನಿಮ್ಮ experience-replay DQN ಗೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಮೂರು ಆವೃತ್ತಿಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ training loss curves ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಎಲ್ಲಾ ಮೂರು ಆವೃತ್ತಿಗಳಿಗೂ ಅಂತಿಮ policy quality ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Sweeping Target Network Update Frequency to Find the Sweet Spot',
      titleKn: 'Sweet Spot ಕಂಡುಹಿಡಿಯಲು Target Network Update Frequency ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ Sweep ಮಾಡುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely test the update-frequency tradeoff discussed conceptually in the first exercise of this lesson.\n\n1) Genuinely train your full DQN (replay + target network) at several different target-network update frequencies N (e.g. N=1 which is equivalent to no target network at all, N=10, N=100, N=1000) on your grid-world, keeping all other hyperparameters fixed.\n\n2) Genuinely measure and report training loss stability (e.g. variance of the loss over a training window) and learning speed (episodes/steps needed to reach a target performance level) for each N -- report whether very small N shows the instability of no-target-network (as predicted, since N=1 makes the target network update every step, nearly identical to using the main network directly), while very large N shows slower learning (as predicted, since the target lags too far behind).\n\n3) Genuinely identify the N value that achieves the best overall tradeoff (stable training AND reasonably fast learning) on your specific setup, and report your actual measured results across the full sweep, providing a genuine, evidence-based answer to what update frequency works best here rather than simply citing a commonly-used default value from the literature without verifying it holds for your specific problem.",
      problemKn: "ಈ ಪಾಠದ ಮೊದಲ exercise ya ಪರಿಕಲ್ಪನಾತ್ಮಕವಾಗಿ ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿದ update-frequency tradeoff ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಹಲವಾರು ಭಿನ್ನ target-network update frequencies N ನಲ್ಲಿ ನಿಮ್ಮ ಪೂರ್ಣ DQN ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ grid-world ಮೇಲೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) ಪ್ರತಿ N ಗೆ training loss ಸ್ಥಿರತೆ ಮತ್ತು learning speed ಎರಡನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆದು ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ setup ಮೇಲೆ ಅತ್ಯುತ್ತಮ ಒಟ್ಟಾರೆ tradeoff ಸಾಧಿಸುವ N ಮೌಲ್ಯವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಗುರುತಿಸಿ." },
  ]),

  // ── Module 177: Policy Gradients: REINFORCE ──
  ...set(m177, l177_1, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'Learning a Policy Directly versus Deriving It from Q-Values',
      titleKn: 'Q-Values ಇಂದ ಪಡೆಯುವ ಬದಲಿಗೆ ನೇರವಾಗಿ ಒಂದು Policy ಕಲಿಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "All methods covered so far in this phase (Q-Learning, SARSA, DQN) learn a VALUE function (Q or V) and derive a policy indirectly by acting greedily with respect to it. Policy Gradient methods instead learn a PARAMETERIZED POLICY directly -- a function that outputs action probabilities given a state, trained to directly maximize expected return, with no value function required at all (in this basic REINFORCE form).\n\n1) Explain a genuine limitation of value-based methods that policy gradient methods can address: value-based methods with a discrete max-over-actions step (like Q-Learning/DQN) don't naturally extend to CONTINUOUS action spaces (e.g. a robot's joint torques, which can take any real value in a range) -- why does computing 'the action that maximizes Q(s,a)' become genuinely difficult when there are infinitely many possible continuous actions to search over, whereas a policy network can directly OUTPUT a continuous action (or the parameters of a continuous distribution to sample from) without needing this max-search step at all?\n\n2) Explain a second genuine advantage: policy gradient methods can naturally learn STOCHASTIC policies (explicit probability distributions over actions), which can be provably necessary for optimal behavior in certain environments (e.g. games with hidden information or adversarial elements, where a deterministic policy could be exploited by an opponent who learns to predict it) -- why does a value-based method's typical greedy (deterministic, aside from exploration noise) policy structure make it awkward to represent genuinely optimal stochastic behavior in such cases, while policy gradient methods handle this naturally.",
      problemKn: "ಈ phase ನಲ್ಲಿ ಇಲ್ಲಿಯವರೆಗೆ ಕವರ್ ಮಾಡಿದ ಎಲ್ಲಾ ವಿಧಾನಗಳು (Q-Learning, SARSA, DQN) ಒಂದು VALUE function ಕಲಿಯುತ್ತವೆ ಮತ್ತು ಅದಕ್ಕೆ ಸಂಬಂಧಿಸಿ greedy ಆಗಿ ವರ್ತಿಸುವ ಮೂಲಕ ಪರೋಕ್ಷವಾಗಿ ಒಂದು policy ಪಡೆಯುತ್ತವೆ. Policy Gradient ವಿಧಾನಗಳು ಬದಲಿಗೆ ಒಂದು PARAMETERIZED POLICY ಅನ್ನೂ ನೇರವಾಗಿ ಕಲಿಯುತ್ತವೆ.\n\n1) value-based methods ya ಒಂದು ನಿಜ ಮಿತಿಯನ್ನೂ ವಿವರಿಸಿ, ಇದನ್ನೂ policy gradient methods ಪರಿಹರಿಸಬಹುದು: continuous action spaces ಗಳಿಗೆ.\n\n2) ಒಂದು ಎರಡನೇ ನಿಜ ಅನುಕೂಲ ವಿವರಿಸಿ: STOCHASTIC policies ಕಲಿಯುವುದೂ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing REINFORCE from Scratch',
      titleKn: 'REINFORCE ಅನ್ನೂ ಮೊದಲಿನಿಂದ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Implement the REINFORCE algorithm from scratch: a small neural network taking a state as input and outputting a probability distribution over actions (e.g. via softmax), trained using the policy gradient theorem's update rule -- for each episode, compute the discounted return following each time step, and update the policy network's parameters in the direction that increases the log-probability of the actions actually taken, weighted by their respective returns (actions in episodes/timesteps with higher return get pushed to become more probable; lower-return ones get pushed to become less probable).\n\n1) Genuinely implement this full episode-based training loop (REINFORCE is fundamentally a Monte Carlo method, requiring complete episodes before updating, connecting back to the Monte Carlo methods module earlier in this phase) on your grid-world MDP.\n\n2) Genuinely train it and track episode return over training -- report your actual measured learning curve.\n\n3) Genuinely compare the final learned STOCHASTIC policy (the actual action probability distribution output by your trained network for a few specific states) against the deterministic optimal policy from dynamic programming in an earlier module -- report whether your REINFORCE policy converges to be nearly deterministic and matching the true optimal action in each state (as you'd expect for a simple grid-world with a genuinely unique best action per state), or whether it retains meaningful randomness, and discuss why.",
      problemKn: "ಮೊದಲಿನಿಂದ REINFORCE algorithm ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ: ಒಂದು state ಅನ್ನೂ input ಆಗಿ ತೆಗೆದುಕೊಂಡು actions ಮೇಲೆ ಒಂದು probability distribution ಔಟ್ಪುಟ್ ಮಾಡುವ ಒಂದು ಸಣ್ಣ neural network, policy gradient theorem ya update rule ಬಳಸಿ trained.\n\n1) ಈ ಪೂರ್ಣ episode-based training loop ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ grid-world MDP ಮೇಲೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಇದನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು episode return ಟ್ರ್ಯಾಕ್ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ಫಲಿತಾಂಶ ಕಲಿತ stochastic policy ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜ optimal policy ಗೆ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Measuring REINFORCE\'s High Variance and Comparing to Your Value-Based Methods',
      titleKn: 'REINFORCE ya High Variance ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆದು ನಿಮ್ಮ Value-Based Methods ಗೆ ಹೋಲಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "REINFORCE is known to suffer from high-variance gradient estimates, since it uses the full, noisy Monte Carlo return (which can vary a lot between episodes due to environment/policy stochasticity) as its learning signal, rather than a bootstrapped, lower-variance TD-style target. Genuinely investigate this.\n\n1) Genuinely train your REINFORCE implementation from the previous exercise MULTIPLE independent times (different random seeds) on the same grid-world, and measure how much the learning curve (episode return over training) VARIES across these independent runs.\n\n2) Genuinely repeat this same multi-seed variance measurement for your DQN implementation from an earlier lesson in this module -- report whether REINFORCE shows measurably HIGHER run-to-run variance in its learning curves than DQN, consistent with the theoretical high-variance concern.\n\n3) Genuinely implement one simple variance-reduction technique for REINFORCE: subtract a BASELINE (e.g. the average return observed so far across episodes, a simple running average) from the return before using it to weight the policy gradient update, rather than using the raw return directly -- report whether this baseline subtraction measurably reduces REINFORCE's run-to-run variance while (ideally) not changing its final converged policy quality, providing a preview of the actor-critic methods covered in a later module of this phase, which formalize this baseline idea by learning a value function specifically to serve as this variance-reducing baseline.",
      problemKn: "REINFORCE high-variance gradient estimates ಇಂದ ಬಳಲುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ತಿಳಿದಿದೆ, ಏಕೆಂದರೆ ಇದೂ ತನ್ನ learning signal ಆಗಿ ಪೂರ್ಣ, ಗದ್ದಲದ Monte Carlo return ಬಳಸುತ್ತದೆ. ಇದನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ತನಿಖೆ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n1) ಅದೇ grid-world ಮೇಲೆ ಹಿಂದಿನ exercise ya ನಿಮ್ಮ REINFORCE implementation ಅನ್ನೂ ಅನೇಕ ಸ್ವತಂತ್ರ ಬಾರಿ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು learning curve ಎಷ್ಟು ಬದಲಾಗುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n2) ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ ನಿಮ್ಮ DQN implementation ಗಾಗಿ ಅದೇ multi-seed variance ಅಳತೆಯನ್ನೂ ಪುನರಾವರ್ತಿಸಿ.\n\n3) REINFORCE ಗಾಗಿ ಒಂದು ಸರಳ variance-reduction technique ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ: return ಇಂದ ಒಂದು BASELINE ಕಳೆಯಿರಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m177, l177_2, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'The Log-Probability Trick: Why Policy Gradients Use Log Instead of Raw Probability',
      titleKn: 'Log-Probability Trick: Policy Gradients Raw Probability ಬದಲಿಗೆ Log ಏಕೆ ಬಳಸುತ್ತವೆ',
      problem: "REINFORCE's update rule involves the GRADIENT of the LOG-probability of the taken action (nabla log pi(a|s)), not the gradient of the raw probability itself.\n\n1) Explain the mathematical derivation intuition (without needing the full formal proof): the policy gradient theorem starts from wanting the gradient of an EXPECTATION (expected return, which is a sum/integral over all possible trajectories weighted by their probability under the policy), and a standard calculus identity (the 'log-derivative trick': nabla p(x) = p(x) * nabla log p(x)) is what allows this otherwise-intractable expectation gradient to be rewritten as an expectation itself (something you CAN estimate via sampling, i.e. via Monte Carlo averaging over actually-observed trajectories) -- why is being able to express the gradient AS an expectation specifically what makes this quantity practically estimable from sampled experience, rather than requiring the intractable exact computation?\n\n2) Explain why using log-probability specifically (rather than just any arbitrary transformation of probability) has the convenient practical property that for a simple softmax-output policy network, the gradient of the log-probability with respect to the network's parameters has a clean, easy-to-compute closed form -- connecting to why softmax's mathematical structure and log-probability's gradient work particularly well together, a pairing you may recognize from cross-entropy loss in standard classification training earlier in this curriculum.",
      problemKn: "REINFORCE ya update rule ತೆಗೆದುಕೊಂಡ action ya LOG-probability ya GRADIENT ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಳಗೊಂಡಿದೆ, raw probability ya gradient ಅಲ್ಲ.\n\n1) ಗಣಿತದ derivation ಅಂತಃಪ್ರಜ್ಞೆಯನ್ನೂ ವಿವರಿಸಿ: policy gradient theorem ಒಂದು EXPECTATION ya gradient ಬಯಸುವುದರಿಂದ ಪ್ರಾರಂಭವಾಗುತ್ತದೆ, ಮತ್ತು 'log-derivative trick' ಈ otherwise-intractable expectation gradient ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಂದು expectation ಆಗಿ ಪುನಃ ಬರೆಯಲು ಹೇಗೆ ಬಿಡುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) log-probability ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟವಾಗಿ ಬಳಸುವುದೂ ಏಕೆ ಒಂದು ಅನುಕೂಲಕರ ಪ್ರಾಯೋಗಿಕ ಗುಣ ಹೊಂದಿದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ, cross-entropy loss ಗೆ ಸಂಪರ್ಕಿಸುತ್ತಾ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Verifying the Log-Probability Gradient by Hand and Against Autodiff',
      titleKn: 'Log-Probability Gradient ಅನ್ನೂ ಕೈಯಾರೆ ಮತ್ತು Autodiff ವಿರುದ್ಧ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely verify the mathematical mechanics underlying your REINFORCE implementation from the previous lesson.\n\n1) For a simple softmax policy (a linear layer followed by softmax, applied to a small toy state representation), genuinely derive BY HAND the gradient of log pi(a|s) with respect to the linear layer's weights, for a specific chosen action a -- show your exact derivation and the resulting closed-form gradient expression.\n\n2) Genuinely implement this hand-derived gradient computation directly in code (without using PyTorch's autograd) for a specific numerical example (concrete weight values, concrete state, concrete chosen action), and compute the actual numerical gradient values from your hand-derived formula.\n\n3) Genuinely compare your hand-computed gradient against PyTorch's autograd-computed gradient for the EXACT same setup (same weights, state, action) -- report whether they match numerically (within floating-point tolerance), providing a rigorous verification that both your mathematical understanding of the log-probability gradient AND your practical REINFORCE implementation from the previous lesson (which relies on autograd to compute this same gradient automatically) are genuinely correct, rather than merely appearing to work due to the algorithm's overall robustness masking a subtle implementation bug.",
      problemKn: "ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ ನಿಮ್ಮ REINFORCE implementation ಆಧಾರಿತ ಗಣಿತದ ಯಾಂತ್ರಿಕತೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಒಂದು ಸರಳ softmax policy ಗಾಗಿ, ಒಂದು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ ಆಯ್ಕೆಮಾಡಿದ action a ಗಾಗಿ, linear layer ya weights ಗೆ ಸಂಬಂಧಿಸಿ log pi(a|s) ya gradient ಅನ್ನೂ ಕೈಯಾರೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪಡೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n2) ಈ ಕೈಯಾರೆ-ಪಡೆದ gradient ಲೆಕ್ಕಾಚಾರವನ್ನೂ ನೇರವಾಗಿ code ನಲ್ಲಿ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಕೈಯಾರೆ-ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಿದ gradient ಅನ್ನೂ PyTorch ya autograd-computed gradient ಗೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Testing How Gradient Estimation Noise Depends on Batch Size (Episodes per Update)',
      titleKn: 'Gradient Estimation Noise Batch Size ಮೇಲೆ ಹೇಗೆ ಅವಲಂಬಿತವಾಗಿದೆ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Since the policy gradient is estimated via Monte Carlo averaging over sampled trajectories (as established in the first exercise of this lesson), the number of episodes averaged together per gradient update should genuinely affect the NOISE of that gradient estimate, similar to how averaging over more samples reduces estimation variance in any Monte Carlo context. Genuinely test this.\n\n1) Genuinely modify your REINFORCE implementation to support updating based on a BATCH of multiple episodes at once (averaging the policy gradient contribution across all episodes in the batch) rather than updating after every single episode.\n\n2) Genuinely train your REINFORCE agent with several different batch sizes (e.g. 1 episode per update, 8 episodes per update, 32 episodes per update) on your grid-world, keeping the total number of episodes experienced roughly matched across configurations (so you're comparing 'same total data, different batching' rather than confounding batch size with total training amount).\n\n3) Genuinely measure and compare the SMOOTHNESS of the training loss/return curve across your different batch sizes -- report whether larger batch sizes produce visibly smoother, less noisy learning curves (consistent with the variance-reduction-via-averaging prediction), and also measure whether larger batches slow down WALL-CLOCK convergence (since fewer gradient updates happen per unit of environment interaction with a larger batch) -- report your genuine measured tradeoff between gradient-estimate smoothness and update frequency across your batch-size sweep.",
      problemKn: "policy gradient ಅನ್ನೂ sampled trajectories ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ Monte Carlo ಸರಾಸರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವ ಮೂಲಕ ಅಂದಾಜು ಮಾಡುವುದರಿಂದ, ಪ್ರತಿ gradient update ಗೆ ಒಟ್ಟಿಗೆ ಸರಾಸರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿದ episodes ya ಸಂಖ್ಯೆ ಆ gradient estimate ya NOISE ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಣಾಮ ಬೀರಬೇಕು. ಇದನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ REINFORCE implementation ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಂದು ಬಾರಿಗೆ ಅನೇಕ episodes ya ಒಂದು BATCH ಆಧಾರದ ಮೇಲೆ update ಮಾಡಲು ಬೆಂಬಲಿಸಲು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಮಾರ್ಪಡಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಹಲವಾರು ಭಿನ್ನ batch sizes ಜೊತೆ ನಿಮ್ಮ REINFORCE agent ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ grid-world ಮೇಲೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಭಿನ್ನ batch sizes ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ training loss/return curve ya SMOOTHNESS ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆದು ಹೋಲಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m177, l177_3, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'Continuous Action Spaces: Outputting a Distribution Instead of a Category',
      titleKn: 'Continuous Action Spaces: ಒಂದು Category ಬದಲಿಗೆ ಒಂದು Distribution ಔಟ್ಪುಟ್ ಮಾಡುವುದೂ',
      problem: "For continuous action spaces (e.g. a real-valued steering angle or force magnitude), a policy network cannot output a simple softmax over a finite set of discrete actions (as your grid-world's up/down/left/right policy does) -- instead, it typically outputs the PARAMETERS of a continuous probability distribution (most commonly, the mean and standard deviation of a Gaussian), and actions are sampled from this distribution.\n\n1) Explain why this Gaussian-parameterization approach still fits within the SAME log-probability-gradient framework covered in the previous lesson -- since a Gaussian distribution has a well-defined, differentiable log-probability-density function (just like a discrete softmax distribution has a well-defined log-probability), why does the same REINFORCE update rule (using nabla log pi(a|s), just now computed via the Gaussian's log-density formula rather than a discrete categorical log-probability) apply essentially unchanged to this continuous-action setting?\n\n2) Explain a genuine practical challenge specific to continuous action spaces: the network must learn to output an appropriate STANDARD DEVIATION (controlling how much exploration/randomness the policy has) alongside the mean -- explain why a standard deviation that's too large would make the policy overly random/imprecise (never reliably converging to a good specific action), while one that's too small would make the policy nearly deterministic too early, potentially getting stuck without exploring enough to discover better actions, connecting this exploration-controlling role of the learned standard deviation to the broader exploration-exploitation theme that has recurred throughout this phase.",
      problemKn: "continuous action spaces ಗಳಿಗೆ, ಒಂದು policy network ಒಂದು ಸೀಮಿತ ಸೆಟ್ discrete actions ಮೇಲೆ ಒಂದು ಸರಳ softmax ಔಟ್ಪುಟ್ ಮಾಡಲು ಸಾಧ್ಯವಿಲ್ಲ -- ಬದಲಿಗೆ, ಇದೂ ಸಾಮಾನ್ಯವಾಗಿ ಒಂದು continuous probability distribution ya parameters ಅನ್ನೂ ಔಟ್ಪುಟ್ ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) ಈ Gaussian-parameterization ವಿಧಾನ ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ ಅದೇ log-probability-gradient framework ಒಳಗೆ ಏಕೆ ಹೊಂದಿಕೆಯಾಗುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) continuous action spaces ಗೆ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟವಾಗಿ ಒಂದು ನಿಜ ಪ್ರಾಯೋಗಿಕ ಸವಾಲನ್ನೂ ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing a Gaussian Policy for a Continuous Control Task',
      titleKn: 'ಒಂದು Continuous Control Task ಗಾಗಿ ಒಂದು Gaussian Policy ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely construct a simple continuous-control task and implement a Gaussian-policy REINFORCE agent for it.\n\n1) Genuinely build a small synthetic continuous-control environment (e.g. a 1D 'point mass' that must move to a target position, where the action is a continuous force/velocity value applied at each step, and the state is the current position/velocity) -- define a reasonable reward function (e.g. negative distance to target).\n\n2) Genuinely implement a Gaussian policy network (outputting mean and log-standard-deviation given the state) and train it with REINFORCE (reusing your log-probability-gradient implementation approach from earlier lessons, adapted to use the Gaussian log-density formula instead of categorical log-probability) on this continuous task.\n\n3) Genuinely evaluate the trained policy: run several episodes and report whether the agent successfully learns to move toward and stay near the target position -- genuinely plot the learned mean action as a function of state (e.g. does the policy learn to apply positive force when far below target and negative force when far above, a sensible control strategy?), and report your genuine assessment of whether the learned Gaussian policy's behavior makes physical/intuitive sense given the task.",
      problemKn: "ಒಂದು ಸರಳ continuous-control task ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ರಚಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಇದಕ್ಕಾಗಿ ಒಂದು Gaussian-policy REINFORCE agent ಅನ್ನೂ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಒಂದು ಸಣ್ಣ synthetic continuous-control environment ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಒಂದು Gaussian policy network ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಇದನ್ನೂ REINFORCE ಜೊತೆ ಈ continuous task ಮೇಲೆ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) trained policy ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಮೌಲ್ಯಮಾಪನ ಮಾಡಿ, ಫಲಿತಾಂಶಗಳನ್ನೂ ಪ್ಲಾಟ್ ಮಾಡಿ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Measuring How Initial Standard Deviation Affects Exploration and Convergence',
      titleKn: 'Initial Standard Deviation Exploration ಮತ್ತು Convergence ಮೇಲೆ ಹೇಗೆ ಪರಿಣಾಮ ಬೀರುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely test the standard-deviation-controls-exploration claim from the first exercise of this lesson on your continuous-control task.\n\n1) Genuinely train your Gaussian-policy REINFORCE agent from the previous exercise with THREE different fixed INITIAL standard deviation values (e.g. a very small initial std like 0.01, a moderate one like 0.5, and a very large one like 5.0), keeping the network free to LEARN to adjust the standard deviation during training in all three cases (only the starting point differs).\n\n2) Genuinely track and compare the learning curves (episode return over training) for all three initial-std settings -- report whether the very-small-initial-std run shows evidence of premature convergence/insufficient exploration (e.g. getting stuck at a mediocre policy and failing to improve further, since it rarely samples actions far from its narrow initial distribution), while the very-large-initial-std run shows slower initial learning (due to highly random early behavior) but potentially better EVENTUAL convergence (having explored more broadly before narrowing down).\n\n3) Genuinely report your actual measured comparison across all three settings, including the FINAL learned standard deviation value each run converges to (does the network learn to shrink an overly-large initial std down to something more precise, and does it learn to grow an overly-small initial std if it was insufficient for exploration?) -- discuss your genuine findings about whether the network's own learning process compensates for a poor initial standard-deviation choice, or whether initial std remains a genuinely consequential hyperparameter even with a fully-learnable standard deviation.",
      problemKn: "ಈ ಪಾಠದ ಮೊದಲ exercise ya standard-deviation-controls-exploration claim ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ continuous-control task ಮೇಲೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಹಿಂದಿನ exercise ya ನಿಮ್ಮ Gaussian-policy REINFORCE agent ಅನ್ನೂ ಮೂರು ಭಿನ್ನ fixed INITIAL standard deviation ಮೌಲ್ಯಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) ಎಲ್ಲಾ ಮೂರು initial-std settings ಗಾಗಿ learning curves ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಟ್ರ್ಯಾಕ್ ಮಾಡಿ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಎಲ್ಲಾ ಮೂರು settings ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ ನಿಮ್ಮ ನಿಜ ಅಳೆದ ಹೋಲಿಕೆಯನ್ನೂ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ, ಪ್ರತಿ run converge ಆಗುವ ಅಂತಿಮ ಕಲಿತ standard deviation ಮೌಲ್ಯ ಸೇರಿ." },
  ]),

  // ── Module 178: Actor-Critic: A2C, A3C ──
  ...set(m178, l178_1, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'The Critic as a Learned Baseline: Combining Policy Gradients and Value Functions',
      titleKn: 'Learned Baseline ಆಗಿ Critic: Policy Gradients ಮತ್ತು Value Functions ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Actor-Critic methods combine the two families of algorithms covered in this phase: an ACTOR (a policy network, like your REINFORCE policy from the previous module) that selects actions, and a CRITIC (a value function, like your DQN's Q-network from an earlier module) that estimates how good the actor's choices are, with the critic's value estimate used as the BASELINE for the actor's policy gradient update (directly connecting to the baseline-subtraction variance-reduction technique you implemented in an earlier REINFORCE exercise, formalized here as a genuinely learned baseline rather than a simple running average).\n\n1) Explain why using a LEARNED value function as the baseline (rather than a simple running-average baseline, as in your earlier REINFORCE variance-reduction exercise) can provide a more accurate, state-SPECIFIC baseline -- since a learned V(s) can differ meaningfully from state to state (correctly reflecting that some states are genuinely more promising than others), while a single running-average baseline treats all states identically, why does this state-specific baseline tend to further reduce the variance of the policy gradient update compared to a single global average?\n\n2) Explain the resulting quantity actor-critic methods typically use in place of the raw return: the ADVANTAGE function A(s,a) = Q(s,a) - V(s) (or an approximation via the TD error r + gamma*V(s') - V(s)), which measures how much BETTER an action was compared to the average action from that state -- explain why using this advantage (rather than the raw, unbaselined return) as the policy-gradient weighting signal makes intuitive sense: you want to increase the probability of actions that were better than average FROM THAT SPECIFIC STATE, not just actions that happened to occur in an episode with high total return for reasons unrelated to that specific action's quality.",
      problemKn: "Actor-Critic ವಿಧಾನಗಳು ಈ phase ನಲ್ಲಿ ಕವರ್ ಮಾಡಿದ ಎರಡು algorithm families ಗಳನ್ನೂ ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸುತ್ತವೆ: ಒಂದು ACTOR ಮತ್ತು ಒಂದು CRITIC, critic ya value estimate ಅನ್ನೂ actor ya policy gradient update ಗಾಗಿ BASELINE ಆಗಿ ಬಳಸುತ್ತಾ.\n\n1) ಒಂದು LEARNED value function ಅನ್ನೂ baseline ಆಗಿ ಬಳಸುವುದೂ ಒಂದು ಸರಳ running-average baseline ಗಿಂತ ಹೆಚ್ಚು ನಿಖರ, state-specific baseline ಒದಗಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಏಕೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ADVANTAGE function A(s,a) = Q(s,a) - V(s) ಬಳಸುವುದೂ ಏಕೆ ಸ್ವಾಭಾವಿಕವಾಗಿ ಅರ್ಥಪೂರ್ಣವಾಗಿದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing a Basic Actor-Critic Agent',
      titleKn: 'ಒಂದು ಮೂಲಭೂತ Actor-Critic Agent ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Implement a basic Actor-Critic agent from scratch, combining your REINFORCE policy network from an earlier module with a value-function critic network.\n\n1) Genuinely build the critic: a small network estimating V(s), trained with a TD-style update (minimizing squared TD error, similar to your DQN's loss but for state values instead of state-action values).\n\n2) Genuinely build the actor: reuse your policy network architecture from your REINFORCE implementation, but now update it using the TD-error-based advantage estimate (r + gamma*V(s') - V(s), computed from your critic) as the weighting signal, INSTEAD OF the full Monte Carlo return REINFORCE used -- note this lets you update after every SINGLE step (bootstrapping via the critic, similar to how TD methods update per-step rather than waiting for full episodes like Monte Carlo methods do) rather than needing to wait for full episode completion.\n\n3) Genuinely train this combined actor-critic agent on your grid-world MDP, and compare its learning curve against your pure REINFORCE agent's learning curve from an earlier module (same environment, comparable hyperparameters) -- report whether the actor-critic agent shows faster and/or smoother learning, consistent with the reduced-variance and per-step-update benefits discussed conceptually in the previous exercise, or whether the difference is less pronounced on your specific small-scale problem.",
      problemKn: "ಒಂದು ಮೂಲಭೂತ Actor-Critic agent ಅನ್ನೂ ಮೊದಲಿನಿಂದ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ, ಒಂದು ಹಿಂದಿನ module ya ನಿಮ್ಮ REINFORCE policy network ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಂದು value-function critic network ಜೊತೆ ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸಿ.\n\n1) critic ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ.\n\n2) actor ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ, TD-error-based advantage estimate ಬಳಸಿ.\n\n3) ಈ ಸಂಯೋಜಿತ actor-critic agent ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ grid-world MDP ಮೇಲೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ, ಫಲಿತಾಂಶ learning curve ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ pure REINFORCE agent ಗೆ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Comparing Actor-Critic\'s Variance and Bias Against Pure REINFORCE',
      titleKn: 'Actor-Critic ya Variance ಮತ್ತು Bias ಅನ್ನೂ Pure REINFORCE ಗೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಹೋಲಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "The TD-error-based advantage estimate used in Actor-Critic reduces variance compared to REINFORCE's full Monte Carlo return, but introduces some BIAS (since it relies on the critic's potentially-imperfect value estimates, especially early in training when the critic itself is still learning) -- a genuine bias-variance tradeoff, similar in spirit to the Ordinary-versus-Weighted-Importance-Sampling tradeoff from an earlier module. Genuinely measure both sides of this tradeoff.\n\n1) Genuinely measure gradient-estimate VARIANCE for both your Actor-Critic and pure REINFORCE implementations: run each multiple independent times (different seeds) and measure how much the learning curves vary across runs -- report whether Actor-Critic shows measurably lower variance, as the theory predicts.\n\n2) Genuinely investigate the BIAS side: since Actor-Critic's advantage estimate depends on the critic's accuracy, genuinely test what happens EARLY in training (when the critic has had little time to learn accurate values) versus LATE in training (when the critic should be more accurate) -- does Actor-Critic's learning show any signs of being misled by an inaccurate early critic (e.g. a period of unstable or misdirected policy updates before the critic catches up), a bias-related cost that pure REINFORCE (which never relies on a learned critic at all) wouldn't have?\n\n3) Genuinely report your overall measured assessment of this bias-variance tradeoff on your specific small-scale problem, and discuss whether Actor-Critic's variance-reduction benefit outweighs its bias cost for your setup (e.g. does it converge to a comparably good FINAL policy despite any early bias-related instability, and does it do so faster overall than REINFORCE) -- ground your conclusion in your genuine measured results.",
      problemKn: "Actor-Critic ನಲ್ಲಿ ಬಳಸಿದ TD-error-based advantage estimate REINFORCE ya ಪೂರ್ಣ Monte Carlo return ಗೆ ಹೋಲಿಸಿದರೆ variance ಕಡಿಮೆ ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ, ಆದರೆ ಕೆಲವು BIAS ಪರಿಚಯಿಸುತ್ತದೆ. ಈ tradeoff ya ಎರಡೂ ಬದಿಗಳನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ Actor-Critic ಮತ್ತು pure REINFORCE implementations ಎರಡಕ್ಕೂ gradient-estimate VARIANCE ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n2) BIAS ಬದಿಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ತನಿಖೆ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ ಸಣ್ಣ ಪ್ರಮಾಣದ ಸಮಸ್ಯೆ ಮೇಲೆ ಈ bias-variance tradeoff ya ನಿಮ್ಮ ಒಟ್ಟಾರೆ ಅಳೆದ ನಿರ್ಣಯ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m178, l178_2, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'A2C: Synchronous Multi-Environment Advantage Actor-Critic',
      titleKn: 'A2C: Synchronous Multi-Environment Advantage Actor-Critic',
      problem: "A2C (Advantage Actor-Critic) extends the basic actor-critic idea from the previous lesson by running MULTIPLE environment copies in parallel, collecting a batch of experience from all of them SYNCHRONOUSLY at each step, then performing one combined gradient update using data pooled across all the parallel environments.\n\n1) Explain why collecting experience from multiple, independently-progressing environment copies (rather than one single environment, as in your basic actor-critic implementation from the previous lesson) helps address the SAME correlated-data concern discussed for DQN's experience replay in an earlier module -- since each parallel environment is at a different point in its own trajectory, why does pooling their simultaneous experience into one batch produce a more diverse, less-correlated training batch than consecutive steps from a single environment would?\n\n2) Explain why A2C's SYNCHRONOUS design (waiting for ALL parallel environments to finish their step before computing the combined update) is simpler to implement and reason about than a fully asynchronous alternative (A3C, covered in the next lesson), but explain what potential inefficiency this synchronous waiting could introduce -- if different environment copies take different amounts of real wall-clock time to complete a step (e.g. due to varying simulation complexity), why might the synchronous approach be bottlenecked by the SLOWEST environment copy at each round.",
      problemKn: "A2C (Advantage Actor-Critic) ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ ಮೂಲಭೂತ actor-critic ಕಲ್ಪನೆಯನ್ನೂ ಅನೇಕ environment ಪ್ರತಿಗಳನ್ನೂ ಸಮಾನಾಂತರವಾಗಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸುವ ಮೂಲಕ ವಿಸ್ತರಿಸುತ್ತದೆ, ಪ್ರತಿ ಹಂತದಲ್ಲಿ ಎಲ್ಲಾ ಇವುಗಳಿಂದ SYNCHRONOUS ಆಗಿ experience ya ಒಂದು batch ಸಂಗ್ರಹಿಸುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) ಅನೇಕ, ಸ್ವತಂತ್ರವಾಗಿ-ಪ್ರಗತಿಪರ environment ಪ್ರತಿಗಳಿಂದ experience ಸಂಗ್ರಹಿಸುವುದೂ ಒಂದು ಹಿಂದಿನ module ya DQN ya experience replay ಗಾಗಿ ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿದ ಅದೇ correlated-data ಕಾಳಜಿಯನ್ನೂ ಹೇಗೆ ಪರಿಹರಿಸಲು ಸಹಾಯ ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) A2C ya SYNCHRONOUS ವಿನ್ಯಾಸ ಒಂದು ಪೂರ್ಣ asynchronous ಪರ್ಯಾಯಕ್ಕಿಂತ (A3C, ಮುಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದಲ್ಲಿ ಕವರ್ ಮಾಡಲಾಗಿದೆ) ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಲು ಮತ್ತು ತಾರ್ಕಿಕವಾಗಿ ಯೋಚಿಸಲು ಏಕೆ ಸರಳವಾಗಿದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ, ಮತ್ತು ಈ synchronous ಕಾಯುವಿಕೆ ಯಾವ ಸಂಭಾವ್ಯ ಅಸಮರ್ಥತೆಯನ್ನೂ ಪರಿಚಯಿಸಬಹುದು ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing A2C with Parallel Synthetic Environments',
      titleKn: 'Parallel Synthetic Environments ಜೊತೆ A2C ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Implement A2C from scratch, running multiple copies of your grid-world environment in parallel (simulated by simply maintaining several independent environment-state instances in a Python loop, no genuine multiprocessing required for this exercise).\n\n1) Genuinely modify your actor-critic implementation from the previous lesson to step MULTIPLE environment copies (e.g. 8) simultaneously at each timestep, collecting a batch of (s,a,r,s') tuples across all copies before computing a single combined gradient update using data pooled from all environments.\n\n2) Genuinely train this A2C agent on your grid-world and compare its learning curve against your single-environment actor-critic from the previous lesson (matched total environment-step budget, so you're comparing 'same total experience, different parallelism' fairly).\n\n3) Genuinely report whether A2C's pooled-parallel-environment training shows a smoother or faster learning curve than your single-environment actor-critic, connecting your genuine measured result to the decorrelation benefit discussed conceptually in the previous exercise, and report honestly if the benefit is modest or hard to distinguish from noise at your specific small grid-world scale, where a single environment's own experience diversity may already be sufficient.",
      problemKn: "ಮೊದಲಿನಿಂದ A2C ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ, ನಿಮ್ಮ grid-world environment ya ಅನೇಕ ಪ್ರತಿಗಳನ್ನೂ ಸಮಾನಾಂತರವಾಗಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ ನಿಮ್ಮ actor-critic implementation ಅನ್ನೂ ಪ್ರತಿ timestep ನಲ್ಲಿ MULTIPLE environment ಪ್ರತಿಗಳನ್ನೂ ಏಕಕಾಲದಲ್ಲಿ step ಮಾಡಲು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಮಾರ್ಪಡಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಈ A2C agent ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ grid-world ಮೇಲೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಫಲಿತಾಂಶ learning curve ಅನ್ನೂ ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ ನಿಮ್ಮ single-environment actor-critic ಗೆ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) A2C ya pooled-parallel-environment training ಒಂದು ಸ್ಮೂತ್ ಅಥವಾ ವೇಗದ learning curve ತೋರಿಸುತ್ತದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Measuring How Number of Parallel Environments Affects Training Stability',
      titleKn: 'Parallel Environments ya ಸಂಖ್ಯೆ Training Stability ಮೇಲೆ ಹೇಗೆ ಪರಿಣಾಮ ಬೀರುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely investigate how the NUMBER of parallel environments (a hyperparameter of A2C) affects training, extending your comparison from the previous exercise.\n\n1) Genuinely train your A2C implementation with several different numbers of parallel environment copies (e.g. 1, 4, 16, 64), keeping total environment-step budget matched across configurations so you're isolating the effect of parallelism/batch-diversity specifically.\n\n2) Genuinely measure and compare training-curve smoothness (variance of the return curve over a training window) across your different environment counts -- report whether more parallel environments produce measurably smoother learning curves, and whether this improvement shows diminishing returns at some point (e.g. going from 1 to 4 environments helps a lot, but going from 16 to 64 helps much less).\n\n3) Genuinely measure wall-clock training TIME for your different environment counts on your available hardware (even though you're not using genuine multiprocessing here, report the per-step computational overhead of maintaining more environment copies) -- discuss the practical tradeoff your genuine measurements reveal: more parallel environments generally improve gradient-estimate quality/stability, but with diminishing returns and real computational cost, and discuss why real production A2C/A3C implementations typically use genuine multiprocessing/multi-machine parallelism specifically to make scaling up environment count computationally cheap despite this tradeoff.",
      problemKn: "parallel environments ya ಸಂಖ್ಯೆ (A2C ya ಒಂದು hyperparameter) training ಮೇಲೆ ಹೇಗೆ ಪರಿಣಾಮ ಬೀರುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ತನಿಖೆ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n1) ಭಿನ್ನ ಸಂಖ್ಯೆಯ parallel environment ಪ್ರತಿಗಳ ಜೊತೆ ನಿಮ್ಮ A2C implementation ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಭಿನ್ನ environment counts ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ training-curve smoothness ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆದು ಹೋಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಲಭ್ಯವಿರುವ hardware ಮೇಲೆ ಭಿನ್ನ environment counts ಗಾಗಿ wall-clock training TIME ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m178, l178_3, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'A3C: Asynchronous Workers with Independent Updates',
      titleKn: 'A3C: ಸ್ವತಂತ್ರ Updates ಇರುವ Asynchronous Workers',
      problem: "A3C (Asynchronous Advantage Actor-Critic) removes A2C's synchronous-waiting requirement from the previous lesson: multiple worker processes each maintain their OWN copy of the environment and their OWN local copy of the network, computing gradients independently and pushing updates to a SHARED global network AS SOON AS each worker finishes its own local rollout, without waiting for other workers to finish theirs.\n\n1) Explain why this asynchronous design directly addresses the slowest-worker-bottleneck concern from the previous lesson's A2C discussion -- since each worker pushes its update whenever IT is ready (rather than the whole system waiting for the slowest worker), why does this let faster workers contribute more updates per unit time without being held back by slower ones?\n\n2) Explain a genuine new subtlety this asynchronicity introduces: since multiple workers are updating the SAME shared global network concurrently, a worker's gradient (computed using the version of the global network's weights it downloaded at the START of its rollout) might be applied to a global network that has ALREADY CHANGED (due to other workers' updates that happened in the meantime) -- explain why this creates a form of 'stale gradient' problem, where a worker's update is based on a slightly outdated view of the network, and discuss why this staleness is generally considered an acceptable tradeoff in A3C (introducing some noise/imprecision) in exchange for the substantial throughput/parallelism benefit of never having workers sit idle waiting for each other.",
      problemKn: "A3C (Asynchronous Advantage Actor-Critic) ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ A2C ya synchronous-waiting ಅಗತ್ಯವನ್ನೂ ತೆಗೆದುಹಾಕುತ್ತದೆ: ಅನೇಕ worker processes ಪ್ರತಿಯೊಂದೂ environment ya ತಮ್ಮ ಸ್ವಂತ ಪ್ರತಿಯನ್ನೂ ಮತ್ತು network ya ತಮ್ಮ ಸ್ವಂತ local ಪ್ರತಿಯನ್ನೂ ನಿರ್ವಹಿಸುತ್ತವೆ, ಸ್ವತಂತ್ರವಾಗಿ gradients ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕುತ್ತವೆ ಮತ್ತು ಪ್ರತಿ worker ತನ್ನ ಸ್ವಂತ local rollout ಮುಗಿಸಿದ ತಕ್ಷಣ ಒಂದು SHARED global network ಗೆ updates ತಳ್ಳುತ್ತವೆ.\n\n1) ಈ asynchronous ವಿನ್ಯಾಸ ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ slowest-worker-bottleneck ಕಾಳಜಿಯನ್ನೂ ನೇರವಾಗಿ ಹೇಗೆ ಪರಿಹರಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಈ asynchronicity ಪರಿಚಯಿಸುವ ಒಂದು ನಿಜ ಹೊಸ ಸೂಕ್ಷ್ಮತೆಯನ್ನೂ ವಿವರಿಸಿ, 'stale gradient' ಸಮಸ್ಯೆ ಸೇರಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing a Simplified A3C-Style Update Scheme',
      titleKn: 'ಒಂದು ಸರಳೀಕೃತ A3C-Style Update Scheme ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely simulate the essential A3C mechanic (independent workers pushing asynchronous updates to a shared network) without needing genuine multiprocessing, using a simplified round-robin simulation.\n\n1) Genuinely implement a scheme where you maintain ONE shared global network, and simulate several 'workers' by, in a simple sequential loop, having each worker COPY the current global weights, run a short local rollout (a few steps) using that copied local network, compute a gradient from that local rollout, and IMMEDIATELY apply that gradient to the CURRENT global network (which may have already been updated by a 'previous' simulated worker in your loop, genuinely reproducing the staleness effect discussed in the previous exercise, since each worker's gradient was computed against an now-outdated local copy).\n\n2) Genuinely train your grid-world agent with this simulated-A3C update scheme and compare its learning curve against your synchronous A2C implementation from the previous lesson (matched total environment-step budget).\n\n3) Genuinely report your measured comparison, and genuinely construct a DELIBERATE staleness stress-test: artificially increase the 'staleness' by having each simulated worker run a LONGER local rollout before applying its update (making its gradient based on an even more outdated view of the global network by the time it's applied) -- report whether increasing staleness this way measurably degrades learning stability/speed, providing concrete evidence for the staleness tradeoff discussed conceptually in the previous exercise.",
      problemKn: "ನಿಜ multiprocessing ಇಲ್ಲದೆ, ಒಂದು ಸರಳೀಕೃತ round-robin simulation ಬಳಸಿ A3C ya ಪ್ರಮುಖ ಯಾಂತ್ರಿಕತೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಸಿಮ್ಯುಲೇಟ್ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n1) ONE shared global network ನಿರ್ವಹಿಸುವ ಮತ್ತು ಹಲವಾರು 'workers' ಅನ್ನೂ ಸಿಮ್ಯುಲೇಟ್ ಮಾಡುವ ಒಂದು scheme ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಈ simulated-A3C update scheme ಜೊತೆ ನಿಮ್ಮ grid-world agent ಅನ್ನೂ train ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಫಲಿತಾಂಶ learning curve ಅನ್ನೂ ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ ನಿಮ್ಮ synchronous A2C implementation ಗೆ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಒಂದು ಉದ್ದೇಶಪೂರ್ವಕ staleness stress-test ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ರಚಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Building a Capstone Comparison of Every Control Algorithm Built in This Module and Phase',
      titleKn: 'ಈ Module ಮತ್ತು Phase ನಲ್ಲಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿದ ಪ್ರತಿ Control Algorithm ya ಒಂದು Capstone ಹೋಲಿಕೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "As a capstone synthesis spanning this module and the model-free control methods covered throughout this phase, genuinely consolidate a comprehensive comparison.\n\n1) Genuinely identify and re-run (or pull results from) EVERY control algorithm you've genuinely trained on your grid-world MDP across this phase: tabular Q-Learning/SARSA, DQN (with and without stabilization techniques), REINFORCE, and Actor-Critic/A2C/your simulated A3C -- ensure all use a comparable evaluation protocol (same grid-world, matched total environment-step budget where feasible).\n\n2) Genuinely compile a comparison table: final policy quality (versus the true optimal from dynamic programming), sample efficiency (environment steps to reach a target performance threshold), and learning-curve variance across seeds, for every algorithm you've built.\n\n3) Genuinely write a short synthesis (a few paragraphs) reflecting on your own hands-on findings across this entire phase's progression from tabular dynamic programming through Monte Carlo, TD control, DQN, and policy-gradient/actor-critic methods -- discuss which algorithm(s) performed best on YOUR specific small grid-world, whether the theoretical advantages of more sophisticated methods (function approximation, variance reduction, asynchronous parallelism) showed up as measurable benefits at this small scale or mainly matter at larger scale, and what you'd choose to build on for a genuinely larger, more complex RL problem based on the concrete, measured evidence from your own experiments throughout this phase rather than from general reputation alone.",
      problemKn: "ಈ module ಮತ್ತು ಈ phase ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ ಕವರ್ ಮಾಡಿದ model-free control ವಿಧಾನಗಳನ್ನೂ ವ್ಯಾಪಿಸುವ ಒಂದು capstone synthesis ಆಗಿ, ಒಂದು ಸಮಗ್ರ ಹೋಲಿಕೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಒಗ್ಗೂಡಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಈ phase ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ ನಿಮ್ಮ grid-world MDP ಮೇಲೆ ನೀವು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿದ EVERY control algorithm ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಗುರುತಿಸಿ ಮರುಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ (ಅಥವಾ ಫಲಿತಾಂಶಗಳನ್ನೂ ಸೆಳೆಯಿರಿ).\n\n2) ಒಂದು ಹೋಲಿಕೆ table ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಸಂಗ್ರಹಿಸಿ: ಅಂತಿಮ policy quality, sample efficiency, ಮತ್ತು seeds ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ learning-curve variance, ನೀವು ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿದ ಪ್ರತಿ algorithm ಗಾಗಿ.\n\n3) ಈ ಸಂಪೂರ್ಣ phase ya ಪ್ರಗತಿಯ ಬಗ್ಗೆ ಒಂದು ಸಣ್ಣ synthesis ಬರೆಯಿರಿ, ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸ್ವಂತ hands-on ಶೋಧನೆಗಳ ಮೇಲೆ ಪ್ರತಿಬಿಂಬಿಸುತ್ತಾ." },
  ]),

  // ── Module 179: PPO ──
  ...set(m179, l179_1, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'The Problem PPO Solves: Destructively Large Policy Updates',
      titleKn: 'PPO ಪರಿಹರಿಸುವ ಸಮಸ್ಯೆ: ವಿನಾಶಕಾರಿಯಾಗಿ ದೊಡ್ಡ Policy Updates',
      problem: "Vanilla policy gradient methods (REINFORCE, basic Actor-Critic from earlier lessons in this module) can occasionally take an update step that changes the policy TOO much in one go, based on a single noisy gradient estimate -- potentially causing the policy to suddenly become much worse, from which it may struggle to recover (since a badly-changed policy also generates worse future data, compounding the problem, unlike supervised learning where a single bad gradient step doesn't corrupt your future training data). Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) addresses this by explicitly CONSTRAINING how much the policy is allowed to change in a single update.\n\n1) Explain concretely why this problem is potentially more severe for RL than for supervised learning: in supervised learning, a bad gradient step produces a temporarily worse model, but the NEXT batch of training data is unaffected (drawn from a fixed dataset); in RL, explain why a policy that's become worse due to a bad update also generates worse TRAINING DATA going forward (since the policy itself determines what states/actions get experienced), potentially creating a downward spiral that's genuinely harder to recover from than a supervised-learning setback.\n\n2) Explain, at a conceptual level (without the full mathematical formula yet, covered in the next lesson), what PPO's core idea is: instead of allowing an unconstrained gradient step, PPO explicitly limits (clips) how far the new policy's action probabilities are allowed to move away from the OLD policy's probabilities for the same actions within a single update -- why does capping this per-update change directly address the destructively-large-update problem.",
      problemKn: "Vanilla policy gradient methods ಕೆಲವೊಮ್ಮೆ ಒಂದೇ ಬಾರಿಗೆ policy ಅನ್ನೂ ಬಹಳ ಹೆಚ್ಚು ಬದಲಾಯಿಸುವ ಒಂದು update ಹಂತ ತೆಗೆದುಕೊಳ್ಳಬಹುದು, ಒಂದೇ ಗದ್ದಲದ gradient estimate ಆಧರಿಸಿ. Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) ಪ್ರತಿ single update ನಲ್ಲಿ policy ಎಷ್ಟು ಬದಲಾಯಿಸಲು ಅನುಮತಿಸಲಾಗಿದೆ ಎಂದು ಸ್ಪಷ್ಟವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಬಂಧಿಸುವ ಮೂಲಕ ಇದನ್ನೂ ಪರಿಹರಿಸುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) ಈ ಸಮಸ್ಯೆ supervised learning ಗಿಂತ RL ಗೆ ಏಕೆ ಹೆಚ್ಚು ತೀವ್ರವಾಗಿರಬಹುದು ಎಂದು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟವಾಗಿ ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) PPO ya ಪ್ರಮುಖ ಕಲ್ಪನೆಯನ್ನೂ ಪರಿಕಲ್ಪನಾತ್ಮಕವಾಗಿ ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Demonstrating a Destructive Update in Vanilla Policy Gradient',
      titleKn: 'Vanilla Policy Gradient ನಲ್ಲಿ ಒಂದು Destructive Update ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪ್ರದರ್ಶಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely reproduce the destructive-update problem described in the previous exercise on your own REINFORCE/Actor-Critic implementations, to have a concrete failure case to compare PPO against once you implement it in the next lesson.\n\n1) Genuinely train your Actor-Critic implementation from an earlier lesson in this module on your grid-world with a DELIBERATELY aggressive learning rate for the policy (actor) network (much larger than what worked well in earlier exercises) -- run this training and track the episode-return learning curve closely.\n\n2) Genuinely identify and report at least one specific point in training where the return curve shows a SUDDEN, sharp DROP (evidence of a destructively large policy update that made the policy temporarily much worse) -- report the actual episode number and the magnitude of the drop you observe.\n\n3) Genuinely investigate whether the policy RECOVERS from this drop given more training time, or whether it remains permanently degraded/never fully recovers within your training budget -- report your genuine observation, and discuss how this observed failure mode concretely motivates the need for PPO's update-clipping mechanism, which you'll implement and directly compare against this destructive baseline in the next lesson.",
      problemKn: "ಹಿಂದಿನ exercise ya ವಿವರಿಸಿದ destructive-update ಸಮಸ್ಯೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸ್ವಂತ REINFORCE/Actor-Critic implementations ಮೇಲೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪುನರುತ್ಪಾದಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ ನಿಮ್ಮ Actor-Critic implementation ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಂದು ಉದ್ದೇಶಪೂರ್ವಕವಾಗಿ ಆಕ್ರಮಣಕಾರಿ learning rate ಜೊತೆ ನಿಮ್ಮ grid-world ಮೇಲೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) training ನಲ್ಲಿ return curve ಒಂದು ಹಠಾತ್, ತೀಕ್ಷ್ಣ DROP ತೋರಿಸುವ ಕನಿಷ್ಠ ಒಂದು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ ಬಿಂದುವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಗುರುತಿಸಿ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) policy ಈ drop ಇಂದ ಚೇತರಿಸಿಕೊಳ್ಳುತ್ತದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ತನಿಖೆ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Measuring How Learning Rate Interacts with Destructive Update Frequency',
      titleKn: 'Learning Rate Destructive Update Frequency ಜೊತೆ ಹೇಗೆ ಸಂವಹಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely characterize the destructive-update phenomenon more systematically across a range of learning rates, to build a clear empirical picture before implementing PPO's fix in the next lesson.\n\n1) Genuinely train your Actor-Critic implementation at SEVERAL different learning rates (e.g. a very small one that worked well in earlier exercises, and progressively larger ones up to a clearly-too-aggressive value), running multiple independent seeds at each learning rate.\n\n2) Genuinely define and compute a quantitative 'destructiveness' metric: e.g. the largest single-update drop in a moving-average return, measured across training, for each run -- report the actual measured destructiveness at each learning rate.\n\n3) Genuinely report the relationship you observe between learning rate and destructiveness (does destructiveness increase smoothly with learning rate, or is there a sharper threshold beyond which it appears?), and genuinely report whether HIGHER learning rates, despite showing more destructive-update incidents, sometimes still reach a BETTER final policy faster overall (if they recover well) than very conservative low learning rates that never destabilize but also learn very slowly -- discuss this speed-versus-stability tradeoff based on your genuine measurements, framing the motivation for PPO precisely as an attempt to get the FAST learning of a large update step without its destructive-update risk, which you'll test directly once you implement PPO in the next lesson.",
      problemKn: "PPO ya fix ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವ ಮೊದಲು ಒಂದು ಸ್ಪಷ್ಟ ಪ್ರಾಯೋಗಿಕ ಚಿತ್ರ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಲು, destructive-update ವಿದ್ಯಮಾನವನ್ನೂ learning rates ya ಒಂದು ಶ್ರೇಣಿಯಾದ್ಯಂತ ಹೆಚ್ಚು ವ್ಯವಸ್ಥಿತವಾಗಿ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಹಲವಾರು ಭಿನ್ನ learning rates ನಲ್ಲಿ ನಿಮ್ಮ Actor-Critic implementation ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) ಒಂದು ಪರಿಮಾಣಾತ್ಮಕ 'destructiveness' metric ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವ್ಯಾಖ್ಯಾನಿಸಿ ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಿ.\n\n3) learning rate ಮತ್ತು destructiveness ನಡುವೆ ನೀವು ಗಮನಿಸುವ ಸಂಬಂಧವನ್ನೂ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m179, l179_2, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'The Clipped Surrogate Objective: How PPO Limits Update Size',
      titleKn: 'Clipped Surrogate Objective: PPO Update Size ಅನ್ನೂ ಹೇಗೆ ಮಿತಿಗೊಳಿಸುತ್ತದೆ',
      problem: "PPO's core mechanism is the CLIPPED SURROGATE OBJECTIVE: it computes the probability RATIO r(theta) = pi_new(a|s) / pi_old(a|s) (how much more or less likely the new policy makes the taken action compared to the old policy), multiplies this ratio by the advantage estimate, but then CLIPS this ratio to stay within a fixed range (e.g. [0.8, 1.2], i.e. plus-or-minus 20%) before taking the minimum of the clipped and unclipped versions -- explicitly limiting how much credit/blame a single update can assign based on a large probability-ratio change.\n\n1) Explain intuitively why clipping the ratio specifically (rather than clipping the raw gradient magnitude, a simpler-sounding alternative) directly targets the destructive-update problem from the previous lesson -- since the ratio directly measures HOW MUCH the policy's action probabilities have shifted, why does bounding this ratio directly bound how far the policy is allowed to move in probability space for that specific action, in a way that's more directly interpretable than an abstract gradient-magnitude cap?\n\n2) Explain why PPO takes the MINIMUM of the clipped and unclipped objective (rather than just always using the clipped version) -- this asymmetric design means that when the advantage is POSITIVE (the action was good), clipping prevents the objective from providing INCREASING benefit beyond the clip range (discouraging overly aggressive increases in that action's probability), but when the advantage is NEGATIVE (the action was bad), the min-of-two design still allows the UNCLIPPED objective to apply if it's more pessimistic, ensuring the policy can still be pushed AWAY from clearly bad actions without the clip artificially protecting them -- explain why this asymmetry (being cautious about over-reinforcing good actions, but not overly cautious about correcting bad ones) is a sensible design choice.",
      problemKn: "PPO ya ಪ್ರಮುಖ ಯಾಂತ್ರಿಕತೆ CLIPPED SURROGATE OBJECTIVE ಆಗಿದೆ: ಇದೂ probability RATIO r(theta) = pi_new(a|s) / pi_old(a|s) ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕುತ್ತದೆ, ಇದನ್ನೂ advantage estimate ಇಂದ ಗುಣಿಸುತ್ತದೆ, ಆದರೆ ನಂತರ ಈ ratio ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಂದು ಸ್ಥಿರ ಶ್ರೇಣಿಯೊಳಗೆ ಇರಲು CLIPS ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) ratio ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟವಾಗಿ clipping ಮಾಡುವುದೂ ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ destructive-update ಸಮಸ್ಯೆಯನ್ನೂ ನೇರವಾಗಿ ಏಕೆ ಗುರಿಪಡಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ಸ್ವಾಭಾವಿಕವಾಗಿ ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) PPO clipped ಮತ್ತು unclipped objective ya MINIMUM ಅನ್ನೂ ಏಕೆ ತೆಗೆದುಕೊಳ್ಳುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ, ಈ asymmetric ವಿನ್ಯಾಸ ಒಂದು ಸಮಂಜಸ ಆಯ್ಕೆ ಏಕೆ ಎಂದು ಸೇರಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing the Clipped Surrogate Objective and Verifying It Bounds Updates',
      titleKn: 'Clipped Surrogate Objective ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಇದೂ Updates ಮಿತಿಗೊಳಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Implement PPO's clipped surrogate objective from scratch in PyTorch, building on your Actor-Critic implementation from earlier lessons in this module.\n\n1) Genuinely implement the probability-ratio computation, the clipping operation, and the min-of-clipped-and-unclipped objective, using your existing actor (policy) and critic (value function) networks.\n\n2) Genuinely verify the clipping mechanism works as intended with a direct, isolated test: construct a specific scenario with hand-chosen old and new policy probabilities that would produce a ratio OUTSIDE your clip range (e.g. deliberately construct new probabilities that are very different from old probabilities for a specific action), and confirm your implementation's computed objective value matches the CLIPPED (not unclipped) expression in this case -- report your actual computed values for both the clipped and unclipped versions to show the clipping is genuinely taking effect.\n\n3) Genuinely train your full PPO implementation on your grid-world using the AGGRESSIVE learning rate that caused destructive updates in your Actor-Critic implementation from the previous lesson -- report whether PPO's clipping mechanism successfully PREVENTS the sudden, sharp return-curve drops you observed with vanilla Actor-Critic at this same aggressive learning rate, providing direct empirical evidence that PPO's clipping achieves its intended stabilizing purpose.",
      problemKn: "PyTorch ನಲ್ಲಿ ಮೊದಲಿನಿಂದ PPO ya clipped surrogate objective ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ, ಈ module ya ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠಗಳ ನಿಮ್ಮ Actor-Critic implementation ಮೇಲೆ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸುತ್ತಾ.\n\n1) probability-ratio ಲೆಕ್ಕಾಚಾರ, clipping operation, ಮತ್ತು min-of-clipped-and-unclipped objective ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n2) clipping ಯಾಂತ್ರಿಕತೆ ಉದ್ದೇಶಿಸಿದಂತೆ ಕೆಲಸ ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ಒಂದು ನೇರ, ಪ್ರತ್ಯೇಕ test ಜೊತೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ Actor-Critic ಗೆ destructive updates ಉಂಟುಮಾಡಿದ ಆಕ್ರಮಣಕಾರಿ learning rate ಬಳಸಿ ನಿಮ್ಮ ಪೂರ್ಣ PPO implementation ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ grid-world ಮೇಲೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Sweeping the Clip Range and Measuring Its Effect on Learning Speed and Stability',
      titleKn: 'Clip Range ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ Sweep ಮಾಡಿ Learning Speed ಮತ್ತು Stability ಮೇಲಿನ ಅದರ ಪರಿಣಾಮ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "The clip range (epsilon, e.g. 0.2 meaning +/-20%) is itself a tunable hyperparameter controlling how conservative PPO's updates are. Genuinely investigate this tradeoff.\n\n1) Genuinely train your PPO implementation from the previous exercise at several different clip range values (e.g. a very tight 0.05, a moderate 0.2, and a very loose 0.5) at your aggressive learning rate, keeping everything else fixed.\n\n2) Genuinely measure and compare your destructiveness metric from an earlier lesson's exercise (largest single-update drop in moving-average return) across your different clip ranges -- report whether tighter clip ranges show measurably LESS destructive-update behavior, as expected.\n\n3) Genuinely measure overall learning SPEED (episodes/steps to reach a target performance level) across your clip range sweep -- report whether an overly tight clip range (very conservative updates) genuinely slows down learning (since useful, large-but-legitimate updates also get artificially constrained), providing evidence for a genuine speed-versus-stability tradeoff in the clip range choice, similar in spirit to other stability-versus-speed tradeoffs encountered throughout this phase (e.g. target-network update frequency, learning rate itself) -- report your genuine measured sweet-spot clip range for your specific problem, and discuss why PPO's widely-cited default of 0.2 is a reasonable general starting point but not a universally optimal value, given that your own measured tradeoff curve is specific to your particular problem's characteristics.",
      problemKn: "clip range (epsilon) ಸ್ವತಃ PPO ya updates ಎಷ್ಟು conservative ಎಂದು ನಿಯಂತ್ರಿಸುವ ಒಂದು ಟ್ಯೂನ್ ಮಾಡಬಹುದಾದ hyperparameter ಆಗಿದೆ. ಈ tradeoff ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ತನಿಖೆ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n1) ಹಿಂದಿನ exercise ya ನಿಮ್ಮ PPO implementation ಅನ್ನೂ ಹಲವಾರು ಭಿನ್ನ clip range ಮೌಲ್ಯಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ನಿಮ್ಮ ಆಕ್ರಮಣಕಾರಿ learning rate ನಲ್ಲಿ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಭಿನ್ನ clip ranges ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ destructiveness metric ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆದು ಹೋಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ clip range sweep ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ ಒಟ್ಟಾರೆ learning SPEED ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m179, l179_3, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'Multiple Epochs per Batch: Reusing Data Safely Under PPO',
      titleKn: 'ಪ್ರತಿ Batch ಗೆ Multiple Epochs: PPO ಅಡಿಯಲ್ಲಿ Data ಅನ್ನೂ ಸುರಕ್ಷಿತವಾಗಿ ಮರುಬಳಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Vanilla policy gradient methods are strictly ON-POLICY: once a batch of experience is used for one gradient update, it must be discarded, since the policy has now changed and the old data no longer reflects the current policy's behavior (using it again would be somewhat like the off-policy mismatch problem from an earlier Monte Carlo module, without importance-sampling correction). PPO's clipping mechanism, however, makes it SAFE to reuse the same batch of experience for MULTIPLE gradient update epochs before collecting new data.\n\n1) Explain why PPO's clipping specifically enables this safe reuse -- since the clip range bounds how far the ratio (and therefore the effective policy change) is allowed to drift within the surrogate objective, why does this let you take several gradient steps on the SAME batch of old-policy data without the same risk of a wildly-diverging, destructively-updated policy that a vanilla policy gradient method would face if it reused stale data for multiple updates?\n\n2) Explain the practical efficiency benefit this provides -- similar in spirit to how experience replay in DQN improved sample efficiency by reusing stored experience for multiple updates (from an earlier module), why does PPO's multi-epoch reuse of each collected batch let it extract more learning signal from each expensive round of real environment interaction, compared to a strictly single-use-per-batch vanilla policy gradient approach.",
      problemKn: "Vanilla policy gradient methods ಕಟ್ಟುನಿಟ್ಟಾಗಿ ON-POLICY ಆಗಿವೆ: experience ya ಒಂದು batch ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಂದು gradient update ಗಾಗಿ ಬಳಸಿದ ನಂತರ, ಅದನ್ನೂ ಬಿಟ್ಟುಬಿಡಬೇಕು. PPO ya clipping ಯಾಂತ್ರಿಕತೆ, ಆದರೂ, ಹೊಸ data ಸಂಗ್ರಹಿಸುವ ಮೊದಲು ಅದೇ batch ya experience ಅನ್ನೂ MULTIPLE gradient update epochs ಗಾಗಿ ಮರುಬಳಸಲು ಸುರಕ್ಷಿತ ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) PPO ya clipping ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟವಾಗಿ ಈ ಸುರಕ್ಷಿತ ಮರುಬಳಕೆಯನ್ನೂ ಹೇಗೆ ಸಕ್ರಿಯಗೊಳಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಇದೂ ಒದಗಿಸುವ ಪ್ರಾಯೋಗಿಕ ದಕ್ಷತೆಯ ಅನುಕೂಲ ವಿವರಿಸಿ, ಒಂದು ಹಿಂದಿನ module ya DQN ya experience replay ಗೆ ಸಂಪರ್ಕಿಸುತ್ತಾ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing Multi-Epoch PPO Training and Measuring the Sample-Efficiency Gain',
      titleKn: 'Multi-Epoch PPO Training ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ Sample-Efficiency Gain ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Extend your PPO implementation from the previous lesson to perform multiple gradient-update epochs per collected batch of experience.\n\n1) Genuinely modify your training loop: for each batch of collected environment experience, run K gradient update epochs (e.g. K=4) over that SAME batch (reshuffling into minibatches each epoch, as is standard practice) before collecting a new batch of experience, rather than discarding the batch after one update.\n\n2) Genuinely train your PPO agent with several different K values (e.g. K=1, matching vanilla single-use policy gradient, versus K=4, versus K=10) on your grid-world, keeping total environment-step budget matched across configurations.\n\n3) Genuinely measure and compare learning speed (environment steps needed to reach a target performance level) across your different K values -- report whether higher K genuinely improves sample efficiency (reaching target performance with fewer real environment steps, since more learning is extracted from each batch), and genuinely test whether an EXCESSIVELY large K (e.g. K=50) eventually hurts performance despite PPO's clipping protection -- report whether you observe diminishing returns or even degradation at very high K, discussing why even PPO's clipping-based safety has limits: reusing the same batch too many times can still lead the policy to overfit to that specific batch's data or drift further than the clip range can fully compensate for over many repeated epochs.",
      problemKn: "ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ ನಿಮ್ಮ PPO implementation ಅನ್ನೂ ಪ್ರತಿ ಸಂಗ್ರಹಿಸಿದ experience batch ಗೆ ಅನೇಕ gradient-update epochs ನಿರ್ವಹಿಸಲು ವಿಸ್ತರಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ training loop ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಮಾರ್ಪಡಿಸಿ: ಪ್ರತಿ collected experience batch ಗೆ, ಹೊಸ batch ಸಂಗ್ರಹಿಸುವ ಮೊದಲು ಅದೇ batch ಮೇಲೆ K gradient update epochs ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಹಲವಾರು ಭಿನ್ನ K ಮೌಲ್ಯಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ನಿಮ್ಮ PPO agent ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ grid-world ಮೇಲೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಭಿನ್ನ K ಮೌಲ್ಯಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ learning speed ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆದು ಹೋಲಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Building a Final PPO-versus-Everything Capstone Comparison',
      titleKn: 'ಒಂದು ಅಂತಿಮ PPO-vs-Everything Capstone ಹೋಲಿಕೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "As a capstone synthesis for this module, genuinely extend your earlier cross-algorithm comparison (from the Actor-Critic module) to include your fully-implemented, multi-epoch PPO.\n\n1) Genuinely re-run (or compile existing results for) DQN, REINFORCE, basic Actor-Critic, A2C, and your final multi-epoch PPO, all on your grid-world MDP with matched total environment-step budgets.\n\n2) Genuinely measure, for all algorithms: final policy quality, sample efficiency, learning-curve variance across seeds, AND your destructiveness metric from earlier PPO lessons (how large the worst single-update drop in performance was during training) -- compile a complete comparison table.\n\n3) Genuinely write a final, evidence-grounded assessment: does PPO achieve the best OVERALL combination of these properties on your specific small-scale problem (as its wide real-world adoption as a robust, general-purpose default RL algorithm would suggest), or does a simpler method perform comparably well or better on some specific dimension for your particular small grid-world -- discuss honestly where PPO's benefits show up clearly in YOUR measurements and where they don't, and reflect on what you've learned about the general principle (recurring throughout this entire phase) that a method's theoretical advantages don't automatically guarantee a measurable practical advantage at every scale and on every problem, making genuine empirical verification -- exactly what you've been doing throughout every exercise in this phase -- an essential complement to understanding the theory.",
      problemKn: "ಈ module ಗಾಗಿ ಒಂದು capstone synthesis ಆಗಿ, ಹಿಂದಿನ Actor-Critic module ya ನಿಮ್ಮ cross-algorithm ಹೋಲಿಕೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ ಪೂರ್ಣವಾಗಿ-ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿದ, multi-epoch PPO ಸೇರಿಸಲು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿಸ್ತರಿಸಿ.\n\n1) DQN, REINFORCE, ಮೂಲಭೂತ Actor-Critic, A2C, ಮತ್ತು ನಿಮ್ಮ ಅಂತಿಮ multi-epoch PPO ಅನ್ನೂ, ಎಲ್ಲಾ ನಿಮ್ಮ grid-world MDP ಮೇಲೆ ಹೊಂದಿಸಿದ ಒಟ್ಟು environment-step budgets ಜೊತೆ, ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಮರುಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಎಲ್ಲಾ algorithms ಗಾಗಿ ಅಂತಿಮ policy quality, sample efficiency, learning-curve variance, ಮತ್ತು destructiveness metric ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆದು ಒಂದು ಪೂರ್ಣ ಹೋಲಿಕೆ table ಸಂಗ್ರಹಿಸಿ.\n\n3) PPO ನಿಮ್ಮ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ ಸಣ್ಣ ಪ್ರಮಾಣದ ಸಮಸ್ಯೆ ಮೇಲೆ ಈ ಗುಣಗಳ ಅತ್ಯುತ್ತಮ ಒಟ್ಟಾರೆ ಸಂಯೋಜನೆ ಸಾಧಿಸುತ್ತದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ಒಂದು ಅಂತಿಮ, ಸಾಕ್ಷ್ಯ-ಆಧಾರಿತ ನಿರ್ಣಯ ಬರೆಯಿರಿ." },
  ]),

  // ── Module 180: Reward Modeling and RLHF ──
  ...set(m180, l180_1, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'When the Reward Function Itself Must Be Learned',
      titleKn: 'Reward Function ಸ್ವತಃ ಕಲಿಯಬೇಕಾದಾಗ',
      problem: "Every RL algorithm covered so far in this phase assumed the REWARD FUNCTION was simply given/known (e.g. your grid-world's hand-designed -1-per-step, +10-for-goal reward). For many real tasks (as introduced conceptually in an earlier Phase 10 module on RLHF), a good reward function genuinely cannot be hand-specified -- e.g. 'write a helpful, harmless response' has no simple mathematical formula. Reward modeling instead LEARNS a reward function from human preference judgments (comparing pairs of outputs and indicating which is better), then uses this LEARNED reward model as the reward signal for standard RL training (e.g. PPO, from the previous module).\n\n1) Explain why comparing PAIRS of outputs (relative judgments: 'A is better than B') is generally easier and more reliable for humans to provide than absolute numeric scores (e.g. 'rate this response 7.3 out of 10') -- why might people disagree wildly on absolute numeric ratings for the same output while agreeing much more consistently on relative comparisons between two specific outputs?\n\n2) Explain the resulting two-stage pipeline this creates: STAGE 1 trains a reward model (a supervised learning problem: given many human preference comparisons, learn to predict which of two outputs a human would prefer) and STAGE 2 uses this trained reward model as the reward signal for RL training (e.g. PPO) of the actual policy being optimized -- why does treating this as two distinct, sequential learning problems (rather than trying to learn the policy and reward function simultaneously from scratch) make the overall problem more tractable.",
      problemKn: "ಈ phase ನಲ್ಲಿ ಇಲ್ಲಿಯವರೆಗೆ ಕವರ್ ಮಾಡಿದ ಪ್ರತಿ RL algorithm REWARD FUNCTION ಅನ್ನೂ ಕೇವಲ ಕೊಟ್ಟಿದೆ/ತಿಳಿದಿದೆ ಎಂದು ಊಹಿಸಿತು. ಅನೇಕ ನಿಜ tasks ಗಳಿಗೆ, ಒಂದು ಉತ್ತಮ reward function ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಕೈಯಾರೆ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟಪಡಿಸಲು ಸಾಧ್ಯವಿಲ್ಲ. Reward modeling ಬದಲಿಗೆ human preference judgments ಇಂದ ಒಂದು reward function ಕಲಿಯುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) ಔಟ್ಪುಟ್ಸ್ ya PAIRS ಹೋಲಿಸುವುದೂ ಜನರಿಗೆ absolute numeric scores ಗಿಂತ ಸಾಮಾನ್ಯವಾಗಿ ಏಕೆ ಸುಲಭ ಮತ್ತು ಹೆಚ್ಚು ವಿಶ್ವಾಸಾರ್ಹ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಇದೂ ಸೃಷ್ಟಿಸುವ ಫಲಿತಾಂಶ two-stage pipeline ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Training a Reward Model from Synthetic Preference Data',
      titleKn: 'Synthetic Preference Data ಇಂದ ಒಂದು Reward Model ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ Train ಮಾಡುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely build and train a reward model on a controllable synthetic task, so you have known ground truth to verify against.\n\n1) Genuinely define a simple synthetic 'output' space with a KNOWN true quality function you control (e.g. outputs are simple numeric vectors, and true quality is a known function of those vectors, like negative distance from a target vector -- this stands in for an unknown-to-the-model true human preference function).\n\n2) Genuinely generate synthetic preference data: sample many pairs of outputs, compute which one has higher TRUE quality (using your known function), and record this as a preference label ('A preferred over B') -- this simulates human preference judgments without needing actual human annotators.\n\n3) Genuinely train a reward model (a small neural network taking an output and predicting a scalar reward score) using the standard Bradley-Terry-style preference loss (training the model so that reward(preferred) > reward(rejected) for each pair, e.g. via a logistic/sigmoid loss on the score difference) -- report your actual training loss curve, and genuinely evaluate your trained reward model on a HELD-OUT set of preference pairs, reporting what fraction of held-out preferences it correctly predicts, and separately checking whether the reward model's predicted scores correlate well with the TRUE quality function you defined (even though the reward model never saw the true quality values directly, only pairwise preferences derived from them).",
      problemKn: "ಒಂದು controllable synthetic task ಮೇಲೆ ಒಂದು reward model ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ train ಮಾಡಿ, ಇದರಿಂದ ನೀವು ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಲು ತಿಳಿದ ground truth ಹೊಂದಿರುತ್ತೀರಿ.\n\n1) ಒಂದು ತಿಳಿದ true quality function ಇರುವ ಒಂದು ಸರಳ synthetic 'output' space ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವ್ಯಾಖ್ಯಾನಿಸಿ.\n\n2) synthetic preference data ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಉತ್ಪಾದಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಪ್ರಮಾಣಿತ Bradley-Terry-style preference loss ಬಳಸಿ ಒಂದು reward model ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ, ಫಲಿತಾಂಶಗಳನ್ನೂ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Measuring How Preference Data Quantity and Noise Affect Reward Model Accuracy',
      titleKn: 'Preference Data Quantity ಮತ್ತು Noise Reward Model Accuracy ಮೇಲೆ ಹೇಗೆ ಪರಿಣಾಮ ಬೀರುತ್ತವೆ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Real human preference labels are both LIMITED in quantity (expensive to collect at scale) and NOISY (humans sometimes disagree with themselves or each other, or make inconsistent judgments). Genuinely investigate both factors using your synthetic setup from the previous exercise.\n\n1) Genuinely train your reward model with several different amounts of preference training data (e.g. 50, 500, 5000 pairs) -- report how held-out preference-prediction accuracy scales with data quantity, and identify roughly how much data was needed to reach a reasonably high accuracy on your specific synthetic task.\n\n2) Genuinely inject artificial LABEL NOISE into your preference data: for a chosen fraction of training pairs (e.g. 10%, 30%), FLIP the preference label to be incorrect (simulating inconsistent/noisy human judgments) -- train reward models on these noise-corrupted datasets and report how held-out accuracy (evaluated against the TRUE, uncorrupted preference labels) degrades as label noise increases.\n\n3) Genuinely report your combined findings on the data-quantity-versus-noise-tolerance tradeoff, and discuss the practical implication for real RLHF systems: given that real human preference labels are both limited and somewhat noisy, why does this motivate techniques like collecting MULTIPLE human judgments per comparison (to average out individual noise) or actively prioritizing which comparisons to collect labels for (to get the most informative data per limited labeling budget) -- connect your genuine measured findings to why reward-model data quality and quantity is often a central practical bottleneck in real RLHF pipelines, not merely a footnote to the RL algorithm itself.",
      problemKn: "ನಿಜ human preference labels ಪ್ರಮಾಣದಲ್ಲಿ LIMITED ಮತ್ತು NOISY ಎರಡೂ ಆಗಿವೆ. ಹಿಂದಿನ exercise ya ನಿಮ್ಮ synthetic setup ಬಳಸಿ ಎರಡೂ ಅಂಶಗಳನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ತನಿಖೆ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n1) ಹಲವಾರು ಭಿನ್ನ ಪ್ರಮಾಣದ preference training data ಜೊತೆ ನಿಮ್ಮ reward model ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) ನಿಮ್ಮ preference data ಗೆ ಕೃತಕ LABEL NOISE ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಚುಚ್ಚಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸಂಯೋಜಿತ ಶೋಧನೆಗಳನ್ನೂ data-quantity-vs-noise-tolerance tradeoff ಮೇಲೆ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m180, l180_2, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'Reward Hacking: Optimizing the Proxy Instead of the Real Goal',
      titleKn: 'Reward Hacking: ನಿಜ Goal ಬದಲಿಗೆ Proxy ಅನ್ನೂ Optimize ಮಾಡುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Since a learned reward model is only a PROXY for true human preferences (trained on a limited, imperfect sample of preference comparisons, as investigated in the previous lesson), an RL policy optimized aggressively against this proxy reward model can learn to exploit systematic flaws/blind-spots in the reward model rather than genuinely improving on the true underlying objective -- a phenomenon called 'reward hacking' or 'reward model overoptimization.'\n\n1) Explain concretely why this can happen even with a genuinely well-trained reward model: since the reward model is itself just a neural network trained on finite data, it will have SOME regions of the output space where its predictions are inaccurate/miscalibrated (e.g. an unusual style of output it saw few examples of during its own training) -- explain why an RL policy, in its search for reward-maximizing outputs, might specifically discover and exploit these inaccurate regions (finding an output the reward model rates highly due to its own error, even though a real human would NOT actually prefer that output), since the policy's optimization pressure is directed at maximizing the proxy score specifically, not at genuinely satisfying the true underlying preference the proxy was meant to approximate.\n\n2) Explain why this problem tends to get WORSE, not better, the more aggressively/for-longer the policy is optimized against the reward model -- why might a policy trained for a MODERATE amount against the reward model track true quality reasonably well, while a policy trained EXTENSIVELY against the same fixed reward model increasingly find and exploit the reward model's specific flaws, since extended optimization pressure specifically searches out and finds whatever weaknesses exist in the fixed proxy.",
      problemKn: "ಒಂದು ಕಲಿತ reward model ನಿಜ human preferences ಗೆ ಕೇವಲ ಒಂದು PROXY ಆಗಿರುವುದರಿಂದ, ಈ proxy reward model ಎದುರು ಆಕ್ರಮಣಕಾರಿಯಾಗಿ optimize ಮಾಡಲಾದ ಒಂದು RL policy ನಿಜ ಆಧಾರಿತ objective ಮೇಲೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಸುಧಾರಿಸುವ ಬದಲಿಗೆ reward model ನಲ್ಲಿನ ವ್ಯವಸ್ಥಿತ ದೋಷಗಳನ್ನೂ ಬಳಸಿಕೊಳ್ಳಲು ಕಲಿಯಬಹುದು -- 'reward hacking' ಎಂಬ ವಿದ್ಯಮಾನ.\n\n1) ಒಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಚೆನ್ನಾಗಿ-trained reward model ಜೊತೆಯೂ ಇದೂ ಏಕೆ ಸಂಭವಿಸಬಹುದು ಎಂದು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟವಾಗಿ ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಈ ಸಮಸ್ಯೆ policy ಅನ್ನೂ ಹೆಚ್ಚು ಆಕ್ರಮಣಕಾರಿಯಾಗಿ/ಹೆಚ್ಚು ಸಮಯ optimize ಮಾಡಿದಂತೆ ಏಕೆ ಕೆಟ್ಟದಾಗುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Reproducing Reward Hacking on Your Synthetic Preference Task',
      titleKn: 'ನಿಮ್ಮ Synthetic Preference Task ಮೇಲೆ Reward Hacking ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪುನರುತ್ಪಾದಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely reproduce reward hacking directly on your synthetic reward-model setup from the previous lesson, where you have a known TRUE quality function to check against.\n\n1) Using your trained reward model from the previous lesson (trained on a limited, finite amount of preference data, so it has genuine imperfections), genuinely set up a simple optimization procedure that searches for outputs MAXIMIZING the reward model's predicted score (e.g. gradient-based optimization directly on the output representation, or a simple random-search/hill-climbing procedure if your output space is discrete).\n\n2) Genuinely run this optimization for an INCREASING number of optimization steps (e.g. 10, 100, 1000 steps), and at each checkpoint, record BOTH the reward model's predicted score for the current best-found output AND the TRUE quality (using your known ground-truth quality function) of that same output.\n\n3) Genuinely plot proxy reward-model score against true quality as optimization progresses -- report whether the two initially track each other reasonably well (early optimization genuinely improves true quality) but eventually DIVERGE (the reward-model score keeps climbing while true quality plateaus or even declines), providing a direct, concrete, measured demonstration of reward hacking/overoptimization on your controllable synthetic setup, rather than only understanding it as an abstract concern from real-world RLHF research.",
      problemKn: "ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ ನಿಮ್ಮ synthetic reward-model setup ಮೇಲೆ ನೇರವಾಗಿ reward hacking ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪುನರುತ್ಪಾದಿಸಿ, ಇಲ್ಲಿ ನಿಮಗೆ ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಲು ಒಂದು ತಿಳಿದ TRUE quality function ಇದೆ.\n\n1) ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ ನಿಮ್ಮ trained reward model ಬಳಸಿ, reward model ya predicted score ಅನ್ನೂ ಗರಿಷ್ಠಗೊಳಿಸುವ outputs ಹುಡುಕುವ ಒಂದು ಸರಳ optimization procedure ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಸ್ಥಾಪಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಈ optimization ಅನ್ನೂ ಹೆಚ್ಚುತ್ತಿರುವ ಸಂಖ್ಯೆಯ ಹಂತಗಳಿಗೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ, ಪ್ರತಿ checkpoint ನಲ್ಲಿ BOTH reward model ya predicted score ಮತ್ತು TRUE quality ಎರಡನ್ನೂ ದಾಖಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) optimization ಪ್ರಗತಿಪರವಾಗಿ ಆದಂತೆ proxy reward-model score ಅನ್ನೂ true quality ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಪ್ಲಾಟ್ ಮಾಡಿ, ಅವು ಕೊನೆಗೊಳ್ಳಲು DIVERGE ಆಗುತ್ತವೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Testing Whether More Reward-Model Training Data Delays Reward Hacking',
      titleKn: 'ಹೆಚ್ಚು Reward-Model Training Data Reward Hacking ಅನ್ನೂ ವಿಳಂಬಗೊಳಿಸುತ್ತದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "If reward hacking occurs because the reward model has imperfections/blind-spots from limited training data (as suggested in the first exercise), then a BETTER-trained reward model (more preference data, as you investigated in the previous lesson) should have FEWER exploitable flaws, and therefore should delay or reduce reward hacking when optimized against. Genuinely test this connection.\n\n1) Genuinely train TWO reward models on your synthetic task: one on a SMALL amount of preference data (prone to more overfitting/blind-spots) and one on a substantially LARGER amount (from your data-scaling investigation in the previous lesson).\n\n2) Genuinely run your reward-hacking optimization procedure from the previous exercise against BOTH reward models separately, tracking proxy score versus true quality over optimization steps for each.\n\n3) Genuinely report whether the reward-hacking divergence (proxy score climbing while true quality plateaus/declines) happens LATER (requires more optimization steps) or is LESS SEVERE (smaller true-quality decline at a given proxy-score level) when optimizing against the better-trained (more-data) reward model, compared to the poorly-trained one -- report your actual measured comparison, and discuss why this genuine empirical finding (if it holds on your specific setup) supports the real-world RLHF practice of investing significant resources in reward model DATA QUALITY AND QUANTITY specifically as a mitigation for overoptimization/reward-hacking risk, connecting this practical lesson back to why reward modeling (this module's actual subject) deserves just as much careful attention as the RL algorithm (PPO, from the previous module) that ultimately optimizes against it.",
      problemKn: "reward hacking ಸಂಭವಿಸುವುದೂ reward model ya ಸೀಮಿತ training data ಇಂದ ಅಪೂರ್ಣತೆಗಳ ಕಾರಣದಿಂದ ಆಗಿದ್ದರೆ, ಒಂದು ಉತ್ತಮ-trained reward model ಕಡಿಮೆ ಬಳಸಿಕೊಳ್ಳಬಹುದಾದ ದೋಷಗಳನ್ನೂ ಹೊಂದಿರಬೇಕು, ಆದ್ದರಿಂದ reward hacking ಅನ್ನೂ ವಿಳಂಬಗೊಳಿಸಬೇಕು ಅಥವಾ ಕಡಿಮೆ ಮಾಡಬೇಕು. ಈ ಸಂಪರ್ಕವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ synthetic task ಮೇಲೆ ಎರಡು reward models ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ: ಒಂದು ಸಣ್ಣ ಪ್ರಮಾಣದ preference data ಮೇಲೆ, ಒಂದು ಗಣನೀಯವಾಗಿ ದೊಡ್ಡ ಪ್ರಮಾಣದ ಮೇಲೆ.\n\n2) ಹಿಂದಿನ exercise ya ನಿಮ್ಮ reward-hacking optimization procedure ಅನ್ನೂ BOTH reward models ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಪ್ರತ್ಯೇಕವಾಗಿ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ.\n\n3) reward-hacking divergence ಉತ್ತಮ-trained reward model ವಿರುದ್ಧ optimize ಮಾಡುವಾಗ ನಂತರ ಸಂಭವಿಸುತ್ತದೆಯೇ ಅಥವಾ ಕಡಿಮೆ ತೀವ್ರವಾಗಿದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m180, l180_3, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'KL Penalty: Keeping the Policy Close to a Trusted Reference',
      titleKn: 'KL Penalty: Policy ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಂದು ವಿಶ್ವಾಸಾರ್ಹ Reference ಗೆ ಹತ್ತಿರ ಇಡುವುದೂ',
      problem: "A standard mitigation for reward hacking (from the previous lesson) is adding a KL-DIVERGENCE PENALTY to the RL training objective: in addition to maximizing the learned reward model's score, the policy is ALSO penalized for drifting too far (in terms of KL divergence between action-probability distributions) from a trusted REFERENCE policy (typically the original, pre-RL-finetuned policy, e.g. the base language model before any RLHF training).\n\n1) Explain why staying close to a trusted reference policy helps mitigate reward hacking specifically -- since the reward model was likely trained on preference comparisons of outputs that look somewhat like what the reference policy would produce (a reasonably 'normal' output distribution), why might the reward model's predictions become increasingly UNRELIABLE for outputs that are very far from this distribution (the reward model extrapolating poorly to unusual, out-of-distribution outputs it never saw during its own training) -- and why does penalizing large KL divergence from the reference policy keep the optimized policy within a region where the reward model's judgments remain more trustworthy?\n\n2) Explain the resulting tradeoff this KL penalty introduces: a LARGER KL penalty coefficient keeps the policy safer/closer to the reference (less reward hacking risk) but also more CONSTRAINED (less able to genuinely improve on the reference policy's behavior even in legitimate, non-hacky ways) -- explain why this is fundamentally a genuine safety-versus-capability tradeoff, not a free win, requiring careful tuning rather than simply setting the penalty as high as possible.",
      problemKn: "ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ reward hacking ಗಾಗಿ ಒಂದು ಪ್ರಮಾಣಿತ ಪರಿಹಾರವೆಂದರೆ RL training objective ಗೆ ಒಂದು KL-DIVERGENCE PENALTY ಸೇರಿಸುವುದೂ: ಕಲಿತ reward model ya score ಗರಿಷ್ಠಗೊಳಿಸುವುದರ ಜೊತೆಗೆ, policy ಒಂದು ವಿಶ್ವಾಸಾರ್ಹ REFERENCE policy ಇಂದ ಬಹಳ ದೂರ ದೂರ ಹೋಗಲು ಸಹ ದಂಡಿಸಲ್ಪಡುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) ಒಂದು ವಿಶ್ವಾಸಾರ್ಹ reference policy ಗೆ ಹತ್ತಿರ ಉಳಿಯುವುದೂ reward hacking ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟವಾಗಿ ಹೇಗೆ ಪರಿಹರಿಸಲು ಸಹಾಯ ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಈ KL penalty ಪರಿಚಯಿಸುವ ಫಲಿತಾಂಶ tradeoff ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing a KL Penalty and Measuring Its Effect on Reward Hacking',
      titleKn: 'ಒಂದು KL Penalty ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ Reward Hacking ಮೇಲಿನ ಅದರ ಪರಿಣಾಮ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely add a KL penalty to your reward-hacking optimization setup from the previous lesson and test whether it mitigates the hacking you observed.\n\n1) Genuinely define a 'reference' output distribution for your synthetic task (e.g. the distribution your outputs came from before optimization began), and implement a KL-divergence-based penalty term that discourages your optimization procedure from moving too far from this reference distribution.\n\n2) Genuinely re-run your reward-hacking optimization from the previous lesson (maximizing your reward model's score) but now WITH this KL penalty subtracted from the objective, at a few different penalty strengths (e.g. small, moderate, large).\n\n3) Genuinely re-plot proxy reward-model score against TRUE quality over optimization steps for each penalty strength, comparing against your unpenalized baseline from the previous lesson -- report whether adding the KL penalty measurably delays or reduces the divergence between proxy score and true quality (evidence the penalty is mitigating reward hacking, as predicted), and report whether a stronger penalty comes at the cost of reaching a lower PEAK true quality overall (evidence of the safety-versus-capability tradeoff discussed in the previous exercise) -- report your genuine measured results across penalty strengths.",
      problemKn: "ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ ನಿಮ್ಮ reward-hacking optimization setup ಗೆ ಒಂದು KL penalty ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಸೇರಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಇದೂ ನೀವು ಗಮನಿಸಿದ hacking ಅನ್ನೂ ಪರಿಹರಿಸುತ್ತದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ synthetic task ಗಾಗಿ ಒಂದು 'reference' output distribution ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವ್ಯಾಖ್ಯಾನಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಒಂದು KL-divergence-based penalty term ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಕೆಲವು ಭಿನ್ನ penalty strengths ನಲ್ಲಿ ಈ KL penalty ಜೊತೆ ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ ನಿಮ್ಮ reward-hacking optimization ಅನ್ನೂ ಮರುಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಪ್ರತಿ penalty strength ಗೆ proxy score ಮತ್ತು TRUE quality ಅನ್ನೂ ಮರುಪ್ಲಾಟ್ ಮಾಡಿ, ನಿಮ್ಮ unpenalized baseline ಗೆ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Building a Complete Mini-RLHF Pipeline and Reflecting on This Phase\'s Full Progression',
      titleKn: 'ಒಂದು ಪೂರ್ಣ Mini-RLHF Pipeline ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ ಈ Phase ya ಪೂರ್ಣ ಪ್ರಗತಿಯ ಮೇಲೆ ಪ್ರತಿಬಿಂಬಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "As a capstone for this module and this entire phase, genuinely assemble a complete, working mini-RLHF pipeline connecting everything from dynamic programming through PPO through reward modeling.\n\n1) Genuinely combine: (a) your reward model trained on synthetic preferences from an earlier lesson, (b) a KL penalty term as implemented in the previous exercise, and (c) your PPO implementation from the previous module -- use PPO to optimize a policy (which could be as simple as your grid-world policy network, reinterpreted here to output a synthetic 'response' rather than a grid action, adapting your synthetic preference task's output space to fit your existing PPO machinery) against the KL-penalized reward-model objective.\n\n2) Genuinely train this complete pipeline and track BOTH the reward-model score AND, since you have access to it in this synthetic setup, the TRUE underlying quality over training -- report whether your complete pipeline (with the KL penalty as a hacking mitigation) achieves genuine improvement in true quality without the divergence/hacking pattern you saw in your unpenalized reward-hacking exercise.\n\n3) Genuinely write a short reflective synthesis connecting this capstone exercise back to the full arc of this phase: from hand-specified rewards and known-model dynamic programming, through model-free Monte Carlo and TD control, through function-approximation-based DQN, through policy-gradient and actor-critic methods, through PPO's stability improvements, to this module's culminating insight that even the REWARD FUNCTION itself can and often must be learned rather than hand-specified -- discuss, based on your own hands-on experience building every one of these pieces across this phase, what you found most surprising or most clarifying about how these techniques build on and address each other's limitations in sequence.",
      problemKn: "ಈ module ಮತ್ತು ಈ ಸಂಪೂರ್ಣ phase ಗಾಗಿ ಒಂದು capstone ಆಗಿ, dynamic programming ಇಂದ PPO ಇಂದ reward modeling ವರೆಗೆ ಎಲ್ಲವನ್ನೂ ಸಂಪರ್ಕಿಸುವ ಒಂದು ಪೂರ್ಣ, ಕೆಲಸ ಮಾಡುವ mini-RLHF pipeline ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜೋಡಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸಿ: (a) ಒಂದು ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ ನಿಮ್ಮ reward model, (b) ಹಿಂದಿನ exercise ya ಒಂದು KL penalty term, ಮತ್ತು (c) ಹಿಂದಿನ module ya ನಿಮ್ಮ PPO implementation.\n\n2) ಈ ಪೂರ್ಣ pipeline ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಫಲಿತಾಂಶಗಳನ್ನೂ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ಈ ಸಂಪೂರ್ಣ phase ya ಪೂರ್ಣ ಪ್ರಗತಿಗೆ ಸಂಪರ್ಕಿಸುವ ಒಂದು ಸಣ್ಣ ಪ್ರತಿಬಿಂಬಿತ synthesis ಬರೆಯಿರಿ." },
  ]),

  // ── Module 181: Multi-Agent RL ──
  ...set(m181, l181_1, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'Why Multi-Agent RL Breaks the Single-Agent Stationarity Assumption',
      titleKn: 'Multi-Agent RL Single-Agent Stationarity ಊಹೆಯನ್ನೂ ಏಕೆ ಮುರಿಯುತ್ತದೆ',
      problem: "Every algorithm covered so far in this phase assumed a SINGLE agent interacting with a fixed, stationary environment (the environment's response to a given state-action pair doesn't change over the course of training). In multi-agent RL, multiple agents learn SIMULTANEOUSLY, and each agent's own learning changes its behavior over time -- meaning from any ONE agent's perspective, the 'environment' (which now includes the other learning agents) is genuinely NON-STATIONARY, since other agents' policies keep changing as they too learn.\n\n1) Explain concretely why this non-stationarity breaks a key theoretical assumption underlying convergence guarantees for algorithms like Q-Learning (which assumes the environment's transition dynamics are fixed) -- if agent A's optimal policy against agent B's CURRENT policy might become suboptimal once agent B updates ITS policy in response to A's own changes, why does this create a genuinely different, harder learning problem than standard single-agent RL, where a well-designed algorithm has provable convergence guarantees precisely because the environment doesn't move the goalposts during learning?\n\n2) Explain why this problem is often described using the framing of a game rather than a simple environment -- give a concrete example of a simple two-player scenario (e.g. a variant of rock-paper-scissors, or a simple pursuit game) where the 'correct' action for one agent genuinely depends on what the OTHER agent is currently doing, and where neither agent can find a single fixed best policy independent of the other's behavior.",
      problemKn: "ಈ phase ನಲ್ಲಿ ಇಲ್ಲಿಯವರೆಗೆ ಕವರ್ ಮಾಡಿದ ಪ್ರತಿ algorithm ಒಂದು SINGLE agent ಒಂದು ಸ್ಥಿರ, stationary environment ಜೊತೆ ಸಂವಹಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ಊಹಿಸಿತು. Multi-agent RL ನಲ್ಲಿ, ಅನೇಕ agents ಏಕಕಾಲದಲ್ಲಿ ಕಲಿಯುತ್ತವೆ, ಮತ್ತು ಪ್ರತಿ agent ya ಸ್ವಂತ learning ಸಮಯದೊಂದಿಗೆ ಅದರ ವರ್ತನೆಯನ್ನೂ ಬದಲಾಯಿಸುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) ಇದೂ Q-Learning ಯಂತಹ algorithms ಗಾಗಿ ಆಧಾರಿತ ಒಂದು ಪ್ರಮುಖ ಸೈದ್ಧಾಂತಿಕ ಊಹೆಯನ್ನೂ ಏಕೆ ಮುರಿಯುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟವಾಗಿ ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಈ ಸಮಸ್ಯೆ ಸಾಮಾನ್ಯವಾಗಿ ಒಂದು ಸರಳ environment ಗಿಂತ ಒಂದು game ya framing ಬಳಸಿ ಏಕೆ ವಿವರಿಸಲಾಗಿದೆ ಎಂದು ಒಂದು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ ಉದಾಹರಣೆಯೊಂದಿಗೆ ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Building a Two-Agent Grid-World and Observing Non-Stationarity',
      titleKn: 'ಒಂದು Two-Agent Grid-World ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ Non-Stationarity ಗಮನಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely extend your grid-world MDP from earlier modules in this phase into a two-agent version, and directly observe the non-stationarity problem.\n\n1) Genuinely build a simple two-agent grid-world (e.g. a pursuit scenario: one 'chaser' agent trying to catch a 'runner' agent, both learning simultaneously via independent Q-Learning, each treating the other as part of the environment).\n\n2) Genuinely train BOTH agents simultaneously using independent Q-Learning (each agent maintains its own Q-table, updating based on its own observed rewards, with no explicit coordination or awareness that the other agent is also learning).\n\n3) Genuinely track and plot each agent's Q-values for a SPECIFIC state-action pair over the course of training -- report whether these Q-value estimates show signs of genuine non-stationarity (e.g. oscillating or repeatedly changing direction as the other agent's policy shifts, rather than smoothly converging to a fixed value the way your single-agent Q-Learning did in an earlier module) -- report your actual observed pattern, providing a direct empirical demonstration of the non-stationarity concern discussed conceptually in the previous exercise, on a problem genuinely simple enough to inspect closely.",
      problemKn: "ಈ phase ya ಹಿಂದಿನ modules ya ನಿಮ್ಮ grid-world MDP ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಂದು two-agent ಆವೃತ್ತಿಗೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿಸ್ತರಿಸಿ, ಮತ್ತು non-stationarity ಸಮಸ್ಯೆಯನ್ನೂ ನೇರವಾಗಿ ಗಮನಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಒಂದು ಸರಳ two-agent grid-world ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ.\n\n2) BOTH agents ಅನ್ನೂ ಏಕಕಾಲದಲ್ಲಿ independent Q-Learning ಬಳಸಿ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) training ಅವಧಿಯಲ್ಲಿ ಪ್ರತಿ agent ya ಒಂದು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ state-action pair ಗಾಗಿ Q-values ಅನ್ನೂ ಟ್ರ್ಯಾಕ್ ಮಾಡಿ ಪ್ಲಾಟ್ ಮಾಡಿ, ಇವು ನಿಜ non-stationarity ya ಚಿಹ್ನೆಗಳನ್ನೂ ತೋರಿಸುತ್ತವೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Comparing Independent Learning to Self-Play in a Cooperative versus Competitive Setting',
      titleKn: 'Cooperative vs Competitive Setting ನಲ್ಲಿ Independent Learning ಅನ್ನೂ Self-Play ಗೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಹೋಲಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely investigate whether the severity of non-stationarity differs between COOPERATIVE and COMPETITIVE multi-agent settings, using your two-agent grid-world infrastructure from the previous exercise.\n\n1) Genuinely construct TWO variants of your two-agent scenario: (a) a COMPETITIVE version (like your original chaser-runner pursuit, where the agents' interests are directly opposed), and (b) a COOPERATIVE version (e.g. both agents must reach a shared goal together, or each benefits when the other succeeds, so their interests are aligned rather than opposed).\n\n2) Genuinely train independent Q-Learning agents on BOTH variants, and measure the same non-stationarity indicator from the previous exercise (Q-value oscillation/instability over training) for both.\n\n3) Genuinely report whether the competitive setting shows MORE severe non-stationarity symptoms (more oscillation, slower/less reliable convergence) than the cooperative setting -- report your actual measured comparison, and discuss a plausible explanation for any difference you observe: in a competitive/adversarial setting, one agent's improvement is often specifically the OTHER agent's loss (creating a more actively-shifting, adversarial pressure on each agent's optimal policy), while in a cooperative setting, both agents' improvements can be more mutually reinforcing (each agent's improved behavior tends to make the OTHER agent's job easier too, potentially leading to more stable, co-improving dynamics) -- ground this discussion in your genuine measured results rather than assuming this pattern holds without checking.",
      problemKn: "ಹಿಂದಿನ exercise ya ನಿಮ್ಮ two-agent grid-world infrastructure ಬಳಸಿ, COOPERATIVE ಮತ್ತು COMPETITIVE multi-agent settings ನಡುವೆ non-stationarity ya ತೀವ್ರತೆ ಭಿನ್ನವಾಗಿದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ತನಿಖೆ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ two-agent ಸನ್ನಿವೇಶದ ಎರಡು variants ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ರಚಿಸಿ: (a) ಒಂದು COMPETITIVE ಆವೃತ್ತಿ, (b) ಒಂದು COOPERATIVE ಆವೃತ್ತಿ.\n\n2) BOTH variants ಮೇಲೆ independent Q-Learning agents ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ, ಎರಡಕ್ಕೂ ಅದೇ non-stationarity indicator ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n3) competitive setting ಹೆಚ್ಚು ತೀವ್ರ non-stationarity ರೋಗಲಕ್ಷಣಗಳನ್ನೂ ತೋರಿಸುತ್ತದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m181, l181_2, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'Centralized Training with Decentralized Execution',
      titleKn: 'Decentralized Execution ಜೊತೆ Centralized Training',
      problem: "A common paradigm for mitigating multi-agent non-stationarity is Centralized Training with Decentralized Execution (CTDE): DURING TRAINING, each agent's critic (value function) is given access to the FULL joint state/actions of ALL agents (making the training-time value estimation problem stationary again, since the critic can see everything relevant), but at DEPLOYMENT/EXECUTION time, each agent's actor (policy) only uses its OWN local observations to decide actions (since in most real multi-agent settings, an agent genuinely can't observe everything about every other agent at execution time).\n\n1) Explain why giving the CRITIC full joint information during training directly addresses the non-stationarity problem from the previous lesson -- if the critic's input includes what all agents are doing, why does this make the value-estimation problem stationary again (the critic's job is now to predict value given the FULL joint action, which is a well-defined, non-moving target, rather than trying to predict value assuming some fixed but actually-changing behavior from other agents)?\n\n2) Explain why the actor must remain DECENTRALIZED despite the critic being centralized -- why would a policy that requires observing all other agents' full state at execution time often be impractical or impossible in real multi-agent deployments (e.g. robots that can only observe their local sensors, or players in a game who genuinely don't have access to opponents' internal state), making the CTDE split (centralized only during the training-time critic, decentralized for the actually-deployed actor) a practical compromise between addressing non-stationarity and remaining realistically deployable.",
      problemKn: "multi-agent non-stationarity ಪರಿಹರಿಸಲು ಒಂದು ಸಾಮಾನ್ಯ paradigm ಎಂದರೆ Centralized Training with Decentralized Execution (CTDE): TRAINING ಸಮಯದಲ್ಲಿ, ಪ್ರತಿ agent ya critic ALL agents ya ಪೂರ್ಣ joint state/actions ಗೆ ಪ್ರವೇಶ ಪಡೆಯುತ್ತದೆ, ಆದರೆ EXECUTION ಸಮಯದಲ್ಲಿ, ಪ್ರತಿ agent ya actor ಕೇವಲ ಅದರ ಸ್ವಂತ local observations ಬಳಸಿ actions ನಿರ್ಧರಿಸುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) training ಸಮಯದಲ್ಲಿ CRITIC ಗೆ ಪೂರ್ಣ joint ಮಾಹಿತಿ ನೀಡುವುದೂ ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ non-stationarity ಸಮಸ್ಯೆಯನ್ನೂ ನೇರವಾಗಿ ಹೇಗೆ ಪರಿಹರಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) critic centralized ಆಗಿದ್ದರೂ actor ಏಕೆ DECENTRALIZED ಆಗಿಯೇ ಉಳಿಯಬೇಕು ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing a Centralized Critic for Your Two-Agent Grid-World',
      titleKn: 'ನಿಮ್ಮ Two-Agent Grid-World ಗಾಗಿ ಒಂದು Centralized Critic ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Implement a CTDE-style Actor-Critic setup for your two-agent grid-world from the previous lesson.\n\n1) Genuinely build a centralized critic: a value network that takes the FULL joint state (both agents' positions) AND the full joint action (both agents' chosen actions) as input, trained to predict the joint value/return -- this differs from your earlier independent-Q-Learning agents, which each only saw their own local state.\n\n2) Genuinely build decentralized actors: each agent still has its own policy network that only takes ITS OWN local observation as input (no access to the other agent's state at execution time), but is trained using advantage estimates computed from the CENTRALIZED critic (which does see everything during training).\n\n3) Genuinely train this CTDE setup on your two-agent grid-world and compare its Q-value/advantage-estimate stability (reusing your non-stationarity measurement methodology from the previous lesson) against your original independent-Q-Learning baseline -- report whether the centralized-critic approach shows measurably LESS oscillation/instability in its value estimates than independent learning did, providing direct empirical evidence for the stabilization benefit discussed conceptually in the previous exercise.",
      problemKn: "ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ ನಿಮ್ಮ two-agent grid-world ಗಾಗಿ ಒಂದು CTDE-style Actor-Critic setup ಅನ್ನೂ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಒಂದು centralized critic ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ.\n\n2) decentralized actors ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಈ CTDE setup ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ two-agent grid-world ಮೇಲೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಫಲಿತಾಂಶ ಸ್ಥಿರತೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ ಮೂಲ independent-Q-Learning baseline ಗೆ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Measuring Whether CTDE Improves Final Joint Performance, Not Just Stability',
      titleKn: 'CTDE ಕೇವಲ Stability ಮಾತ್ರವಲ್ಲ, Final Joint Performance ಸಹ ಸುಧಾರಿಸುತ್ತದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely investigate whether the training stability improvement from CTDE (measured in the previous exercise) translates into genuinely better FINAL task performance, not just smoother training curves.\n\n1) Genuinely train your CTDE agents and your independent-Q-Learning agents from the previous lesson to full convergence (or a fixed training budget) on BOTH your cooperative and competitive two-agent scenarios from an earlier lesson's exercise.\n\n2) Genuinely measure final task performance for both approaches on both scenarios: for the cooperative scenario, measure joint/shared reward achieved; for the competitive scenario, measure something like win rate or relative advantage between the two agents.\n\n3) Genuinely report whether CTDE achieves measurably BETTER final performance than independent learning on either or both scenario types, or whether the two approaches converge to comparable final performance despite CTDE's smoother training dynamics -- report your genuine measured comparison, and discuss what this reveals about the distinction between TRAINING STABILITY (which CTDE clearly helps with, per your earlier measurement) and ULTIMATE SOLUTION QUALITY (which may or may not differ) -- these are related but genuinely separate questions, and conflating 'trains more smoothly' with 'reaches a better final answer' would be an unwarranted assumption without the kind of direct measurement you've just performed.",
      problemKn: "ಹಿಂದಿನ exercise ya CTDE ಇಂದ training stability ಸುಧಾರಣೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಉತ್ತಮ FINAL task performance ಗೆ ಭಾಷಾಂತರಗೊಳ್ಳುತ್ತದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ತನಿಖೆ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ CTDE agents ಮತ್ತು independent-Q-Learning agents ಎರಡನ್ನೂ ಪೂರ್ಣ convergence ವರೆಗೆ ನಿಮ್ಮ cooperative ಮತ್ತು competitive ಸನ್ನಿವೇಶಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) ಎರಡೂ ವಿಧಾನಗಳಿಗೂ final task performance ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n3) CTDE ಅಳೆಯಬಹುದಾಗಿ ಉತ್ತಮ final performance ಸಾಧಿಸುತ್ತದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m181, l181_3, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'Self-Play: Training an Agent Against Itself',
      titleKn: 'Self-Play: ಒಂದು Agent ಅನ್ನೂ ಅದರ ವಿರುದ್ಧವೇ Train ಮಾಡುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Self-play trains a SINGLE agent by having it play against copies of ITSELF (or recent past versions of itself), rather than against a fixed opponent or a separately-learning second agent -- a technique famously used in game-playing systems.\n\n1) Explain a genuine advantage self-play has for competitive games: since the agent's opponent (itself) improves exactly as fast as the agent does, why does this create a naturally well-matched, appropriately-challenging opponent at every stage of training -- explain why training against a FIXED, unchanging opponent (e.g. a simple hand-coded bot) would eventually plateau (the agent surpasses the fixed opponent's skill level and has nothing left to learn from beating it easily), while self-play's opponent difficulty automatically scales with the agent's own improving skill.\n\n2) Explain a genuine risk specific to self-play: since the agent only ever trains against itself, it might develop strategies that are specifically effective against ITS OWN playing style/blind-spots but that would fail badly against a genuinely different opponent with different strategies (a form of overfitting to its own self-play history) -- explain why this motivates techniques like training against a POOL of past self-play versions (rather than only the single most-recent version) to maintain more diverse practice and reduce this narrow-overfitting risk.",
      problemKn: "Self-play ಒಂದೇ agent ಅನ್ನೂ ಅದರ ಸ್ವಂತ ಪ್ರತಿಗಳ (ಅಥವಾ ಇತ್ತೀಚಿನ ಹಿಂದಿನ ಆವೃತ್ತಿಗಳ) ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಆಡುವಂತೆ ಮಾಡುವ ಮೂಲಕ train ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ, ಒಂದು ಸ್ಥಿರ opponent ಅಥವಾ ಒಂದು ಪ್ರತ್ಯೇಕವಾಗಿ-ಕಲಿಯುವ ಎರಡನೇ agent ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಅಲ್ಲ.\n\n1) competitive games ಗಾಗಿ self-play ya ಒಂದು ನಿಜ ಅನುಕೂಲ ವಿವರಿಸಿ: agent ya opponent (ಅದು ಸ್ವತಃ) agent ಅಷ್ಟೇ ವೇಗವಾಗಿ ಸುಧಾರಿಸುತ್ತದೆ, ಇದೂ training ya ಪ್ರತಿ ಹಂತದಲ್ಲಿ ಒಂದು ಸ್ವಾಭಾವಿಕವಾಗಿ ಚೆನ್ನಾಗಿ-ಹೊಂದಾಣಿಕೆಯಾಗುವ, ಸೂಕ್ತವಾಗಿ-ಸವಾಲಿನ opponent ಅನ್ನೂ ಏಕೆ ಸೃಷ್ಟಿಸುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n2) self-play ಗೆ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟವಾದ ಒಂದು ನಿಜ ಅಪಾಯ ವಿವರಿಸಿ: agent ಕೇವಲ ಅದರ ಸ್ವಂತ ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಮಾತ್ರ train ಆಗುವುದರಿಂದ, ಇದೂ ತನ್ನದೇ playing style ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಪರಿಣಾಮಕಾರಿ ಆದರೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಭಿನ್ನ opponent ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಕೆಟ್ಟದಾಗಿ ವಿಫಲಗೊಳ್ಳುವ strategies ಅಭಿವೃದ್ಧಿಪಡಿಸಬಹುದು." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing Self-Play and Measuring Skill Progression',
      titleKn: 'Self-Play ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ Skill Progression ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Implement self-play training on your competitive two-agent grid-world from an earlier lesson in this module.\n\n1) Genuinely train a single policy using self-play: at each training round, have the current policy play against a copy of itself, updating the SAME shared policy based on the outcomes (rather than maintaining two genuinely separate agents as in your earlier independent-learning exercises).\n\n2) Genuinely measure skill progression over training by periodically evaluating the current policy against a FIXED reference opponent (e.g. a simple random policy, or an early-training snapshot of the self-play policy itself, kept frozen as a fixed benchmark) -- report whether win rate against this fixed reference genuinely improves over the course of self-play training, confirming the agent is actually getting better rather than just oscillating against its own moving-target self.\n\n3) Genuinely compare this self-play agent's final skill level against your independent-Q-Learning competitive agents from an earlier lesson (evaluate both against the same fixed reference opponent) -- report which approach produces a more skilled final agent on your specific small-scale competitive scenario, and discuss your genuine findings.",
      problemKn: "ಈ module ya ಒಂದು ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ ನಿಮ್ಮ competitive two-agent grid-world ಮೇಲೆ self-play training ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) self-play ಬಳಸಿ ಒಂದೇ policy ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) ಒಂದು ಸ್ಥಿರ reference opponent ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಪ್ರಸ್ತುತ policy ಅನ್ನೂ ಆವರ್ತಕವಾಗಿ ಮೌಲ್ಯಮಾಪನ ಮಾಡುವ ಮೂಲಕ training ಅವಧಿಯಲ್ಲಿ skill progression ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n3) ಈ self-play agent ya ಅಂತಿಮ skill level ಅನ್ನೂ ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ ನಿಮ್ಮ independent-Q-Learning competitive agents ಗೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Testing Whether a Pool of Past Opponents Reduces Self-Play Overfitting',
      titleKn: 'ಹಿಂದಿನ Opponents ya ಒಂದು Pool Self-Play Overfitting ಕಡಿಮೆ ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely test the narrow-overfitting risk and its proposed mitigation from the first exercise of this lesson.\n\n1) Genuinely train your self-play agent from the previous exercise, but periodically SAVE snapshots of the policy at different points during training (e.g. every 100 rounds), building up a POOL of past versions.\n\n2) Genuinely implement a modified self-play scheme: instead of always playing against the MOST RECENT self-copy, have the agent play against a RANDOMLY SAMPLED past version from the pool at each training round.\n\n3) Genuinely compare the FINAL skill level of your pool-based self-play agent against your most-recent-only self-play agent from the previous exercise, evaluating both against a diverse set of test opponents (e.g. several different fixed policies, or several different snapshots from an INDEPENDENTLY trained agent, not drawn from either self-play run's own history, to genuinely test generalization rather than just testing against opponents similar to their own training) -- report whether the pool-based approach shows more ROBUST performance across this diverse opponent set (evidence it avoided narrow-overfitting to one specific playing style) compared to the most-recent-only approach, which might perform excellently against opponents similar to its own final self-play style but poorly against genuinely different strategies -- report your genuine measured comparison, providing concrete empirical evidence for whether this specific mitigation technique delivers on its intended purpose on your particular small-scale reproduction.",
      problemKn: "ಈ ಪಾಠದ ಮೊದಲ exercise ya narrow-overfitting ಅಪಾಯ ಮತ್ತು ಅದರ ಪ್ರಸ್ತಾಪಿತ ಪರಿಹಾರವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಹಿಂದಿನ exercise ya ನಿಮ್ಮ self-play agent ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ, ಆದರೆ training ಸಮಯದಲ್ಲಿ ಭಿನ್ನ ಬಿಂದುಗಳಲ್ಲಿ policy ya snapshots ಅನ್ನೂ ಆವರ್ತಕವಾಗಿ SAVE ಮಾಡಿ, ಹಿಂದಿನ ಆವೃತ್ತಿಗಳ ಒಂದು POOL ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಯಾವಾಗಲೂ ಅತ್ಯಂತ ಇತ್ತೀಚಿನ ಸ್ವಂತ ಪ್ರತಿ ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಆಡುವ ಬದಲಿಗೆ, ಪ್ರತಿ training round ನಲ್ಲಿ pool ಇಂದ ಒಂದು ಯಾದೃಚ್ಛಿಕವಾಗಿ sampled ಹಿಂದಿನ ಆವೃತ್ತಿ ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಆಡುವ ಒಂದು ಮಾರ್ಪಡಿಸಿದ self-play scheme ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ pool-based self-play agent ya ಅಂತಿಮ skill level ಅನ್ನೂ ಹಿಂದಿನ exercise ya ನಿಮ್ಮ most-recent-only self-play agent ಗೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ, ಒಂದು ವೈವಿಧ್ಯಮಯ ಸೆಟ್ test opponents ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಮೌಲ್ಯಮಾಪನ ಮಾಡುತ್ತಾ." },
  ]),

  // ── Module 182: Sim-to-Real Transfer ──
  ...set(m182, l182_1, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'The Reality Gap: Why a Simulator-Trained Policy Can Fail in the Real World',
      titleKn: 'Reality Gap: ಒಂದು Simulator-Trained Policy ನಿಜ ಜಗತ್ತಿನಲ್ಲಿ ಏಕೆ ವಿಫಲಗೊಳ್ಳಬಹುದು',
      problem: "Every RL algorithm in this phase has been trained and tested in a SIMULATED environment (your grid-world). Real-world deployment (e.g. an actual robot) introduces the 'reality gap': a policy trained in simulation may perform much worse, or fail entirely, when deployed in the real world, because the simulator is inevitably an IMPERFECT model of real-world physics/dynamics.\n\n1) Explain concretely why an RL policy trained via extensive optimization AGAINST a specific simulator can end up implicitly exploiting quirks/inaccuracies specific to that simulator's physics model (similar in spirit to the reward-hacking phenomenon from an earlier module, but here the 'proxy' being exploited is the simulator's imperfect dynamics model rather than a reward model) -- give a concrete example of a plausible simulator inaccuracy (e.g. slightly wrong friction coefficients, unrealistic sensor noise characteristics, or missing real-world effects like motor backlash) that a policy might learn to rely on in ways that don't transfer to real hardware.\n\n2) Explain why this reality-gap problem tends to get WORSE, not better, with MORE extensive training/optimization against the simulator -- similar to how longer reward-model optimization increased reward-hacking severity in an earlier module, why might a policy trained to squeeze out every last bit of simulated performance become increasingly specialized to (and dependent on) that specific simulator's exact quirks, at the expense of the more ROBUST, generalizable behavior that would actually transfer well to slightly-different real-world dynamics.",
      problemKn: "ಈ phase ya ಪ್ರತಿ RL algorithm ಒಂದು SIMULATED environment ನಲ್ಲಿ trained ಮತ್ತು ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸಲ್ಪಟ್ಟಿದೆ. ನಿಜ-ಜಗತ್ತಿನ deployment 'reality gap' ಪರಿಚಯಿಸುತ್ತದೆ: simulation ನಲ್ಲಿ trained ಒಂದು policy ನಿಜ ಜಗತ್ತಿನಲ್ಲಿ deploy ಮಾಡಿದಾಗ ಬಹಳ ಕೆಟ್ಟದಾಗಿ ಕಾರ್ಯನಿರ್ವಹಿಸಬಹುದು, ಏಕೆಂದರೆ simulator ಅನಿವಾರ್ಯವಾಗಿ ನಿಜ-ಜಗತ್ತಿನ physics/dynamics ya ಒಂದು ಅಪೂರ್ಣ ಮಾದರಿಯಾಗಿದೆ.\n\n1) ಒಂದು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ simulator ವಿರುದ್ಧ ವ್ಯಾಪಕ optimization ಮೂಲಕ trained ಒಂದು RL policy ಆ simulator ya physics model ಗೆ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟವಾದ quirks/ಅಸಮರ್ಪಕತೆಗಳನ್ನೂ ಪರೋಕ್ಷವಾಗಿ ಬಳಸಿಕೊಳ್ಳುವುದೂ ಏಕೆ ಸಂಭವಿಸಬಹುದು ಎಂದು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟವಾಗಿ ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಈ reality-gap ಸಮಸ್ಯೆ simulator ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಹೆಚ್ಚು ವ್ಯಾಪಕ training/optimization ಜೊತೆ ಏಕೆ ಕೆಟ್ಟದಾಗುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Creating a Simulator Mismatch and Measuring Transfer Failure',
      titleKn: 'ಒಂದು Simulator Mismatch ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ರಚಿಸಿ Transfer Failure ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely simulate the reality gap using two deliberately-different versions of your grid-world MDP: a 'simulator' version and a slightly different 'real world' version.\n\n1) Genuinely construct your original grid-world as the 'simulator,' and create a second, slightly-modified version as the 'real world' (e.g. change the transition stochasticity slightly, such as the probability of successfully moving in the intended direction, or add a small systematic bias to one action's effect).\n\n2) Genuinely train a policy (using your best-performing algorithm from earlier modules, e.g. PPO or DQN) EXTENSIVELY on the 'simulator' version until it reaches strong performance there.\n\n3) Genuinely evaluate this simulator-trained policy DIRECTLY on the 'real world' version WITHOUT any further training -- report the actual measured performance drop (comparing its return on the simulator version versus the real-world version), providing a direct, concrete, measured demonstration of the reality gap on your own controllable setup, and genuinely test whether a policy trained for FEWER simulator steps (less extensively optimized) transfers better or worse than your extensively-trained one, connecting to the previous exercise's claim about extensive optimization worsening the reality gap.",
      problemKn: "ನಿಮ್ಮ grid-world MDP ya ಎರಡು ಉದ್ದೇಶಪೂರ್ವಕವಾಗಿ-ಭಿನ್ನ ಆವೃತ್ತಿಗಳನ್ನೂ ಬಳಸಿ reality gap ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಸಿಮ್ಯುಲೇಟ್ ಮಾಡಿ: ಒಂದು 'simulator' ಆವೃತ್ತಿ ಮತ್ತು ಸ್ವಲ್ಪ ಭಿನ್ನ 'real world' ಆವೃತ್ತಿ.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಮೂಲ grid-world ಅನ್ನೂ 'simulator' ಆಗಿ ಮತ್ತು ಒಂದು ಎರಡನೇ, ಸ್ವಲ್ಪ-ಮಾರ್ಪಡಿಸಿದ ಆವೃತ್ತಿಯನ್ನೂ 'real world' ಆಗಿ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ರಚಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಒಂದು policy ಅನ್ನೂ 'simulator' ಆವೃತ್ತಿ ಮೇಲೆ ವ್ಯಾಪಕವಾಗಿ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ಈ simulator-trained policy ಅನ್ನೂ 'real world' ಆವೃತ್ತಿ ಮೇಲೆ ಯಾವುದೇ ಹೆಚ್ಚಿನ training ಇಲ್ಲದೆ ನೇರವಾಗಿ ಮೌಲ್ಯಮಾಪನ ಮಾಡಿ, ನಿಜ ಅಳೆದ performance drop ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Measuring How the Size of the Simulator-Reality Gap Affects Transfer Failure',
      titleKn: 'Simulator-Reality Gap ya ಗಾತ್ರ Transfer Failure ಮೇಲೆ ಹೇಗೆ ಪರಿಣಾಮ ಬೀರುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely characterize how transfer failure scales with the MAGNITUDE of the simulator-reality mismatch, extending your investigation from the previous exercise.\n\n1) Genuinely construct several 'real world' variants with INCREASING degrees of deviation from your original simulator (e.g. small, medium, and large changes to the transition stochasticity or action-effect bias).\n\n2) Genuinely evaluate your simulator-trained policy from the previous exercise on ALL these real-world variants, without any additional training on any of them.\n\n3) Genuinely plot/report transfer performance (return achieved) as a function of mismatch magnitude -- report whether performance degrades smoothly/gradually as mismatch increases, or whether there's a sharper threshold beyond which transfer performance collapses -- report your actual measured pattern, and discuss the practical implication: this measured relationship between simulator-reality mismatch magnitude and transfer failure severity is exactly the kind of empirical characterization real robotics teams need to understand BEFORE deploying a simulator-trained policy on expensive, potentially fragile real hardware, and it directly motivates the domain-randomization and other sim-to-real techniques covered in the remaining lessons of this module, which aim to make policies robust across a RANGE of possible dynamics rather than narrowly optimized for one specific (and inevitably imperfect) simulator.",
      problemKn: "simulator-reality mismatch ya MAGNITUDE ಜೊತೆ transfer failure ಹೇಗೆ scale ಆಗುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿವರಿಸಿ, ಹಿಂದಿನ exercise ya ನಿಮ್ಮ ತನಿಖೆಯನ್ನೂ ವಿಸ್ತರಿಸುತ್ತಾ.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಮೂಲ simulator ಇಂದ ಹೆಚ್ಚುತ್ತಿರುವ ಪ್ರಮಾಣದ ವಿಚಲನೆ ಇರುವ ಹಲವಾರು 'real world' variants ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ರಚಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಹಿಂದಿನ exercise ya ನಿಮ್ಮ simulator-trained policy ಅನ್ನೂ ಈ ಎಲ್ಲಾ real-world variants ಮೇಲೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಮೌಲ್ಯಮಾಪನ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) mismatch magnitude ya ಒಂದು ಫಂಕ್ಷನ್ ಆಗಿ transfer performance ಅನ್ನೂ ಪ್ಲಾಟ್ ಮಾಡಿ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m182, l182_2, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'Domain Randomization: Training on Many Simulators at Once',
      titleKn: 'Domain Randomization: ಒಂದೇ ಬಾರಿಗೆ ಅನೇಕ Simulators ಮೇಲೆ Train ಮಾಡುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Domain Randomization addresses the reality gap (from the previous lesson) by training the policy across MANY RANDOMIZED VARIATIONS of the simulator's dynamics parameters (e.g. randomizing friction, mass, sensor noise across a wide range on every training episode) rather than training on one single, fixed simulator configuration.\n\n1) Explain the core intuition: if a policy is trained to perform well across a WIDE RANGE of simulated dynamics (not knowing in advance which specific variant it will face each episode), why might it be forced to learn a more ROBUST, generally-applicable strategy that doesn't rely on any one simulator's exact specific quirks -- and why might the genuine real world, with its own specific (and somewhat unknown) dynamics, be more likely to fall WITHIN the range of variation the policy was trained to handle, compared to training on just one fixed simulator configuration that might differ from reality in an unanticipated direction?\n\n2) Explain a genuine tradeoff domain randomization introduces: training across a wide range of dynamics variations is a strictly HARDER learning problem than training on one fixed configuration (the policy must find a strategy that works reasonably well across ALL the randomized variants, rather than being allowed to specialize perfectly for just one) -- explain why this might mean a domain-randomized policy achieves somewhat LOWER peak performance on any single specific configuration (including the true real-world dynamics, if you could test against it directly) compared to a policy trained and specialized for exactly that one configuration, even though the domain-randomized policy is expected to be more ROBUST across genuinely unknown/varying real-world conditions.",
      problemKn: "Domain Randomization ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ reality gap ಅನ್ನೂ simulator ya dynamics parameters ya ಅನೇಕ RANDOMIZED VARIATIONS ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ policy ಅನ್ನೂ train ಮಾಡುವ ಮೂಲಕ ಪರಿಹರಿಸುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) ಪ್ರಮುಖ ಅಂತಃಪ್ರಜ್ಞೆಯನ್ನೂ ವಿವರಿಸಿ: ಒಂದು policy ಅನ್ನೂ simulated dynamics ya ಒಂದು ವಿಶಾಲ ಶ್ರೇಣಿಯಾದ್ಯಂತ ಚೆನ್ನಾಗಿ ಕಾರ್ಯನಿರ್ವಹಿಸಲು train ಮಾಡಿದರೆ, ಇದೂ ಒಂದು simulator ya ನಿಖರ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ quirks ಮೇಲೆ ಅವಲಂಬಿಸದ ಒಂದು ಹೆಚ್ಚು robust ತಂತ್ರ ಕಲಿಯಲು ಏಕೆ ಒತ್ತಾಯಿಸಬಹುದು ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) domain randomization ಪರಿಚಯಿಸುವ ಒಂದು ನಿಜ tradeoff ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing Domain Randomization and Measuring Transfer Improvement',
      titleKn: 'Domain Randomization ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ Transfer ಸುಧಾರಣೆ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely implement domain randomization on your grid-world setup from the previous lesson and test whether it improves transfer to your 'real world' variant.\n\n1) Genuinely modify your training loop to RANDOMIZE the transition stochasticity/action-bias parameters (the same parameters you varied to create your 'real world' variants in the previous lesson) at the START of every training episode, sampling from a wide range, rather than training on one fixed simulator configuration.\n\n2) Genuinely train a policy with this domain-randomized training procedure (using the same total training budget as your original single-configuration policy from the previous lesson, for a fair comparison).\n\n3) Genuinely evaluate this domain-randomized policy on ALL your 'real world' variants from the previous lesson's exercise (small, medium, large mismatch) WITHOUT any additional training on them -- report the actual measured transfer performance at each mismatch level, and compare against your original single-simulator-trained policy's transfer performance from the previous lesson -- report whether domain randomization measurably improves transfer robustness (smaller performance drop across the mismatch range), and also report the domain-randomized policy's performance ON THE ORIGINAL SIMULATOR configuration itself, checking whether it shows the peak-performance cost predicted conceptually in the previous exercise.",
      problemKn: "ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ ನಿಮ್ಮ grid-world setup ಮೇಲೆ domain randomization ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಇದೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ 'real world' variant ಗೆ transfer ಸುಧಾರಿಸುತ್ತದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಪ್ರತಿ training episode ya ಆರಂಭದಲ್ಲಿ transition stochasticity/action-bias parameters ಅನ್ನೂ RANDOMIZE ಮಾಡಲು ನಿಮ್ಮ training loop ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಮಾರ್ಪಡಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಈ domain-randomized training procedure ಜೊತೆ ಒಂದು policy ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ಈ policy ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ ಎಲ್ಲಾ 'real world' variants ಮೇಲೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಮೌಲ್ಯಮಾಪನ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಫಲಿತಾಂಶಗಳನ್ನೂ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Sweeping Randomization Range to Find the Robustness-Performance Sweet Spot',
      titleKn: 'Robustness-Performance Sweet Spot ಕಂಡುಹಿಡಿಯಲು Randomization Range ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ Sweep ಮಾಡುವುದೂ',
      problem: "The WIDTH of the randomization range (how much variation to include during domain-randomized training) is itself a tunable hyperparameter with its own tradeoff. Genuinely investigate this.\n\n1) Genuinely train domain-randomized policies at several different randomization range widths (e.g. a narrow range, a moderate range, and a very wide range of dynamics variation).\n\n2) Genuinely evaluate all these policies across your full set of 'real world' variants (small, medium, large mismatch) from earlier exercises, AND on the original simulator configuration -- compile a full comparison table.\n\n3) Genuinely report whether a narrow randomization range gives good peak performance but poor robustness to larger mismatches (behaving more like your original non-randomized policy), while a very wide randomization range gives more uniform robustness but at some cost to peak performance everywhere (consistent with the tradeoff discussed in the first exercise of this lesson) -- identify the randomization range that achieves the best overall balance for your specific grid-world and mismatch range, and discuss why choosing this range in real robotics sim-to-real work requires similar empirical tuning informed by how much dynamics uncertainty is genuinely expected between the simulator and the real deployment target -- too narrow a range fails to cover the real gap, too wide a range needlessly sacrifices achievable performance.",
      problemKn: "randomization range ya WIDTH (domain-randomized training ಸಮಯದಲ್ಲಿ ಎಷ್ಟು variation ಸೇರಿಸಬೇಕು) ಸ್ವತಃ ಅದರ ಸ್ವಂತ tradeoff ಇರುವ ಒಂದು ಟ್ಯೂನ್ ಮಾಡಬಹುದಾದ hyperparameter ಆಗಿದೆ. ಇದನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ತನಿಖೆ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n1) ಹಲವಾರು ಭಿನ್ನ randomization range widths ನಲ್ಲಿ domain-randomized policies ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) ಈ ಎಲ್ಲಾ policies ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ ಪೂರ್ಣ 'real world' variants ಸೆಟ್ ಮತ್ತು ಮೂಲ simulator configuration ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಮೌಲ್ಯಮಾಪನ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ grid-world ಮತ್ತು mismatch range ಗೆ ಅತ್ಯುತ್ತಮ ಒಟ್ಟಾರೆ ಸಮತೋಲನ ಸಾಧಿಸುವ randomization range ಗುರುತಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m182, l182_3, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'System Identification: Narrowing the Simulator to Match Real Data',
      titleKn: 'System Identification: Real Data ಹೊಂದಿಸಲು Simulator ಅನ್ನೂ ಕಿರಿದುಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "An alternative (or complementary) approach to domain randomization (from the previous lesson) is System Identification: collecting REAL-WORLD data (e.g. from a small number of real robot trials) and using it to CALIBRATE the simulator's dynamics parameters to more accurately match the genuine real-world dynamics, rather than training broadly across a wide randomized range and hoping reality falls within it.\n\n1) Explain the core difference in approach: domain randomization tries to make the policy robust to NOT KNOWING the exact real dynamics (training across many possibilities), while system identification tries to actually FIGURE OUT the real dynamics more precisely (using real data to narrow down the simulator's parameters), then train more narrowly/precisely-targeted at that identified configuration -- explain a scenario where system identification's approach might be preferable (e.g. when at least some real-world data collection is feasible, even if limited, and the real dynamics are reasonably close to a SPECIFIC, identifiable configuration rather than being highly variable/unpredictable) versus a scenario where domain randomization's approach might be preferable (e.g. when even collecting a small amount of real data is prohibitively expensive/risky, or when the real dynamics are expected to vary significantly across different deployment conditions, making a single 'identified' configuration insufficient).\n\n2) Explain why these two approaches are not mutually exclusive -- describe how a practical sim-to-real pipeline might combine them: use system identification to narrow the domain-randomization range toward the actually-plausible real-world region (informed by limited real data) rather than randomizing across an arbitrarily wide, uninformed range, getting some benefit of both approaches' respective strengths.",
      problemKn: "Domain randomization (ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠ) ಗೆ ಒಂದು ಪರ್ಯಾಯ (ಅಥವಾ ಪೂರಕ) ವಿಧಾನ System Identification ಆಗಿದೆ: REAL-WORLD data ಸಂಗ್ರಹಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು simulator ya dynamics parameters ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜ ಜಗತ್ತಿನ dynamics ಗೆ ಹೆಚ್ಚು ನಿಖರವಾಗಿ ಹೊಂದಿಸಲು CALIBRATE ಮಾಡಲು ಬಳಸುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) ವಿಧಾನದಲ್ಲಿನ ಪ್ರಮುಖ ವ್ಯತ್ಯಾಸ ವಿವರಿಸಿ: domain randomization ನಿಜ dynamics ಅನ್ನೂ ತಿಳಿಯದಿರುವುದಕ್ಕೆ policy ಅನ್ನೂ robust ಮಾಡಲು ಪ್ರಯತ್ನಿಸುತ್ತದೆ, ಆದರೆ system identification ನಿಜ dynamics ಅನ್ನೂ ಹೆಚ್ಚು ನಿಖರವಾಗಿ ಕಂಡುಹಿಡಿಯಲು ಪ್ರಯತ್ನಿಸುತ್ತದೆ. ಪ್ರತಿಯೊಂದೂ ಆದ್ಯತೆ ಪಡೆಯುವ ಸನ್ನಿವೇಶಗಳನ್ನೂ ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಈ ಎರಡು ವಿಧಾನಗಳು ಪರಸ್ಪರ ಪ್ರತ್ಯೇಕವಲ್ಲ ಏಕೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ, ಒಂದು ಪ್ರಾಯೋಗಿಕ sim-to-real pipeline ಅವುಗಳನ್ನೂ ಹೇಗೆ ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸಬಹುದು ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸುತ್ತಾ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing System Identification from Limited Real-World Data',
      titleKn: 'ಸೀಮಿತ Real-World Data ಇಂದ System Identification ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely implement a simple system identification procedure on your grid-world setup from earlier lessons in this module.\n\n1) Genuinely simulate 'collecting real-world data': generate a SMALL number of trajectories (e.g. 20 episodes) from your 'real world' variant (from an earlier lesson), treating this as the limited real data you'd realistically be able to collect from actual hardware.\n\n2) Genuinely implement a simple parameter-estimation procedure: using this small real-data sample, estimate the real-world transition stochasticity/action-bias parameters (e.g. by counting how often actions succeed versus fail/slip in your collected trajectories) -- report your estimated parameters and compare them against the TRUE real-world parameters you know (since you control the 'real world' simulation), reporting the actual estimation error.\n\n3) Genuinely train a policy using your ESTIMATED (system-identified) parameters as the simulator configuration (rather than your original, unmodified simulator), and evaluate this policy on the TRUE real-world variant -- compare its transfer performance against both your original single-simulator-trained policy and your domain-randomized policy from the previous lesson, reporting your genuine measured three-way comparison.",
      problemKn: "ಈ module ya ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠಗಳ ನಿಮ್ಮ grid-world setup ಮೇಲೆ ಒಂದು ಸರಳ system identification procedure ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) 'ನಿಜ-ಜಗತ್ತಿನ data ಸಂಗ್ರಹಿಸುವುದನ್ನೂ' ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಸಿಮ್ಯುಲೇಟ್ ಮಾಡಿ: ನಿಮ್ಮ 'real world' variant ಇಂದ ಒಂದು ಸಣ್ಣ ಸಂಖ್ಯೆಯ trajectories ಉತ್ಪಾದಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಒಂದು ಸರಳ parameter-estimation procedure ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಅಂದಾಜು parameters ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜ parameters ಗೆ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ ESTIMATED parameters ಬಳಸಿ ಒಂದು policy ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಇದನ್ನೂ TRUE real-world variant ಮೇಲೆ ಮೌಲ್ಯಮಾಪನ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Combining System Identification and Domain Randomization',
      titleKn: 'System Identification ಮತ್ತು Domain Randomization ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely build the combined pipeline discussed conceptually in the second exercise of this lesson: using system identification to inform a NARROWER, better-targeted domain randomization range.\n\n1) Genuinely use your estimated parameters (with their measured estimation uncertainty/error, from the previous exercise) to define a NARROW randomization range CENTERED on your system-identified estimate (rather than the arbitrarily wide range you used in your original domain-randomization exercise) -- the width of this narrow range should reflect your genuine estimation uncertainty (e.g. wider if you collected very little real data, narrower if you collected more).\n\n2) Genuinely train a policy with this system-identification-informed, narrow domain randomization, and evaluate it on your true real-world variant.\n\n3) Genuinely compare this combined approach's transfer performance against all three of your previous approaches (single-simulator, wide domain randomization, system-identification-only) -- report whether the combined approach achieves the best overall result (or at least a favorable balance of peak performance and robustness), and discuss why this genuine empirical comparison across all four approaches on your own controllable setup provides a much more concrete, evidence-based understanding of sim-to-real tradeoffs than reading about these techniques in the abstract -- reflecting on how this module's progression (reality gap diagnosis, domain randomization, system identification, and their combination) mirrors the practical decision-making process a real robotics team would need to work through when deploying any RL policy from simulation onto genuine physical hardware.",
      problemKn: "ಈ ಪಾಠದ ಎರಡನೇ exercise ya ಪರಿಕಲ್ಪನಾತ್ಮಕವಾಗಿ ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿದ ಸಂಯೋಜಿತ pipeline ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಹಿಂದಿನ exercise ya ನಿಮ್ಮ ಅಂದಾಜು parameters ಬಳಸಿ ಒಂದು ಕಿರಿದಾದ randomization range ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವ್ಯಾಖ್ಯಾನಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಈ system-identification-informed, ಕಿರಿದಾದ domain randomization ಜೊತೆ ಒಂದು policy ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಇದನ್ನೂ ನಿಜ real-world variant ಮೇಲೆ ಮೌಲ್ಯಮಾಪನ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ಈ ಸಂಯೋಜಿತ ವಿಧಾನದ transfer performance ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ ಹಿಂದಿನ ಎಲ್ಲಾ ಮೂರು ವಿಧಾನಗಳಿಗೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ." },
  ]),

  // ── Module 183: RL for Games ──
  ...set(m183, l183_1, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'Perfect versus Imperfect Information Games',
      titleKn: 'Perfect vs Imperfect Information Games',
      problem: "Games can be categorized by whether all players have access to the FULL game state at every decision point (perfect information, e.g. chess or Go, where the entire board is always visible to both players) or only PARTIAL information (imperfect information, e.g. poker, where each player's cards are hidden from opponents).\n\n1) Explain why perfect-information games fit naturally within the standard MDP framework covered throughout this phase -- since the full state is always observable, why is the state representation straightforward (just the current board configuration), letting algorithms like the ones you've built throughout this phase (value-based, policy-gradient, actor-critic methods) apply relatively directly?\n\n2) Explain why imperfect-information games genuinely break the standard MDP assumption in a specific way -- since a player's own OBSERVATION (what they can see) is not the same as the true underlying game STATE (which includes information hidden from them, like opponents' cards), explain why this requires a different framework (a Partially Observable MDP, or POMDP) where the optimal action may depend on the player's entire OBSERVATION HISTORY (to infer likely hidden information) rather than just their current single observation, and why bluffing/deceptive strategies can be genuinely optimal in imperfect-information games in a way that has no clean analog in perfect-information games like chess.",
      problemKn: "Games ಅನ್ನೂ ಪ್ರತಿ ನಿರ್ಧಾರ ಬಿಂದುವಿನಲ್ಲಿ ಎಲ್ಲಾ players ಗೆ ಪೂರ್ಣ game state ಗೆ ಪ್ರವೇಶ ಇದೆಯೇ ಎಂಬುದರ ಆಧಾರದ ಮೇಲೆ ವರ್ಗೀಕರಿಸಬಹುದು.\n\n1) perfect-information games ಈ phase ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ ಕವರ್ ಮಾಡಿದ ಪ್ರಮಾಣಿತ MDP framework ಒಳಗೆ ಸ್ವಾಭಾವಿಕವಾಗಿ ಏಕೆ ಹೊಂದಿಕೆಯಾಗುತ್ತವೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) imperfect-information games ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪ್ರಮಾಣಿತ MDP ಊಹೆಯನ್ನೂ ಒಂದು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ ರೀತಿಯಲ್ಲಿ ಏಕೆ ಮುರಿಯುತ್ತವೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ, POMDP framework ಸೇರಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Building a Simple Imperfect-Information Game and Testing a Standard RL Agent on It',
      titleKn: 'ಒಂದು ಸರಳ Imperfect-Information Game ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ ಒಂದು ಪ್ರಮಾಣಿತ RL Agent ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely construct a minimal imperfect-information game and test whether a standard (observation-only, no history) RL agent struggles with it as predicted conceptually.\n\n1) Genuinely build a simple two-player card-guessing-style game: each player has a hidden card, players take turns making a bet or fold decision based on their OWN card and the observable betting history, and the outcome depends on the (initially hidden) comparison of both cards at the end -- a minimal simplified poker-like structure.\n\n2) Genuinely train a standard RL agent (e.g. your DQN or PPO implementation from earlier modules) that observes only its OWN current hand and the immediate last action taken (NOT the full betting history) against a simple fixed opponent strategy.\n\n3) Genuinely train a SECOND version of the same agent, but this time giving it access to the FULL observation history (all past betting actions in the current game, not just the most recent one) as part of its state representation -- report whether this history-aware agent achieves measurably better performance than the history-blind agent, providing concrete empirical evidence for why imperfect-information games genuinely require reasoning over observation history (connecting to the POMDP framing from the previous exercise) rather than being solvable with the simple current-observation-only state representations that sufficed for your perfect-information grid-world throughout the rest of this phase.",
      problemKn: "ಒಂದು ಕನಿಷ್ಠ imperfect-information game ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಒಂದು ಪ್ರಮಾಣಿತ RL agent ಇದರೊಂದಿಗೆ ಪರಿಕಲ್ಪನಾತ್ಮಕವಾಗಿ ಊಹಿಸಿದಂತೆ ಕಷ್ಟಪಡುತ್ತದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಒಂದು ಸರಳ two-player card-guessing-style game ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಕೇವಲ ಅದರ ಸ್ವಂತ ಪ್ರಸ್ತುತ ಕೈ ಮತ್ತು ತಕ್ಷಣದ ಕೊನೆಯ action ಗಮನಿಸುವ ಒಂದು ಪ್ರಮಾಣಿತ RL agent ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ಪೂರ್ಣ observation history ಗೆ ಪ್ರವೇಶ ಇರುವ ಒಂದು ಎರಡನೇ ಆವೃತ್ತಿಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಫಲಿತಾಂಶಗಳನ್ನೂ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Testing Whether the History-Aware Agent Learns to Bluff',
      titleKn: 'History-Aware Agent Bluff ಮಾಡಲು ಕಲಿಯುತ್ತದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely investigate whether your history-aware agent from the previous exercise learns genuinely deceptive/bluffing strategies, a distinctive behavior possible only in imperfect-information settings (as discussed conceptually in the first exercise of this lesson).\n\n1) Genuinely inspect your trained history-aware agent's behavior: for a specific WEAK hand (a card that should genuinely lose most comparisons), check whether the agent sometimes still bets AGGRESSIVELY (a bluff, attempting to induce the opponent to fold rather than genuinely trying to win a fair comparison) rather than always folding or betting conservatively with weak hands.\n\n2) Genuinely quantify this: compute the actual measured correlation between the agent's hand strength and its betting aggressiveness across many games -- report whether this correlation is PERFECT (always betting proportionally to true hand strength, no bluffing at all) or shows genuine INCONSISTENCY (sometimes betting aggressively with weak hands), providing quantitative evidence for or against genuine bluffing behavior having emerged.\n\n3) Genuinely test whether this bluffing behavior (if you find it) is actually EFFECTIVE against your fixed opponent strategy: compare the history-aware agent's win rate WITH its observed bluffing behavior against a modified/ablated version where you force it to always bet strictly proportionally to true hand strength (no bluffing allowed) -- report whether genuine bluffing measurably improves win rate, or whether it turns out to be a spurious/ineffective pattern that emerged without actually helping performance, providing a rigorous, evidence-based conclusion about whether your agent's imperfect-information reasoning produced genuinely useful deceptive strategy or not, rather than assuming any observed betting inconsistency must be meaningful bluffing.",
      problemKn: "ಹಿಂದಿನ exercise ya ನಿಮ್ಮ history-aware agent ನಿಜವಾಗಿ deceptive/bluffing strategies ಕಲಿಯುತ್ತದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ತನಿಖೆ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ trained history-aware agent ya ವರ್ತನೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ: ಒಂದು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ WEAK hand ಗೆ, agent ಕೆಲವೊಮ್ಮೆ ಇನ್ನೂ ಆಕ್ರಮಣಕಾರಿಯಾಗಿ bet ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಇದನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಮಾಣಾತ್ಮಕಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಈ bluffing ವರ್ತನೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಣಾಮಕಾರಿಯಾಗಿದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m183, l183_2, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'Tree Search Combined with Learned Value Functions',
      titleKn: 'ಕಲಿತ Value Functions ಜೊತೆ ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸಿದ Tree Search',
      problem: "For perfect-information games with a known model (like the transition dynamics you've always had explicit access to in your grid-world), a powerful approach combines LOOKAHEAD TREE SEARCH (explicitly simulating multiple possible future move sequences before choosing an action) with a LEARNED VALUE FUNCTION (used to evaluate positions at the search tree's leaves, rather than needing to search all the way to genuine game-end, which is often computationally infeasible for complex games).\n\n1) Explain why pure tree search alone (without a learned value function) becomes computationally infeasible for games with a large branching factor and long games -- since the number of possible move sequences grows exponentially with search depth, why can't you simply search all the way to every possible game-ending outcome for a genuinely complex game?\n\n2) Explain why using a LEARNED value function to evaluate positions at a limited search depth (rather than searching to the true end) is a practical compromise -- the tree search handles near-term, precisely-computable tactical considerations (a few moves of genuine lookahead), while the learned value function provides an approximate but computationally-cheap estimate of longer-term position quality beyond the search horizon, similar in spirit to how bootstrapping in TD learning (from an earlier module) uses an approximate value estimate rather than requiring a full genuine rollout to the true end of an episode.",
      problemKn: "ಒಂದು ತಿಳಿದ model ಇರುವ perfect-information games ಗಾಗಿ, ಒಂದು ಶಕ್ತಿಶಾಲಿ ವಿಧಾನ LOOKAHEAD TREE SEARCH ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಂದು LEARNED VALUE FUNCTION ಜೊತೆ ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) ಒಂದು ಕಲಿತ value function ಇಲ್ಲದೆ ಶುದ್ಧ tree search ಮಾತ್ರ ದೊಡ್ಡ branching factor ಇರುವ games ಗೆ ಏಕೆ computationally infeasible ಆಗುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಒಂದು ಸೀಮಿತ search depth ನಲ್ಲಿ positions ಮೌಲ್ಯಮಾಪನ ಮಾಡಲು ಒಂದು LEARNED value function ಬಳಸುವುದೂ ಏಕೆ ಒಂದು ಪ್ರಾಯೋಗಿಕ ಒಪ್ಪಂದ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ, TD learning ya bootstrapping ಗೆ ಸಂಪರ್ಕಿಸುತ್ತಾ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing Minimax Tree Search with a Learned Evaluation Function',
      titleKn: 'ಒಂದು ಕಲಿತ Evaluation Function ಜೊತೆ Minimax Tree Search ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Implement minimax tree search combined with a learned value function on a simple two-player perfect-information game.\n\n1) Genuinely build a small two-player game with a KNOWN model (e.g. a tiny variant of tic-tac-toe on a small board, or a simple turn-based grid game where both players' positions are always visible) small enough that you could in principle search to the true end, but genuinely limit your search to a fixed, shallow DEPTH (e.g. 2-3 moves ahead) to force reliance on your value function at the search horizon.\n\n2) Genuinely train a value function (via self-play, reusing your self-play methodology from an earlier lesson) to evaluate positions, then implement minimax search that uses this learned value function to evaluate leaf nodes at your fixed search depth.\n\n3) Genuinely compare this tree-search-plus-value-function agent's performance against your PURE value-function-only agent (no tree search, just always picking the action the value function directly favors) from an earlier lesson -- report whether adding tree search on top of the same learned value function measurably improves win rate, providing concrete empirical evidence for the combined approach's benefit over relying on the learned value function alone.",
      problemKn: "ಒಂದು ಸರಳ two-player perfect-information game ಮೇಲೆ minimax tree search ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಂದು ಕಲಿತ value function ಜೊತೆ ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಒಂದು ತಿಳಿದ model ಇರುವ ಒಂದು ಸಣ್ಣ two-player game ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ, ಆದರೆ ನಿಮ್ಮ search ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಂದು ಸ್ಥಿರ, ಆಳವಿಲ್ಲದ DEPTH ಗೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಮಿತಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಒಂದು value function ಅನ್ನೂ (self-play ಮೂಲಕ) ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ, ನಂತರ ಈ ಕಲಿತ value function ಬಳಸಿ minimax search ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಈ tree-search-plus-value-function agent ya ಪ್ರದರ್ಶನವನ್ನೂ ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ ನಿಮ್ಮ pure value-function-only agent ಗೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Measuring How Search Depth Affects Playing Strength and Compute Cost',
      titleKn: 'Search Depth Playing Strength ಮತ್ತು Compute Cost ಮೇಲೆ ಹೇಗೆ ಪರಿಣಾಮ ಬೀರುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely investigate the search-depth-versus-compute-cost tradeoff for your tree-search-plus-value-function agent from the previous exercise.\n\n1) Genuinely evaluate your agent's playing strength (win rate against a fixed reference opponent, e.g. your pure value-function-only agent from an earlier lesson) at several different search depths (e.g. depth 1, 2, 4, 6).\n\n2) Genuinely measure the actual computational cost (wall-clock time per move decided) at each search depth, and report how it scales -- confirm whether it grows roughly exponentially with depth (consistent with the branching-factor argument from the first exercise of this lesson).\n\n3) Genuinely report your measured playing-strength-versus-depth curve alongside your compute-cost-versus-depth curve, and discuss the practical tradeoff this reveals: does playing strength show diminishing returns at higher search depths (e.g. depth 4 to 6 improving win rate only marginally despite a large jump in compute cost), and if so, discuss why real game-playing systems must make a genuine engineering tradeoff between search depth and available compute budget per move, rather than simply searching as deep as theoretically possible -- connect your own measured tradeoff curve to why famous game-playing systems combine deep learned value functions specifically to reduce the NEED for very deep search, since a sufficiently accurate value function at shallow depth can substitute for much deeper search with a cruder evaluation, echoing this lesson's core theme.",
      problemKn: "ಹಿಂದಿನ exercise ya ನಿಮ್ಮ tree-search-plus-value-function agent ಗಾಗಿ search-depth-vs-compute-cost tradeoff ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ತನಿಖೆ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n1) ಹಲವಾರು ಭಿನ್ನ search depths ನಲ್ಲಿ ನಿಮ್ಮ agent ya playing strength ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಮೌಲ್ಯಮಾಪನ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) ಪ್ರತಿ search depth ನಲ್ಲಿ ನಿಜ computational cost ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಅಳೆದ playing-strength-vs-depth curve ಅನ್ನೂ compute-cost-vs-depth curve ಜೊತೆಗೆ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಇದೂ ಬಹಿರಂಗಪಡಿಸುವ ಪ್ರಾಯೋಗಿಕ tradeoff ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m183, l183_3, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'Monte Carlo Tree Search: Sampling Instead of Exhaustive Search',
      titleKn: 'Monte Carlo Tree Search: Exhaustive Search ಬದಲಿಗೆ Sampling',
      problem: "Monte Carlo Tree Search (MCTS) offers an alternative to the exhaustive minimax search from the previous lesson: rather than exploring every possible move at every depth, MCTS builds its search tree ASYMMETRICALLY, spending more simulated rollouts exploring promising-looking branches (guided by a balance of estimated value and uncertainty, directly connecting to the exploration-exploitation theme that has recurred throughout this phase since the epsilon-greedy and UCB-style considerations in early modules) while spending fewer rollouts on branches that look clearly worse.\n\n1) Explain why this asymmetric allocation of search effort is more efficient than uniformly exploring every branch to the same depth (as plain minimax does) -- given a FIXED total computational budget (number of simulated rollouts), why does concentrating that budget on the most promising-looking lines of play, rather than spreading it uniformly across all possible moves including obviously-bad ones, let MCTS search more deeply and accurately along the lines that actually matter for the final decision?\n\n2) Explain, at a conceptual level, the four-phase MCTS loop (Selection: traverse the tree favoring promising-but-underexplored branches; Expansion: add a new node to the tree; Simulation/Rollout: play out a rough estimate of the outcome from the new node, e.g. via a fast random or heuristic policy; Backpropagation: update the value estimates of all nodes along the path based on the rollout's outcome) -- explain why repeating this loop many times progressively refines the tree's value estimates, with the Selection phase's balance of favoring high-value versus under-explored branches directly echoing the exploration-exploitation tradeoff you first encountered with epsilon-greedy exploration many modules ago in this same phase.",
      problemKn: "Monte Carlo Tree Search (MCTS) ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ exhaustive minimax search ಗೆ ಒಂದು ಪರ್ಯಾಯ ಒದಗಿಸುತ್ತದೆ: ಪ್ರತಿ depth ನಲ್ಲಿ ಪ್ರತಿ ಸಂಭಾವ್ಯ move ಪರಿಶೋಧಿಸುವ ಬದಲಿಗೆ, MCTS ತನ್ನ search tree ಅನ್ನೂ ASYMMETRICALLY ನಿರ್ಮಿಸುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) ಈ asymmetric ಹಂಚಿಕೆ ಸಮಾನವಾಗಿ ಪ್ರತಿ branch ಅನ್ನೂ ಅದೇ depth ಗೆ ಪರಿಶೋಧಿಸುವುದಕ್ಕಿಂತ ಏಕೆ ಹೆಚ್ಚು ದಕ್ಷ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ನಾಲ್ಕು-ಹಂತದ MCTS loop ಅನ್ನೂ ಪರಿಕಲ್ಪನಾತ್ಮಕವಾಗಿ ವಿವರಿಸಿ: Selection, Expansion, Simulation/Rollout, Backpropagation, ಮತ್ತು Selection ಹಂತದ exploration-exploitation ಸಮತೋಲನ ಈ ಅದೇ phase ya ಬಹಳ ಹಿಂದೆ epsilon-greedy exploration ಜೊತೆ ನೀವು ಮೊದಲು ಎದುರಿಸಿದ ಅದೇ ಪ್ರತಿಧ್ವನಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing MCTS and Comparing Its Efficiency to Minimax',
      titleKn: 'MCTS ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಅದರ Efficiency ಅನ್ನೂ Minimax ಗೆ ಹೋಲಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Implement the four-phase MCTS loop from scratch on the same two-player game you built in the previous lesson.\n\n1) Genuinely implement Selection (using a standard UCB-style formula balancing estimated value and visit-count-based uncertainty to choose which branch to explore at each step), Expansion, Simulation (using a fast random rollout policy to estimate outcomes from newly-expanded nodes), and Backpropagation (updating value/visit-count statistics along the traversed path).\n\n2) Genuinely run your MCTS agent for a fixed rollout budget (e.g. 500 simulations per move) and compare its playing strength (win rate against a fixed reference opponent) against your minimax-plus-learned-value-function agent from the previous lesson at a COMPARABLE computational budget (matching total simulated positions evaluated as closely as feasible between the two approaches).\n\n3) Genuinely report your measured comparison, and genuinely inspect MCTS's actual tree statistics (e.g. how many total nodes were expanded, and how unevenly visit counts were distributed across different branches) to confirm the asymmetric-search-effort-allocation behavior discussed conceptually in the previous exercise -- report whether your MCTS implementation shows the expected concentration of search effort on promising branches rather than uniform exploration.",
      problemKn: "ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದಲ್ಲಿ ನೀವು ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿದ ಅದೇ two-player game ಮೇಲೆ ಮೊದಲಿನಿಂದ ನಾಲ್ಕು-ಹಂತದ MCTS loop ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) Selection, Expansion, Simulation, ಮತ್ತು Backpropagation ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಒಂದು ಸ್ಥಿರ rollout budget ಗಾಗಿ ನಿಮ್ಮ MCTS agent ಅನ್ನೂ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಅದರ playing strength ಅನ್ನೂ ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ ನಿಮ್ಮ minimax-plus-learned-value-function agent ಗೆ ಹೋಲಿಸಬಹುದಾದ ಒಂದು computational budget ನಲ್ಲಿ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಅಳೆದ ಹೋಲಿಕೆ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು MCTS ya ನಿಜ tree statistics ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ asymmetric-search-effort ವರ್ತನೆ ಖಚಿತಪಡಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Building a Capstone AlphaZero-Style Agent Combining Self-Play, MCTS, and a Learned Value Network',
      titleKn: 'Self-Play, MCTS, ಮತ್ತು ಒಂದು Learned Value Network ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸುವ ಒಂದು Capstone AlphaZero-Style Agent ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "As a capstone for this module and the closing lesson of this entire phase, genuinely combine everything: self-play (from an earlier module), MCTS (from this lesson), and a learned value/policy network, into a simplified AlphaZero-style training loop.\n\n1) Genuinely implement the core loop: use MCTS (guided by your current learned value/policy network to bias the Selection phase toward the network's favored moves, rather than pure random rollouts) to generate game trajectories via self-play, then use the OUTCOMES of these MCTS-guided self-play games as training data to improve the value/policy network itself (the network learns to predict what MCTS-with-search eventually decided was good, gradually internalizing the search process's conclusions) -- repeat this loop for several iterations.\n\n2) Genuinely train this combined system on your simple two-player game for several training iterations, and track playing strength (against a fixed reference opponent) after each iteration.\n\n3) Genuinely report whether playing strength improves over these training iterations, and genuinely compare your FINAL AlphaZero-style agent's strength against every other approach you've built across this module (pure value-function-only, minimax-plus-value-function, and plain MCTS-with-random-rollouts) -- report your full, genuine measured comparison, and write a brief reflective synthesis connecting this capstone exercise back to the full arc of this phase: from hand-specified dynamic programming through model-free control, through policy gradients and actor-critic methods, through PPO, reward modeling, multi-agent RL, sim-to-real transfer, and now this culminating combination of search and learning for games -- reflecting on what you found most valuable about building every one of these pieces yourself, hands-on, across this entire phase.",
      problemKn: "ಈ module ಮತ್ತು ಈ ಸಂಪೂರ್ಣ phase ya ಮುಕ್ತಾಯದ ಪಾಠಕ್ಕೆ ಒಂದು capstone ಆಗಿ, ಎಲ್ಲವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸಿ: self-play, MCTS, ಮತ್ತು ಒಂದು ಕಲಿತ value/policy network, ಒಂದು ಸರಳೀಕೃತ AlphaZero-style training loop ಗೆ.\n\n1) ಪ್ರಮುಖ loop ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ: self-play ಮೂಲಕ game trajectories ಉತ್ಪಾದಿಸಲು MCTS ಬಳಸಿ, ನಂತರ ಈ MCTS-guided self-play games ya OUTCOMES ಅನ್ನೂ value/policy network ಅನ್ನೂ ಸ್ವತಃ ಸುಧಾರಿಸಲು training data ಆಗಿ ಬಳಸಿ.\n\n2) ಈ ಸಂಯೋಜಿತ system ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸರಳ two-player game ಮೇಲೆ ಹಲವಾರು training iterations ಗಾಗಿ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ಫಲಿತಾಂಶ playing strength ಅನ್ನೂ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ನಿಮ್ಮ ಅಂತಿಮ AlphaZero-style agent ಅನ್ನೂ ಈ module ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ ನೀವು ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿದ ಪ್ರತಿ ಇತರ ವಿಧಾನಕ್ಕೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ, ಮತ್ತು ಈ ಸಂಪೂರ್ಣ phase ya ಪ್ರಗತಿಗೆ ಸಂಪರ್ಕಿಸುವ ಒಂದು ಸಂಕ್ಷಿಪ್ತ ಪ್ರತಿಬಿಂಬಿತ synthesis ಬರೆಯಿರಿ." },
  ]),
];
